The Prague Stock Exchange at the close of the market had a negative slump.
After the slump of the morning, the Prague Stock Exchange restored losses.
Transactions of CEZ actions reached almost half of the current daily trade.
Since the beginning of Tuesday, the Prague Stock Exchange has resumed since the collapse of the day before it was almost 6%.
Behind this decline is the collapse of stocks on Wall Street.
Thus, even from us, there is a reaction of the market to the result of the vote of the House of Representatives in America, which refused to support the plan for stabilizing the local financial sector.
Collapsing stocks in Asia
Shares in the Asian Stock Exchanges on Tuesday experienced a dramatic slump, although the indices, however, canceled a portion of the losses during the day.
The Hong Kong Stock Exchange's Hang Seng index cancelled nearly 4 percent during the day, but later also a portion of the losses stabilizing at 2.5 percent.
The Hang Seng China Enterprises index, which follows the movement of Chinese shares on the Hong Kong Stock Exchange, dropped 3.8 percent while in Shangai markets were closed.
Shares on the Sydney Stock Exchange lost more than 5%, but in the end lost to 4.3 percent.
Thay-Wan's Stock Exchange, according to the local index, dropped 3.6 percent.
"Bailout action planning is uncertain and that will affect financial markets around the world," Hong Kong Currency Office chief Joseph Yam noted.
Hong Kong, despite being part of China, manages financial policy on its own, therefore without depending on the Central Bank.
Hong Kong has interest rates at the same level as the United States.
U.S. legislators, according to Australian Prime Minister Kevin Rudd, are expected to quickly return to dialogue and approve the proposal for financial support.
Otherwise, there is a risk that other countries will also be affected.
Massacre of American actions
The U.S. Parliament on Monday rejected the support plan of the financial system, for which 700 billion dollars (almost twelve bilions of Czech wreaths) had to be allocated.
Legislators have thus ignored President Bush's call to support the plan.
According to Bush, the plan would solve the main causes of the financial crisis and help stabilise the entire economy.
U.S. stocks Monday experienced a massacre and major stock indices recorded the biggest slump in more than twenty years.
The Dow Jones index fell by almost 7% and a slump of similar was last observed in 1987.
The indenx lowered already before the vote, but as soon as it was seen that the bill was not approved, it went into free fall.
Congregation has succumbed: the U.S. government can extend 700 billion dollars to the banks.
America's highest congressional representatives and President George W. Bush's government have agreed on a broader form of an understanding of economic support for America's financial system.
Voting on it will be early next week.
U.S. legislators broke the ice in the dialogue on approving the bailout in the form of economic aid for the U.S. financial system for a total of US$700 million (almost twelve of wreaths).
But it's still not said.
Representatives of Congress, therefore, must define some details of the agreement before publishing the final form of the law and voting for it.
The financial system's support plan will be debated on Monday on the territory of the House of Representatives.
On Sunday he told Reuters, the chairman of the Barney Frank Chamber's Banking and Financial Services Committee.
In the Senate, according to some sources, you could vote regarding the plan probably not before Monday.
According to the economists, the announcement that the approval of the financial plan should be the first and fundamental psychological factor capable of awakening the financial markets.
Subsequently, however, there will be a return to reality due to the complexity of the mechanisms with which, in practice, to achieve the help of the markets.
Paulson: the Plan must be effective
"We've taken a big step forward."
“We’ve clarified our different views on the form of the presentation of the Market Stabilisation Package,” Democrat Nancy Pelosi told Bloomberg.
According to the same , the final vote could still take place on Sunday.
Delegates of lawmakers met the night between Saturday and Sunday with U.S. Finance Minister Henry Paulson, to give final shape to the government's financial funds.
These funds should serve to acquire non-transferable mortgage assets from financial companies, which lead financial companies to serious losses and put the stability of the entire system at risk.
“We are fulfilling the definitive agreement of the plan that will work and that must also be effective on the market.”
“We still need to carry out the recovery plan, but I think we’re there,” Paulson said.
The Signal for Asian Trade
The global financial crisis falls sharply into the stock markets that are collapsing sharply.
According to Nevada Democratic Senator Harry Reid, legislators are trying to reach a definitive agreement in Congress even before Sunday.
That is, they want to calm investors before trade on the Asian financial markets, which are, in order of time, the first to influence Monday's market following the decision of Congress.
But for now it is not definitively clear when the two U.S. Chambers of Congress will vote on the law and although negotiations can be stopped at the last moment on some problem.
Legislators hope it will be approved in the coming days.
But the law will still undergo many changes.
The total sum provided for the support of the system must be divided into two parts.
The first 350 billion dollars must be available as soon as possible as President George Bush asked.
But Congress can block the allocation of the rest of the sum in the event that it will have the impression that the program does not perform its function, blocking the next 100 billion dollars and after the finals 350.
Bush appreciated progress in negotiations.
Even if the President can veto this decision, Congress can overwhelm its veto.
Despite these changes, the basic thinking of the programme has been maintained, i.e. getting the finances to buy passive mortgage values, whose price has plummeted due to hundreds of thousands of Americans who are unabled to pay their mortgages.
“We’ve processed everything.
The House of Representatives should be able to vote regarding the law Sunday and the Senate on Monday," as the Republican senator mentioned.
U.S. President Bush is also satisfied with progress in the negotiations.
His spokesman Tony Fratto said that Bush is pleased with Saturday's positive trend and appreciates "the effort of both parties to stabilise our financial markets and protect our economy."
Restriction of Golden Parachutes
Senate Banking Committee Chairman Christopher Dodd, the main spokeswoman, said legislators agreed on responsibility for the financial programme, the protection of taxpayers from losses, the closure of the reductions budget, the fractionation of the financial plan.
The new law must also limit so-called gold parachutes for executives of companies participating in the program and is setting up a committee that will control funds.
These should be managed by the Ministry of Finance.
“We’ve worked very hard, and we’ve taken a big step towards the deal that will work and it’s going to be useful for the market and for all Americans,” Paulson said.
The plan counts on helping homeowners who have problems on payments.
The government is expected to negotiate new mortgage installments that it will buy with the aim of lowering monthly expenditures on installments for debtors and allow them to keep their homes.
Marek: Psychological support
The proposed plan is, according to Patria Finance’s main economist David Marek, a good idea, but its realization will be very difficult.
"And that's because we don't know in what way and for what price the problematic assets will be bought
This will be crucial for how the losses between the financial sector and the government split between them," Marek said this week for iHNed.cz .
Whether the plan will be approved now or at the beginning of next week is not, according to Marek, so fundamental.
"Much more importantly, it is that already this week the representatives of the congress find an agreement on the support of this plan and that they baptize it as soon as possible.
The psychological significance of this agreement that will be approved is at this most important time, "Marek stressed.
Duel to the death: MP3 players against cellphones
We'll recommend how to choose
While almost every mobile phone can read MP3 files, no MP3 player can call.
From this it is clear that it is better to buy only the phone.
Despite this, there are many reasons to obtain an autonomous reader.
The choice depends entirely on the mode of use and the needs of the future owner.
Maybe you'd hardly buy yourself an expensive high-quality turntable to listen to Maxim Turbulenc's latest album.
First of all, these musical products do not come out as LP and from the qualitative point of view for this there is not even an objective reason.
However, if on your sheelves you crowd hard-rock vinyl, jazz or blues probably, a turntable of the genre at the very least you dream about it.
It also works with portable music, i.e. compressed music or simply in MP3 format.
There are also prices and qualities here.
For some it may be unacceptable to spend 8,000 kronor for a reader, just as a Pink Floyd fan perhaps hardly digests the twen-rown headphones of the market.
Two extremes between which there is enough room for everyone.
The choice is wide.
To decide is the battery life.
Some high-quality music players manage to do much more than simply read music, but they don’t even make cell phones by mistake.
There is no other current device as universal as the mobile phone.
Alongside the primary functions, phoning and sending sms, he can read music and videos, record both and has a camera, sometimes better sometimes worse and maybe he can even navigate.
And if we take into account smartphones and handhevers you will also have a miniature computer in your pocket.
It’s a small miracle because no refrigerator can do the grill and no television can photograph anything.
The universality of the cell phone also brings disadvantages.
The more functions you use on your mobile phone and the sooner the indicator on the screen will require the charger.
And reading music consumes a lot of energy.
Few cellphones can read music for more than twelve hours, usually, and much less.
In this sense, music players (particularly the simplest ones) are much more effective.
As far as cheap phones are concerned, don’t count that they can last for more than a few hours.
If you still add us, from time to time, some calls, get to work for safety another charger, this also applies to the most expensive appliances.
But you won’t have this problem with an MP3 player, even the worst lasting at a minimum of 9 hours to continuous functionality with a charge.
A further problem arises when you download the music to your mobile phone.
Unfortunately, only a limited part of devices has the universal USB port, and not all of them can be connected to the computer without special software.
Certainly the music can be recorded directly on the memory card, but only a few memory players are able to make it without an adapter for minicards that are not used in mobiles.
Readers, except for a few exceptions (Ipod) do not need any special software.
Just fill the device with music via USB from your computer.
Most mobile phones and readers create themselves a media library sorted according to the names of the interpreters, name of the album, genus or according to the user's classification.
Some devices only reproduce the folder tree structure, similar to the Explorer tool in Windows, and some know how to do both (iriver, Samsung, Sony, Cowon, smart phones with operating system).
Readers are easier to handle, mobiles can do more.
When you choose a particular device you are especially interested in battery resistance, memory capacity, quality and type of audio outputs and clearly ease of use.
If the music serves you above all from the background and if you listen to it every day only briefly on the tram you just need any mobile phone to read MP3.
This succeeds by now, clearly to any cell phone.
You don’t have to deal too much with what formats your phone or reader recognize.
MP3 players support them all, the global majority of the music that comes to you will be in this format.
In addition to this format that belongs to the most popular ones, the total majority of devices support music in WMA format.
There is less the AAC (format used by the Apple company and its Ipods).
The best mobile phones also allow, for example, to buy music online.
Just visit, through the mobile internet, the corresponding pages of the operator (or another supplier) and buy the song or album.
This same service already could be a valid argument to buy a mobile-reader.
Unfortunately, the offer of the operators is miserable and is usually limited only to hits of the moment of the radio.
The prices aren’t very low.
But it is generally assumed that precisely this mode of sale of music (whether via GSM, the internet or the wireless internet) in the future will dominate the music industry.
Most people always carry their cell phones.
Anyone who wants to listen to something rummages in the pocket while the MP3 player is forgotten at home.
But it also depends on this, if you always carry behind the headphones or at least the cordless headset.
If not, you can use the built-in player that almost every phone, but only a small part of MP3 players, has.
Perhaps the biggest advantage of mobile-readers will, for many, be the price.
The cheaper ones normally cost less than 3,000 kronor.
For 3 and 4 thousand crowns you will already buy a very dignified walkman.
Usually, however, you’ll be limited by the absence of an input for common headphones.
The best mobile phones and from the technical point of view more equipped and also with the 3.5 mm socket for common headphones, cost up to 15 thousand crowns.
On the other hand, the advantage of readers is, in addition to others, easy handling.
The simplest ones not necessarily cheaper (see our choice) anyone really manages to handle them.
The minimum of functions, the similarity of handling a time with the common stereo or perhaps the turntable, is obvious.
There is no danger to accidentally impact the start-up of the mobile internet and as a result of a giant datable for data transmission.
Which braces to take
Even the cheapest phones (e.g. Vodafone 527) normally have an interchangeable memory card slot (M2, microSD etc.).
Also with regard to the purchase of the respective card, which fell within 2 000 crowns.
But be careful
Base models only support cards with a maximum capacity of 2GB.
Only the more expensive ones also make it with 8 GB memory cards.
If you already have a mobile phone and you also want to buy yourself an inexpensive MP3 player, take Cowon, Creative, Sony, Samsung, Emgeton or iriver.
According to capacity (max. 2 GB) you don’t have to spend more than 1 100 crowns.
If you're part of the most discerning listeners, preferably take specialized mobiles - walkman (Sony Ericsson, some Nokia and Samsung).
These devices have individual MP3 keys and the convenience of the service thus approaches the same MP3 player.
In this context, Sony Ericsson has made its way more, which walkman mobiles have the same scale as MP3 players sold separately.
MP3 players of the brands indicated above ensure the same service and price category up to 4 000 crowns, but with a larger memory capacity (up to 16 GB) and battery resistance (commonly 15 hours, in principle up to 35 hours).
The first virtual operator will reach the Czech Republic as early as this year.
Maybe
Perhaps the mobile phone market in the Czech Republic is quite mature and our operators offer virtually all services as their partners in Western Europe.
Here, however, something is missing – a virtual operator that obliges traditional ones to lower the price.
Maybe he won't be missed for a long time.
Of the fact that Czech operators belong to those most expensive in Europe we have already written many times on Mobil.cz.
At the end of the day, ČTÚ also confirmed it, pointing out that the price of SMS is higher than in the rest of the Union.
After the last steps of the European Commission in the field of the regularisation of roaming tariffs, perhaps a paradoxical situation will be created - for some customers of Czech mobile operators, SMS will cost less from abroad than at home.
How is it possible that prices in the Czech Republic remain so high for Europe?
The answer is simple, it lacks the competition that would really like to start the clash.
Maybe it sounds absurd, even here we have three mobile networks and coverage highly above 100 %.
On the other hand, mobile telophony operators like to whine that the Czech market is extremely competitive.
In this sense he often also emphasizes the head of the Czech Telecommunications Council (ČTÚ), Pavel Dvořák, who in the past has sometimes expressed that there is no place for another operator in the Czech Republic.
It is perhaps true that the traditional operator with its own network no longer has many possibilities. (After all, perhaps it would be difficult now to find other frequencies).
But there's yet another possibility -the virtual operator.
Precisely the entry into the market of virtual operators has led to a considerable fall in prices, to which traditional operators should have reacted.
At the same time, even before the entry of sharks such as Tesko Mobile and Virgin Mobile into the market, similar voices sounded in Britain as from us.
However, it has been shown that the place for operators like this is there and that they can be really successful.
At the end of the day, we don’t have to go very far.
In February of this year we informed you about the arrival of virtual operator Mobilking in Poland.
In just half a year this operator has managed to conquer more than 100,000 customers and to become the fifth-largest local operator.
At the same time behind Mobilking is the financial group Penta to which in the Czech Republic belongs among other things U:fon.
The advantage of virtual operators is the ability to focus on a much more concrete group-target.
In order to actually work, that is, it is enough many fewer customers than to a traditional operator.
They do not carry relatively high expenses for the creation, operation and maintenance of the mobile network.
So in the world virtual operators usually show up at a low cost, they offer calls and SMS at a low price, which is often in balance with the more restricted service offering
So for example Tesco Mobile in Britain offers SMS that costs at least half compared to its competitors.
But as Mobilking's example shows in Poland it can be a pretty good move even the bet on a group-target not little.
This operator focuses specifically on men.
“Real men choose Mobilking operator they like and can trust,” says David Seibertz CEO Mobile Entertainment Company, which manufactures this virtual operator.
Difficult to say if we can also affirm a similar concept in Poland.
Vodafone Miroslav Čepický: "The Czech market is relatively small for a fragmented approach, on which most virtual operators are based.
In addition, from the point of view of services it is relatively saturated."
In the Czech Republic no possibility, say the operators of the mobile network
But it seems almost certain that if there will ever be an operator in the Czech Republic it won't be Mobilking.
“There is talk of the expansion of this successful project, but in the Czech Republic negotiations don’t have many expectations.
The ignition of Mobilking will happen rather in the other countries in our area, "said in a telephone dialogue for Mobile.cz Jana Studničková, the press spokesman of the Penta financial group.
So he indirectly confirmed that the current operators in the Czech Republic are definitely not very favorable to this thought.
Quite on the contrary, they try to do it all because you don’t create any potential competitors.
For some time now it has not hided its interest in starting a virtual operator, the GTS Novera group that would like to take another step to become a real competitor of Telefónika O2 in the field of corporate customers.
“We have a great interest in being able to offer our customers not only fixed services but also mobile telecommunications services.
And the sooner you do the better it is.
We have negotiated repeatedly on this possibility with all Czech operators in the mobile network.
“We’ve already been able to progress in negotiations with some of them,” says Group spokesman Pavel Kaidl.
So maybe better times will come.
The unofficial information shows that the first virtual operator will perhaps have to have it by the end of the year.
For now, however, around his creation stands an impenetrable wall of silence.
“Right now, we don’t believe that in the Czech market there is space for the creation of a full-fledged virtual operator.
The Czech market, unlike other markets, is very specific.
Until now, no subjects with a complex and serious project have turned to us," he mentioned in his statement for Mobil.cz Vlastimil Sršeň of the Telefònika O2 press section.
Other operators are also used in the specific nature of the Czech market.
T-Mobil's press spokesman Martina Kemrová adds in a rare agreement with the O2 statement: "For now we have not noticed any vital and beneficial commercial project for both of them, on which it is possible to continue to build."
He adds: "In the T-Mobil network a virtual operator will hardly appear by the end of the year.
At this moment we will have already had to have had a concrete agreement on the table, so that it could be considered on the horizon."
Vodafone focuses its attention rather on reaching its 3G license requests.
Miroslaval Čepický adds: "We are open towards the negotiations of potential virtual operators and this, especially in the context of sharing expenses and infrastructure.
Let’s consider precisely this, from the point of view of customers, the most productive.
Vodafone’s main strategy in this area is to find a partner to build a common network for fast mobile internet.”
Competent authorities - with complicit operators
The attitude of mobile operators can be easily understood, the entry of another operator would mean an increase in competition, it would lead to lowering prices and thereby reducing profit.
So why facilitate the rise of such a competitor?
Annoyances around creation U:fon testify that when operators care about one thing they know how to row on the same side.
Perhaps the great pressure pressed against ČTÚ in the period of creation of U:fon towards T-Mobile's company.
This is evidence of public access information from ČTÚ web pages.
But now just U:fon serves as an argument for which not to accept the virtual operator.
Martina Kemrová noted: “The competitive environment of the mobile phone market has just been potentiated beyond everything with the entry of the fourth mobile operator into the market.
With regard to the type of mobile market in the Czech Republic, it is obvious that the entry of another competitor would put on him and especially on his high-pretensive services."
However, the attitude of mobile operators is quite surprising the complete disinterest on the part of the Czech regulatory bodies.
It is not just the attitude of the ČTÚ that unfortunately in the Czech Republic – unlike Great Britain and other developed countries – has no competence to interfere in economic competition.
It belongs exclusively to the Economic Competition Protection Office (ÚOHS).
This, however, against the whole problem has taken its typical position.
"The entry of the virtual operator into the mobile market would be possible after the conclusion of the contract for the rental of the necessary infrastructure between the mobile operator and the virtual operator had materialized.
No such contract has been concluded so far because the offer of potential buyers has probably not been attractive enough to traditional operators," said Kristián Chalupa ÚOHS External Relations Section ÚOHS in the answer to our question.
Such a statement from the representatives of the operators is perhaps not surprising.
However, on the part of the office that must defend the interest of consumers and, moreover, to deal with the fact that no one takes advantage of its position on the market, it is more than a delicate affair.
Especially when Kristián Chalupa adds: "Abroad where virtual traders commonly work, we can see that their entry into the market has contributed to lowering prices for mobile telephony services."
But, of course, the pressure on the proper functioning of the market environment and the lowering of prices do not belong to ÚOHS interests.
Truly shocking in the whole situation is the almost exciting harmony of these three operators resulting from their reactions.
The question of whether it's really just a coincidence, arises for itself.
Equally, the exact price of gasoline for all gasolines is undoubtedly the result of coincidence and competitive struggle.
“The section found no signs of anti-competitive behavior regarding this fact and therefore has no reason to intervene in any way.
No operator has a dominant position, so it does not have a corresponding greater responsibility for its behaviour on the market.
The tools needed for the opening of the mobile network market for virtual operators are perhaps also lacking for the telecommunications regulator," Chalupa mentioned in his statement.
Interpretation of the dominancing position in the market is one of the many controversial points .
Of course there is the analysis of the relevant markets developed by ČTÚ, according to which telecommunications are from the point of view of economic competition the only market.
On such a market, of course, no Czech operator has a dominant position.
But some legal experts offer another approach - for the creation of a virtual operator, the agreement of the operator of the network is always necessary.
And the mobile operator has – at the end of the day logically – the monopoly of its network.
So for a certain portion it has to be dominant.
But even if we didn’t admit this argument, for ÚOHS it would have to be an allerant minimum that three mobile operators are proceeding so strangely similarly.
At the same time the complaints from those concerned (including the aforementioned company GTS Novera) show that during the negotiations with all operators they encounter very similar obstacles.
Discharging responsibility to ČTÚ in telecommunications is a fairly frequent trick with which ÚOHS keeps itself out of the whole matter.
We've already found this in some similar cases.
Unfortunately, it has failed to promote ČTÚ ČTÚ's expansion of economic competition in telecommunications until the update of telecommunications law approved recently, despite the fact that, many experts were expecting the improvement of the situation.
ÚOHS representatives need to know this fact more than well, but perhaps their interests stand elsewhere.
You can do it, when no one looks
While without us realizing it, there remains the functioning of these pseudo-virtual operators, of which there are even some in Czech mobile networks.
These are companies that publish their price lists of mobile telephony services, but they are only partially public.
It’s usually about multilevel marketing companies.
So for example Amway, Bohemia Telecom, Starlife and a number of others offer their own mobile services.
The price lists of these companies usually only reach pre-established people.
The magic lies in the fact that the company towards the operator figures as a classic business customer, who orders a large amount of SIM cards.
As an important customer then receives special price offers (and more).
The problem is that the commercial conditions of all Czech mobile operators prohibit a similar pre-sale.
"If there were the cases you are talking about, of which we will be interested, it could be a violation of the contractual conditions, and it could result in the conclusion of cooperation with that company," Martina Kemrová answered the question in question.
But it is somewhat unlikely that such an activity did not reach the ears of those responsible for mobile operators.
Especially in the cases of the 3 companies mentioned you can be thousands of customers.
No operator defends himself at the same time from such an attitude.
From the activity of these companies, that is, they make a not insignificant profit and at the same time - unlike the real virtual operators - are not damaged in any way with their official marketing.
Miroslav Čepický warns about possible problems: "The law allows such speculation, but only a negligible percentage of customers use the services of these companies.
They also have to accept that the SIM card in their phone is not registered in their name, so they cannot fully dispose of it as the usual customers of mobile operators
But precisely this should be a strange minimum at ČTÚ.
In addition, another question opens up for ÚOHS.
If mobile operators close their eyes in front of such an attitude, would they block the creation of the virtual operator?
It is necessary to add that ÚOHS has strong enough rights to expose similar activities and if it does not want it will not have to be satisfied with the explanation of operators who have no idea of all this and that their commercial conditions prohibit it.
When will we no longer be expensive
As has already been said several times in the text, the entry of virtual operators into the mobile market has led to a considerable fall in prices for services.
At the same time in practically all the countries where the operators were born, traditional operators tried more or less to prevent their creation.
The topics they used were virtually identical - market too small, to the limit too saturated, problems with the quality of services etc.
But in the developed countries precisely in these cases have intervened the regulatory bodies in defense not of mobile operators but of their customers.
Both with the formal measures and the non-formal pressure, then came to the creation of the first virtual operator and he was joined by others.
Unfortunately, the Czech regulatory bodies launch themselves from one to the other all the case as a hot potato.
In our experience we cannot get rid of the feeling that the lack of clarity of skills in these cases makes it more than comfortable.
But let's not throw in the towel.
If the backstory information is right and the virtual operator will really soon appear from us, the Czech market of mobile telephony will get back on track.
If one of the operators had consented to the creation of a virtual mobile operator, ÚOHS is unlikely to remain impassive to the refusal (discrimination) of the other data subjects.
And maybe we'll stop calling at higher prices than the rest of Europe.
The new Czech anthem has four new versions.
Choose the best one
Listen to the four new versions of the Czech National Anthem.
On Sunday, Prime Minister Mirek Topolánek presented the new recordings of the national anthem "Kde domov můj".
The new anthem recording was made by the National Theatre orchestra under the direction of Jiří Bělohlávek.
There are four versions available – solo with the male or female voice, chorus step and the instrumental edition.
Singing is under the direction of Kateřina Kněžínková and Adam Plachetka.
The authors of the anthem reviews are Otakar Jeremiáš and Jaroslav Krček.
On the iHNed.cz website you can listen to all four versions.
Bělohlávek considers the Czech national song one of the most beautiful hymns.
Even earlier he told "Český rozhlas" radio that he chose specifically to sing the solo variants, the singers with the most representative young voices, which are expressive and popular.
"From the beginning I tried to take care that both and even the chorus, have a clear diction, that the text is well understandable, that they are in harmony," he said.
Then the national anthem will celebrate next year the 175th anniversary.
The song "Kde domov můj" was first played in 1834 on the stage of the Stavovskéh divadlo in Josef Kajetán Tyl’s original opera Fidlovačka accompanied by the music of František Škroupa.
She gained popularity and people spontaneously made her own.
The first verse of the song after the founding of Czechoslavakia in 1918 then became the natural part of the national anthem.
It remained one of the seven national symbols even after the birth of the Autonomous Czech Republic.
How the "new" hymn sounds
Wednesday MHD of Prague will also accompany you for the first time with an electronic card
Wednesday, October 1, 2008 will be written in the history of the Prague Transport Company as the date on where in the metro, tramway and bus will go up the first passengers who instead of the paper ticket will have an electronic card in their pockets.
At this time they can get it about one hundred thousand people who own their Opencard card.
Passengers can buy the 30- and 90-day and annual one (which will run from 1 January 2009) and at the same time to have their card recharged in the Infopoints and offices of the Transport Company and at the Muzeum, Můstek, Nádraží Holešovice, Anděl, Roztyly, Letňany, Kobylisy, Rajská zahrada metro stop
The coupons can be bought as early as yesterday on the internet at the page of the Transport Company www.dpp.cz.
More than four hundred customers took advantage of the service before the afternoon.
Those who will start using the service via the internet can completely forget the counters in the metro stops where so far they had to make long lines for paper tickets.
The coupon is bought via the internet and on the Opencard they recharge it on one of the twelve branches of the Municipal Library.
"In these places passengers have at their disposal a counter with trained personnel who will help those interested in buying on the internet and will also register on their card the credit acquired," project spokesman Martin Opatrný said.
In addition to the libraries, it will be possible to load the electronic coupons on the Opencards in ATMs and self-services that are installed at metro entrances.
How are these ATMs?
They are red, they look like ticket obliterators and the passengers have already noticed them at Roztyly, Budějovická, I. P. Pavlova, Florenc and Vltavská metro stops.
"Now there are five of them throughout the city but by the end of the year there should be eighty," Opatrný concluded.
The coupon will be checked instead of by the driver from the ATM.
The ATM service that will run from tomorrow is very simple.
Just enter the Opencard card and upon payment of the coupon, it will load above.
Electronic cards are valid from tomorrow not only in the capital but also in its surroundings.
That is, in the urban lines of trains and buses from number 300 up to 499, which the inhabitants of the municipalities of Central Bohemia take to reach the capital.
"In the trains added in the integrated transport of Prague there are controllers equipped with card readers with which they verify the temporal and zonal validity," explained the mode of ticket checking by the Ropid organization, which fine-tunes Prague's collective transport schedules.
City buses have card readers integrated into the driver’s crate.
During the climb it is therefore necessary not only to show the magnetic card to the driver, but in addition you have to place the Opencard at the point determined next to the crate.
Thus the driver verifies the validity of the loaded coupons.
"If the passenger needs to verify the validity of the loaded coupons, there's nothing easier than asking the driver for this type of information," Drápal added.
Passengers who take city buses and don't remember, when the validity of the coupon expires, can again go to self-service ATMs, where you load the coupons on paper.
Here you will see the information about the validity of their documents on the appliance screen.
Sudetian Germans wanted a referendum from the U.S
Sudeten Germans in 1945 asked to include Šumava to the United States.
This is according to the letter filed in the Southern Bohemian Scientific Library.
If we cannot be part of Germany or Austria, we want to live at least under the patronage of the United States.
With this desire, according to what is said, some groups of Sudeten Germans turned in 1945 to the president of the United States.
Moreover, this is clear from the letter kept in a private archive and now deposited in the Scientific Library of Bohemia in the south.
Český Krumlov’s Germans ask in the letter to the American president not to allow the restitution of the Šumava “district” – occupied by Hitler under the Munich Agreement – to Czechoslovakia.
"We have understood the situation and we want to be sure of the promises that American unities arrive at German territory not as oppressors and enemies, but as liberators," he wrote in the letter signed with the words "The people who care about Šumava's fate with all their heart."
Because, they say, the annexation to Czechoslovakia – knowing the Czech national character – gives the Germans of the Sudetens hope only to brutality, lack of concern, misunderstanding, oppression and slavery, the authors of the letter begging the American president to assume the protectorate on Šumava now and always.
"We cannot believe that without regard to the opinion of a quarter of a million inhabitants, you would recognize this territory as a state foreign to us, which oppresses and eradicates ethnic minorities and has not contributed at all, at all, to the victory of the Allies," the letter writes.
The reason perpetrators can't sign it is the alleged fear of thirst for revenge by "Czech oppressors."
If the U.S. president were to doubt Šumava’s conviction, it would be enough to organize in the district a referendum on the willingness of its inhabitants to become part of the United States or live under the command of the United States.
Although the text of the letter may seem naïve with our knowledge of post-war history, according to some witnesses it appropriately describes the expectations of the Sudeten Germans.
Even if the Czech Germans had in the slightest sense that it was going to be their move from the republic, only a few of them admitted such a possibility altogether.
Some were expected that the United States and Britain would negotiate some sort of autonomy for the peoples of the Sudetens.
Others believed they would simply remain in Czechoslovakia.
This is also confirmed by the famous Austrian journalist Gustav Chalupa, born in České Budějovice.
He was mobilized during the war as German of Budějovice in the Maritime Power of the Baltic Sea.
When at the end of the war he was captured by the British and placed in the prison camp in Holštýn , the dozens of other Czech Germans went to visit him for him to be taught Czech.
“They believed that once they got home, the Czech would be convenient for him,” Chalupa said.
None of the German prisoners, who lived in Czechoslovakia before the war, according to him counted on the fact that on the basis of Beneš's decrees they would be moved from the Republic.
Don't think, you'll pay!
Emotional Campaign Will Attack Drivers
The most expensive action in Czech history aimed at road safety.
Even so you could define the emotional media campaign, which the Ministry of Transport intends to start Wednesday.
Minister Aleš Řebíček’s office expects this to discourage drivers from dangerous behaviour behind the wheel.
The 150-million campaign will appear on television, radio, cinema, internet but also on flyers.
It should discourage drivers from driving fast and under the influence of alcohol at the drive.
And also remind him to put on his belt before he leaves.
He wants to touch in the conscience especially young drivers, motorcyclists, pedestrians and not least professional drivers.
What's going to appear in commercials?
Particularly stories of the people who will affect emotions.
And this is also evidenced by the "bloody" slogan of the entire campaign:
Don't think, you'll pay!
The word "you don't think" is capital written on the plaque.
And in a pool of blood there's the word "you'll pay."
This must symbolize that a person can in a moment of distraction pay with their life.
Commercials "scared" viewers until 2010.
The Ministry was inspired by similar campaigns in Anglo-Saxon countries, where you can commonly see images with drastic scenes of accident victims.
It thus promises to lower the number of accidents and deaths on Czech roads.
The commercials have been prepared by Euro RSCG agency.
Carrot and stick to drivers
“With drivers, the carrot and stick system works.
Carrot for them is, for example, when they pass with red and so they save time.
The stick is the danger of the fine or accident.
You have to remember the risk to the drivers, "says the psychologist of the Ministry of Transport Martin Kořán of Na Homolce Hospital.
And so we need information campaigns in the press and especially negative advertisements, the psychologist believes.
According to him the panels that warn drivers of the danger are already less effective.
"If you have installed the fixed panel that warns of danger, it will probably soon be confused with the environment and the drivers won't pay more attention to it," Kořán warns.
Olmert would return almost all the occupied territories in exchange for peace
The Prime Minister of the Provisional Israeli government Ehud Olmert spoke out for the restitution of virtually all the territories occupied in 1967, i.e. the borders of Jordan, East Jerusalem and the Golan Heights, in exchange for peace between Palestinians and Syrians.
So he said in the dialogue he published Monday, the Jediot Achronot newspaper.
"We have to come to an agreement with the Palestinians, this means that it will be necessary to return almost all (occupied) areas, if not all," Olmert told the newspaper.
"We'll hold a certain percentage of these territories, but we'll have to leave the Palestinians the same percentage (of the Israeli zones), because there won't be peace without it," he added.
He has admitted that eventual restitution will also include East Jerusalem.
"He who wants security in Jerusalem and above all does not want bulldozers or espanators to pass on him, as happened to one of my best friends, he will have to give up a part of Jerusalem," Olmert noted in the mention of the two July attacks in the city, during which some people were killed and dozens of people injured.
According to the premier it will be necessary to find "a particular solution for the Mountain of the temple, the sacred and historical places," which lie within the Old City. .
"He who wants to maintain Israeli sovereignty over the whole city will have to integrate 270 000 Arabs.
This will not work,” he also stressed.
Withdrawal from Golan Heights
Olmert also ruled for the withdrawal of Israel from the Golan Heights, i.e. according to him the indispensable condition for concluding this peaceful agreement with Syria.
But this would also require some effort from Damascus.
"I do not propose the conclusion of peace with Syria solely on the basis of withdrawal from Golan," he warned.
“Syrians know what they’ll need to give up in order to get Golan Heights.
They will have to give up the current relationship with Iran; they will have to give up their relationship with Hezbollah (Shia movement); they will have to give up on permanent support, which they offer to the terrorism of Hamas, (Shia movement) of Al-Qaida (the terrorist network) and Jihad (the holy war) in Iraq.
Questions after the accident: Are bus stewards illegal?
The law doesn’t recognize them, you can’t walk on the bus making long-distance routes
In the context of the tragic accident near the Italian city of Padua of the coach of the Student Agency company came out the question of whether stewards and passengers can move aboard the bus during the rise.
"If we want to comply with the laws severely and formally, steward's activity is in conflict with the law of road traffic," says Transport Ministry spokesman Karel Hanzelka.
Last week’s accident with which a 22-year-old hostess lost his life was caused by a truck that cut the way for the coach.
Unfortunately, the girl was sitting at that time next to the chauffeur on the less safe spot of the bus, although there were still two other seats assigned.
But the bus can make an accident or brake abruptly even when distributing the caffe or newspapers.
It depends on whether or not the bus is equipped with standing seats according to the circulating booklet.
If it is non-approvaled, passengers must sit.
Student Agency buses have no stage seats, the director of Radim Jančura told Aktuálnĕ.cz.
"We evaluated this with the Ministry and at the beginning it did not allow the movement of passengers on the bus.
After it was revoked and we have concluded that passengers can use the bathroom during the ride if they have the assigned seat," he expressed his point of view.
Law doesn't recognize steward.
“There is no clear legal interpretation of the role of the steward – no law deals with the regularisation of this role.”
No regulation, however, prohibits the carrier from carrying out the services.
The law of road traffic recognizes only the accompanying term of the vehicle, its rights and duties are not defined, "adds spokesman Hanzelka less clearly.
The Director of the Department of Road Traffic of the Ministry of Transport Josef Pokorný, on the other hand, believes that the law quite cures this problem.
It depends on whether the bus has its homologated standing seats.
That's all," he says.
Categorizes buses in cities where there are standing seats and maximum limited speed, intercity with standing seats with higher maximum speed and longer route without the standing seats with the maximum allowed speed and the seats equipped with seat belts.
As on a plane, the expert says.
BESIP road safety expert Jaroslav Hořín does not find the underlying problem in stewarding on board the bus.
"During a quiet ride there are not so many dangers, the situation can be compared to the service on airplanes.
Not even there is sure when a turbulence arrives and the flight attendant can hit his head.”
"If the bus makes an abrupt braking, of course, the laws of physics apply.
Equally as in the urban bus or on the tram.
The transport company must solve the safety of stewards with an internal regulation," he says and also intends that, from the point of view of the law, the problem is not resolved.
Jančura is willing to suspend services.
Student Agency is the only transport company that offers the services of stewards.
This takes about two hundred.
Even after the accident, the director deems their job dangerous.
"As soon as I have the feeling that I have to consider the profession of steward as a dangerous job, I will abolish it.
Passengers can receive cafes and newspapers as they go up.
They'll also decrease expenses," he says.
Asiana director Alexej Litvin, who manages a fraction of lines compared to the Student Agency, motivated the absence of stewards on its means as an interest in safety.
“We don’t have hostesses, we’re opposed from the very beginning.
Sooner or later it'll end with a fatal accident," Týden.cz said less than a year ago.
An immense tragedy has happened.
In the accident of the line for Naples died a young hostess of the Plzeň region, who had worked for the company two years.
A Slovenian truck probably got out of the emergency lane without using the arrow and blocked the road from the coach.
The driver of the bus tried to avoid it with an abrupt manoeuvre, but bumped it with the front right.
Jančura rejects the fact that the two places reserved for the rest of the stewarts had been occupied.
"It is not possible to reserve these seats in long-term coaches, they are unbelievable."
The company plans to denounce the driver of the truck.
The agency will not wait for the judgment of the Court to pay damages to the girl’s family, but he expects financial compensation from the culprit.
Revision of the Law?
Not in sight
The revision of the law, on which a group of 30 experts of the Ministry of Transport for more than a year has been working, could lead to specifying the movement of stewarts on board buses.
"They have not achieved any concrete results though.
They don’t consider buses as much, they deal more with the system with scoring, maximum speed on the highway and the use of the mobile phone while driving,” says expert Hořín.
"The Ministry of Transport does not regard the activity of stewarts as a major threat to the safety of passengers," with this concludes spokesman Havelka, that the control of bus security is the responsibility of the police and security offices.
Düsseldorf's hopes point to Frankfurt
There is no shortage of words in WestLB's letter addressed to employees of the house.
In the letter published on Monday that "the center of the capital market of the financial group of the savings banks" could be formed through the unit meeting of the Düsseldorf bank with the Deka-Bank in Frankfurt.
The West LB would launch "competent lockdown" with lasting profits in such a business.
Moreover, all such a union could be a decisive push for further ties in the Länder banking sector.
So it's readed, when a board of directors in difficult times wants to demonstrate the capacity for action.
As the afflicted WestLB is still under intense pressure.
Not only by the EU Commission, but also through the National Financial Supervision Service Authority, having pointed out some time ago to the Regional Government of the North Rhine-Westphalia that time is running out in search of a future solution.
Of the links between Deka-Bank, which the same customers of the Savings Banks because of their investment funds already know, and WestLB certainly have been talked about for quite a while, even by Frankfurt.
At the end of August, Deka-Bank head Franz Waas reported at the time of the first-half-of-the-year turnover presentation that a merger of the house at Via Magonza with a national bank such as Helaba or West LB makes no sense.
But Deka's board member, who is half brought in by regional banks and savings bank consortia, has delegated the Board of Directors to conduct talks with the West LB on joint negotiations.
It could be market negotiations or parts of them.
Conversations, however, have, as they say in the business square, just begun.
Of the Deka-Bank, with its 3000 collaborators is one of those that matter most in the business square in Frankfurt, there is no talk for the first time when it comes to consolidating the savings sector.
In 2003 for a short time it seemed that Deka-Bank could merge with the regional bank of the Rhineland-Palatinate and the Helaba.
Of this three-way association nothing was done about it.
As soon as the projects were published, Hessia's regional bank was opposed to Helaba's participation.
Even the remaining two institutes proved unable to reach an agreement.
The Rhenania-Palatinate regional bank went instead to Baden-Württemberg's regional bank in Stuttgart.
Yet they have always often thought about a merger of the Deka-Bank with regional banks.
Günther Merl, chairman of Helaba’s Governing Council, finally spoke out against design games to reunite with his house.
Set aside, this seems to create cognition, that it is necessary that the different houses of the public sector be approached.
Given that the election of the Bavarian Parliament has passed, a meeting of the Bavarian Landesbank with that of Baden-Württemberg could first occur.
For Helaba it's not entirely good news.
If it increases the pressure for other mergers, it will gradually miss the partners.
In the South there would now be the junior partner, although so far it has overcome the financial crisis much better than the houses in Munich and Stuttgart.
The association of Assia-Turingia savings banks that participates with 85 percent of the Helaba, has put the bolt at the meeting with WestLB.
Only NordLB would remain
These geo-strategic reasonings are just an account.
On the other hand there is also the question of how the different business models of the individual houses matcher.
In June Merl pointed out that the decisive question, with which business model the future of regional banks would be ensured, would not yet be answered by the association of German savings banks.
In the end it could also come out the division of West LB, which by that of course is not desired.
Thus the letter from the administration's board expresses a willingness to aspire in any case to a "general solution."
We will see how the reunification of the individual parts with the Deka-Bank will work.
The second week of the beer festival is dedicated to Italians
For Elena it's the first time while for Luca it's third or fourth.
This time Elena is present at the party as a companion, and so she says that everything is different.
Behind them next to the barrel of 5l. of the brand Kaiser stands a bearded massif and demonstrates to the about 200 travelers what are the deep tones it manages to produce from the throat.
Elena first gives Luca a pat and after a kiss.
They came from Bologna to Verona.
From here to the bus car park next to Porta Nuova station, leave this evening at 23.45 four courier with destination Monaco, Beer Festival ", arrival scheduled at 6.30 a.m.
Three couriers are mainly reserved for Italians, in another they travel only Brazilians.
In one corner of the car park the South Americans are chanting with the flag in their hands, having already celebrated overnight.
It's so noisy, says Marco, that it's none for Italians.
Marco who wears a long coat with fake fur lining and a pair of goggles with silver wide splints belongs to the buses.
For 5 years they have been going to the Oktoberfest, this year it's 3 tours.
Walking at night, by day at the party, back at 22.30.
