Prices for airline tickets and hotels are falling
In the first half of the year of 2008 flights and overnight stays in Europe were cheaper than in the previous year.
According to a current study in Germany, flights would be up to 50% cheaper
Flights and overnight stays at the hotel have become cheaper again - this is the result of a study by the American Express financial and traveling provider.
The company analyzed its first-half booking data of the year of 2008 and after an increase in prices in the first quarter observed a noticeable decline in the second quarter.
After prices on the hotel market have increased for years, overnight stays in many places have already dropped in price.
From January to March prices rise in 41 of the 48 European cities still surveyed on average at around 6.9 compared to 2007
However from April to June prices were already 3,6 % lower than in the previous year.
The most obvious trend would have been in Paris and Rome, where travelers had to pay 30 up to 37% less than a year earlier.
The American Express sees as the reason for this the weak price of the dollar, which would have led to a "waning desire to travel for American tourists"
Even financial centers such as Frankfurt and London would be affected by the recession of hotel prices more strongly than industrial centers such as Hanover, Colony or Stavanger
In the Norwegian city, hotel prices an increase of 26.3 % even hold the record in Europe
With regard to flight prices, the trend in the first trimester is still up:
Due to cherosene price increases, Economy Class passengers had to pay an average of 7.1% compared to 2007, intensive long-haul fuel flights would be up to 29.2% more expensive
From April to June, Economy Class air ticket prices fell by an average of 12.5% compared to the previous quarter, in the first class and in the Business, however, price changes were far weaker.
In Germany, prices have plummeted even stronger than in other European countries:
From January to March prices had risen by 11,9 %, in the second trimester they fell by 23,6 %.
As for internal German flights according to the study, prices have even halved.
On the one hand, the downward economic growth, the company explains.
Demand in many segments of the air travel market has crumbled.
On the other side there would be due to the agreement on the open skies, which liberalizes air traffic between the United States and Europe, an increased pressure of competition.
A sign of the critical situation would be the suppression of unprofitable routes at Airlines, as well as the high number of airlines that in the first half of 2008 would have declared bankruptcy – in Europe alone it was 24 airlines.
