Dark matter affects the flight of space probes
For years a mysterious phenomenon has created perplexity among scientists: dark matter influences the speed of space probes during the so-called Swing-by manoeuvre.
Experts fear that Albert Einsteins' general theory of relativity may be not entirely accurate.
Ulysses spacecraft has been flying through space for nearly 18 years.
Due to fast-freezing fuel pipings, death by freeze now looms over her.
Among scientists for years a mysterious phenomenon creates havoc – the anomaly of/or Swing-by
Swing-by is a flight manoeuvre of the space probes, with which they achieve additional momentum/push/oscillation in the gravitational field of a celestial body, for example of the Earth.
Probes can save a lot of fuel.
Euro-American research probe Cassini-Huygens used this physical expedient as well as the Ulysses solar probe.
What amazes scholars for years already: rockets do not behave quite as one would expect according to the law on gravitation.
On the contrary, there are irregularities in speed.
Probes suddenly get faster or slower.
So far physicists have not been able to explain this effect.
The year renamed the Swing-by anomaly.
Some experts fear that perhaps even Albert Einstein's general theory of relativity, which describes the attractive force of matter with a curvature of space, could be not entirely accurate.
U.S. physicist Stephen Adler of the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton firmly believes as before Einstein was not wrong, and attempts to explain Swing-by anomaly through the so-called dark matter that astronomers have spotted in the cosmos.
"Responsible for the anomaly it could be the dark matter that could also be very close to the Earth," he writes.
Of the existence of dark mattle, space scholars have only been aware for about 10 years.
From her evidently a force of attraction emerges, whose effect on the other celestial bodies is seen.
She herself is completely invisible, i.e. does not send or reflect any electromagnetic radiation whatsoever, especially no visible light.
According to scientists' calculations, dark matter makes up at least one-fifth of the entire mass of the cosmos.
Kidnapped tourists in Egypt are free
After 10 days in the hands of their kidnappers the tourists kidnapped in Egypt would be free again, as confirmed by the Italian foreign minister.
Kidnappers from Sudan had demanded several million euros of ransom.
In a shooting before the release 6 kidnappers turn out to have been killed.
5 Germans have been abducted in southern Egypt.
Negotiations for the ransom are already ongoing.
The Germans are always in the crosshairs of the kidnappers.
WELT ONLINES describes the most important cases.
European tourists abducted in the Sahara are undoubtedly free.
This was agreed by Egyptian state television, Arab broadcaster Al-Arabija and the Italian media.
Foreign Minister Franco Frattini confirmed the release after the chronicle of Italian broadcaster Sky TG 24: "Our fellow citizens and the other hostages have been freed
We're looking at their state of health," Frattini said.
The 5 Germans, 5 Italians and a Romanian as well as their 8 Egyptian chaperones would be in good health and safe, Egyptian state television reported.
The 19 members of an expedition to the desert are already on their way to Cairo.
It remains unclear the backstory of a shooting that took place before the hostages were released: therefore, the kidnappers after the indications of an Egyptian public security agent would have been ready for the conclusion of the kidnapping, before the Sudanese army killed 6 of them by surprise.
"The problem had been resolved.
They agreed for a ransom," the official said.
It would "still just be about taking over the hostages", when "this surprise" suddenly came.
Sudanese soldiers on Sunday in the border zone between Sudan, Chad and Egypt had shot dead 6 of the kidnappers and captured 2 of them.
The kidnappers had demanded from Germany a ransom of the amount of 6 million euros; the money should have been handed over by the German wife of one of the hostages.
The newspaper "El Masri El Jom" reported in reference to a German intermediary, the release of the hostages would be delayed, because the kidnappers feared for their own safety.
According to the report, the kidnappers wanted to release the women hostage after the payment of ransom.
The men were supposed to be detained as long as the kidnappers didn't have a safe escape.
The German Embassy in Cairo has refused a stance regarding the report.
Patrisan abuse 'led to suicide'
Years of abuse by the stepfather led a woman to kill herself, a court said.
Michael Trerise, 57, of Stithians, Cornwall, has been in jail for 6 years after admitting eight cases of sexual assault.
Truro Crown Court learned that he regularly harassed his stepdaughter Stephanie Randle, when she was between five and seven.
Trerise wrote in a note of the confession: "I feel responsible for his death."
The court learned that Miss Randle had killed herself at her home in Mutley, Plymouth, on April 1, 2007, at the age of 34.
When she was a little girl and living in Coventry, Trerise molested and blindfolded her before she had sex, while her mother was out working in a pub.
Trerise and his wife divorced in the 1980s, and he returned to live in Cornwall.
His stepdaughter went to Plymouth.
But the court learned that Ms. Randle, who had spented a lot of time as a volunteer at the National Trust, was tormented by the abuses they had caused her.
In 2006, Public Prosecutor Ramsey Quaife told the court that Miss Randle's friends, who had been a very good surfer and cyclist, was "emotionally dead," and that she had "leaved with life."
In April 2007 her real father, Terrance Randle, found her dead in her apartment in Plymouth.
She is thought to have killed herself choking herself with a plastic bag.
Mr. Randle comforted his daughter's stepfather, who wrote a letter of confession that ended: "I would tie and blindfold her."
“I didn’t sexually harass her.
“I feel responsible for his death.”
Judge Christopher Elwen told Trerise: "She's an extremely strange fetishist, and it must've been terrifying for her victim, especially when she was only seven.
"This unpleasant saga ends with the victim taking his own life.
Even harsher is the fact that if he didn't, it would have been hard for the facts to have ended up before the court."
I note the effects that Stephanie's father and mother describe having had on their daughter over these years.
"Otherwise it was clearly likely that she had been a promising and happy successful young woman."
The officer who lost data will be punished.
The Cabinet Officer who left top-secret documents on a train in June must be blamed by the Official Secrets Act, the BBB learnt.
The man was away from the Ministry of Defence when he left on the train to Waterloo two highly classified documents.
The documents were passed to the BBC before being handed over to the police.
It's the least delicate of the accusations made according to this law and there's nothing that suggests a criminal intent.
BBC defence correspondent Frank Gardner said the movement would come as a surprise to many of the administration.
"They would have thought that this was being treated internally to the Mod and the Cabinet Office," he said.
The employee, who cannot be appointed, is about to be charged under Section 8.1 of the act, the clause concerning the safeguarding of information.
The Crown Prosecution Service has recommended that the officer must be charged by the Metropolitan Police because of the highly sensitive nature of his work.
Lost documents contained assessments on al-Qaaeda and the capabilities of Iraq's security forces.
He was briefed of the decision on Monday morning and was taken away from his home and taken to a hidden location.
Construction companies deal with B&B’s account
English construction companies could face an account of more than £80,000 after bailing out the Bradford & Bingley bank.
The government has given 14 billion pounds to protect Bradford & Bingley's 2.6-million savers deposits.
Interest in that loan will be paid by all companies that take saver money, including the 59 construction companies.
Building Societies Association (BSA) general manager Adrian Coles said it is "irritating" that companies and their members have to pay.
Loan of 14billion pounds will eventually be repaid when Bradford & Bingley's mortgages are redeemed.
But the interest that will mature in the meantime will be charged every year to the 700 financial companies that take deposits. But the interest that will mature in the meantime will be charged every year to the 700 financial companies that accept deposits.
The Financial Services Compensation Scheme has confirmed to the BBC that the 59 construction companies would be included in that number.
It's irritating that those institutions that have behaved prudently... are now called upon to pay part of the bill of those institutions that have been much less prudent.
Adrian Coles, BSA
He said that every company had to pay in proportion to the deposits it had, and that the companies among them had to pay 18% of the cost.
The Treasury estimates that interest on the loan will amount to £450m in 2009.
This means companies will have to find £81m to pay the first year of interest.
Adrian Coles, general manager of the construction company association, told the BCB he believes is unfair.
"It is irritating that those institutions that have behaved cautiously in the resumpting market of homes are now called upon to pay some of those institutions that have been much less prudent."
According to BSA, no company has failed since documentation began in 1945.
“We need to examine all aspects in the coming weeks to see what options there are to protect building societies and their members from what could be a significant account,” Cole said.
Interest due in 2009 covers seven months from the end of September this year to the end of March.
In 2010 the interest of an entire year will be due and this could be almost twice as high.
Since construction companies are mutual organizations without shareholders, their members end up paying the bill through higher rates or lower interest rates on savings accounts.
The milk rises to 5 pennies per liter
People in Jersey are facing the fact that they have to pay more for their milk.
Jersey Dairy has announced that there will be an increase in the wholesale prices of its milk for an extra five penny per litre from Monday.
