Denied bail to former Prime Minister of Bangladesh
A Bangladeshi court has refused a bailing request from previous Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina, which means she could be arrested when she returns to the country.
Her lawyers said the court refused to grant her bail for extorting 50 million taka (729,700 dollars) to a businessman when he was a member of parliament
The case is one of the many allegations of bribery hanging out against her.
Sheikh Hasina's long-time political rival, Khaleda Zia, had been released on bail earlier in the month.
Sheikh Hasina's return to Bangladesh is planned for the next few weeks but his party, Awami League, has demanded the military-backed country's temporary government first gives it total freedom.
Correspondents say that while she was going abroad for medical care on parole, it's unclear according to the terms of her release, if she can stay free when she returns to Bangladesh.
His attorneys say she will appeal to the Supreme Court against the High Court order.
"The high court has refused the request for bail for Sheikh Hasina alleging that she cannot have the bail since she is already abroad on parole for medical treatment," says his lawyer Rafique-ul Huq.
"We will file an appeal against the Order of the High Court."
His lawyers explain that he needs bail to return to the country from abroad to prepare for the December elections.
He expects the return home of Sheikh Hasina after October 17, an Awami League party senior leader said on Sunday.
She was detained last year after the government flanked the military force took power, but was released in June, to go abroad to receive medical treatment.
Both Sheikh Hasina and Khaleda Zia deny all charges against themselves.
