BSkyB Loses Legal Battle with ITV
BSkyB lost its legal battle with the Competition Commission's decision to oblige the company to reduce its participation in the rival ITV television station.
BSkyB has been told by the competition's appealing tribunal to cut its shares in ITV from 17.9% to less than 7.5%.
Previously, the commission had said BSkyB's participation hampered the competition and allowed an unjust influence on ITV.
BSkyB purchased participation in 2006 in an agreement that effectively blocked NTL, now renamed Virgin Media, from buying ITV.
In April 2007, the Office of Fair Trading reported that the acquisition of BSkyB participation in ITV sows doubts about ITV's independence, causing the Competition Commission to launch an investigation.
Virgin's boss, Sir Richard Branson, consumer groups and Ofcom's regulator opposed the purchase.
BSkyB argued that forcing it to reduce its ITV stake was unreasonable and a disproportiortionate particularly - remedy as BSkyB had it said it would surrender its voting rights.
New U.S. prosecutor's case investigation
The U.S. attorney general appointed a Special Public Prosecutor to examine the dismissal of nine U.S. lawyers in 2006.
The operation occurred when a report on the case by the Inspector General of the Department of Justicial Affairs criticised the actions of administrative officers.
The report was critical towards the White House, lawmakers and former Attorney General Alberto Gonzales.
The investigation was triggered by fear that prosecutors had been removed for political reasons.
Special prosecutor Nora Dannehy will now investigate whether the officers questioned during the investigation lied to investigators in an attempt to obstruct justice.
"Serious accusations regarding potentially criminal behaviour have not been fully investigated or ascertained," Inspector General Glenn Fine said in his report.
The assignment of a special prosecutor with the power to compel witnesses to testify was one of the main recommendations.
The dispute began when nine U.S. prosecutors were removed from their positions by the Department of Justice in December 2006.
At the time, the department said lawyers had been fired for poor performance.
But later it emerged that the dismissed lawyers had not been assessed more poorly than many other colleagues, leading many observers to conclude that the dismissal had been motivated by politics.
Some of the prosecutors had been involved in the prosecution against high-profile congressional Republican members, while others had refused to inquire about fraud against Democratic voters.
U.S. prosecutors investigate and bring lawsuits to court on behalf of the government.
They may be dismissed at any time, but they cannot be dismissed for political reasons.
The then-header of the Justice Department, Attorney General Alberto Gonzales, found himself in the storm for dismissals, and eventually reassed his position.
A number of other officials in the justice department quit.
(The inspector general’s report was very critical of Mr Gonzales’ role in the case, saying that he held a primary responsibility in dismissals, which he had abdicated to his role as leader and had been extraordinarily detached during the trial.
Other officials, including Bush's previous senior assistant Karl Rove, were criticized for not cooperating with investigations.
Meat Loaf epic will be celebrated
Rock star Meat Loaf is about to be honoured at the Q Awards for her epic hymn Bat Out Of Hell.
The singer, which will retire his special gold prize at next week's ceremony in London, said he was "honoured" to earn recognition.
The song, which lasts nine minutes and fifty seconds, was the track title of Meat Loaf's 1977 hit.
Coldplay will conduct nominations for this year's awards, being on the list of nominees in four categories.
Paul Rees, editor-in-chief of Q magazine, said: "There are a few songs that transcend things like time and genre, and Bat Out Of Hell is undoubtedly one of them."
The song, composed by Jim Steinman, required a strong vocality that was provided by Meat Loaf.
It opened with an instrumental section that lasted almost two minutes.
The song was performed as a single in the UK in 1979 and reached 15th place.
But Meat Loaf's album Bat Out Of Hell remained nearly nine years on the charts and sold more than 40 million copies worldwide.
Meat Loaf - real name Marvin Lee Aday - has suffered from health problems in recent years, including surgery for an irregular heartbeat in 2003 after collapsing on stage.
In 2007, acute laryngiths forced him to cancel concerts.
It emerged later that he had a cyst on a vocal string.
Obscure city streets, often forgotten and perhaps soon for sale
New York City has thousands of avenues, boulevards, roads and other crossbars, some famous, others merely used.
But also many veteran taxi drivers and residents for a long time find it difficult to give the location of Extra Place, mostly because they've never heard of it.
The tiny road is in the East Village and goes north from the First Road – without making the Second Road – between Bowery and the Second Avenue.
Approximately 30 feet wide and 120 in length, it looks more like a dusty alley than an active busy route.
There are no traffic signs.
It's virtually impossible to see her on a normal atlas.
However, the road must have admirers.
For example, there's Avalon Bay, the real estative of luxury buildings recently built on First Street.
You want to reopen Extra Place and create a cleaner ride for shops and boutiques that should open in the new buildings.
Other admirers include long-time neighbors who say they were hit by truncated street simply because it was one of the last remnants of the state district of the past.
But the city's department of preservation and development, which has control of the streets, has no interest in keeping it.
During a meeting of the committee for the use of the land of the municipal commission of the district some representatives of the agency of the city and Avalon Bay outlined a proposal for the real estate company to buy the streets.
But the committee prefers that the road will remain in the hands of the city, and has called for a new plan to be presented next month, says Susan Stetzer, the director of the Community Board 3 district.
"There's very little space left to the city property, and we'd like the city to continue to own Extra Place," he said.
"There could be proposals to fix it and manage it, which could be done by Avalon, but we also want to ensure public access."
Avalon Bay many months ago announced that it planned to reopen the small street and install seats, including some to be used for a cafe at which the real estate company hopes to lease the space.
The idea that Avalon Bay would buy the road was made public for the first time at this month's meeting.
Michele de Milly, a spokesman for Avalon Bay, said that although the company wants to use the road, it doesn't necessarily mean he wants to own it.
"Avalon Bay's main concern is to revamp this derelict alley and turn it into a public amenity," he said, adding that the city suggested the sale.
Seth Donlin, a Housing Preservation spokesman, said it was the city's desire to sell the road and pointed out that the agency's purpose was to develop ownership rather than administer it.
Comparing to its most colorful neighbor, Bowery, Extra Place has left a slight historical footprint.
Some believe that it was named in 1802, when a large farm owned by Philip Minthorne was divided between his many children and the narrow strip of land was not claimed.
CBGB's back door, the punk-rock monument that closed in 2006, opened on Extra Place, and the street is perhaps best known as the set for the cover photo of the Ramones album "Rocket to Russia."
"The land was splendid," said Danny Fields, manager of the Ramones, who took the picture in November 1976.
"It was full of garbage, shreds of clothes and pieces of barrels, posters, leaves, ropes."
Some residents, including some bloggers who write about "gentrification" of the East Village, have objected to the idea of selling Extra Place, adducting that the piece of public ownership, modest as it is, should remain public.
Others lauded Extra Place as a rarity – an almost never-changed city fragment that should be left alone.
Jeremiah Moss, who has a blog called Jeremiah's Vanishing New York (subtitled, "The Book of Lamentations: A somewhat nostalgic look at the endangered city"), published part of a City History Speech that appeared on The New Yorker in 1952.
The author, Brendan Gill, wrote: "Extra Place is a cramped dead-end, dark even by day and demarcated doors of rusty iron warehouses and windows with gates, with weeks-old newspapers fluttering along the slums."
With a few exceptions, the road seemed almost the same as a recent evening.
A rat racked easily through the back of the road near a door leading inside the CBGB, which is now the site of an aristocratic clothing store.
On the walls were graffiti and a 22-ounce bottle of empty beer lies next to a group of fresh abandoned newspapers.
Few passers-by have said they rarely thought of Extra Place but suggest it would need to be a bit embellished.
Mr. Fields, for his part, has said she also thinks some people underestimate the attraction of the street in his current state.
“The best years of my life,” he said, “were in black, dirty and disgusting places.”
Bilingual Debate: Immersion in English
In this instance of Educational Gaze, Bruce Fuller and Lance T. Izumi discuss the candidates' position on bilingual education.
Lance T. Izumi, a senior member in California studies and senior director of educational studies at the Pacific Research Institute for Public Policy, is the co-author of the book “Not as good as you think: Because the middle class needs to choose a school.”
Truly attracting Hispanic voters is a complicated business.
Barack Obama's educational projects are a significant example.
Mr. Obama's campaign notes that, "Afro-American and Latin students are significantly less likely to graduate than white students, "which is true.
To combat these shortcomings in achieving goals, Obama's educational plan specifically supports, among other things, "transitional bilingual education" for English students.
And yet, the question for Obama is whether his commitment to bilingual education, which emphasizes classroom teaching of languages other than English, doesn't take into account his interest in filling learning gaps.
Take, for example, Sixth Street Prep, an elementary school in eastern Los Angeles County.
The students of the school are mostly Hispanic and low-income.
More than a third of students, many of whom have recently arrived, are studying English.
But, among the fourth pupils, an astonishing 100 percent of the students took the top-level test in the 2008 math status exam.
An equally surprising 93 per cent of fourth pupils did the expert art-of-language-engline level test.
This incredible result was achieved using a different ingredient from Mr. Obama's preferred one.
Sixth Street emphasizes the review and exercise, constant assessment of skills and an "no apologies" type.
Also, and that's where Mr. Obama is expected to take note, according to Linda Mikels, director of Sixth Street, the school's obstructive approach to English students is "full immersion."
The use of English emphasizes the almost exclusive use of English in the context of education.
Ms. Mikels, who opposes bilingual education, told me, "we've had fantastic success with a student who just came from Mexico and if you'd entered the classroom 12 months later you wouldn't have been able to pinpoint what he was."
"It works," he noted "it works for us."
Would Obama model a school like Sixth Street Prep to replicate it in other Hispanic-majority schools and with English students?
The results achieved by the school make the answer to that question very easy, but educational politics in his own party (the National Education Associations is a long-time advocate of bilingual education) and his own ongoing support for bilingual education prevent from predicting Mr. Obama's response.
While he agrees that immigrants should study English, Obama recently made the topic trivial when he said that people should stop worrying about "English-only" legislation.
Instead, he said, "you have to make sure that your children are able to speak Spanish."
If Obama truly wishes to close these attainment gaps, he should carefully consider the educational models that work rather than despising or trivializing them.
Oil drops abruptly and renews economic fear
Oil prices fell sharply on Monday due to concerns that even America's bailout plan for financial markets will perhaps fail to raise the economy.
Crude oil futures fell 7 percent to 99.80 dollars a barrel at New York's merchant stock exchange in morning operations.
Prices have lost more than 20 dollars a barrel since last Monday.
Over the past two weeks, commodity markets have been shaken by Wall Street's financial agitation, and a couple of powerful hurricanes in the Gulf of Mexico.
After peaking at 145.26 dollars a barrel in June, prices had plummeted to nearly $90 per barrel earlier this month, before rising again, against the backdrop of tremendous uncertainty in the financial markets.
Anxiety affected investors again on Monday even after congressional leaders had said they reached an agreement over the weekend about the bailout plan, the largest in history.
The news was overshadowed by fresh concerns that the financial crisis was far from having passed.
In the latest episode of the progressive merger, Citigroup will buy Wachovia Corporation's banking operations, the government said on Monday.
At the same time the Belgian, Dutch and Luxembourg governments partially nationalised the European financial conglomerate Fortis.
Barclays Capital analysts said fast-paced weekend negotiations had led to a bailout deal 'seem to have failed to revive market sentiment.
As the economic situation worsens, demand for raw materials, including oil, is expected to calm down.
"The outlook for global equity, interest rates, and exchange rate markets has become uncertain," Deutsche Bank analysts wrote in a note to investors.
"We believe that raw materials will not be able to escape contagion.
From a raw materials perspective, our most pressing concern is to what extent the American virus will spread globally and specifically to China.”
Banking analysts lowered their expectations for next year while home oil consumption due to slowing economic growth, reducing oil and gas pricing forecasts by about 20 percent for 2009.
Exploit in the 2nd quarter scores a team record.
After spending more time than anyone with the Jets uniform in the past eight and multiple seasons, defender Shaun Ellis thought he had seen everything.
Then he watched the second-quarter unfold on Sunday when the Jets turned a points-free game into a comeback.
“Never seen such a thing,” Ellis said.
The second quarter began after the Jets and Arizona Cardinals matched the grey of the ominous clouds overpowering them with a sluggish game.
Then quarterback Brett Favre found Laveranues Coles for a touchdown of 12 yards, providing the perfect signal for an afternoon where their growing tuning made a gigantic step forward.
Favre found Coles for two more touchdowns in the fourth, including one when a cornerback fell and another with 10 seconds left at the end of the first half.
"I didn't get out of my path" said Favre of all the passages arrived in Coles.
He's an amazing player.
“Our communication was great, despite what people can think of.”
While Favre and Coles were making takeover games, the defense hit Cardinals quarterback Kurt Warner heavily.
Cornerback Darrelle Revis intercepted a stray throw and relaunched it for 32 yards in a touchdown.
Eric Shimth security predicted another ride and intercepted him.
Linebaker David Bowens forces a Warner fumble.
The latest turnover, the fumble forced by Bowens, may have been the most notable.
The Cardinals went ahead over that possession of the ball, with 10 seconds left, and after the Jets had taken back the ball with 10 minutes left, Jay Feely kicked a goal from 20 yards, for a 34-to-0 lead.
It was more than the Jets ever scored in a quarter.
When asked to describe his madness, Jets coach Eric Mangini said, "The second fourth or third quarter?"
When he was told both - the Jets conceded 21 unanswered points in the third quarter - Mangini said: "I can tell you that I really liked the second quarter than the third."
There have been a lot of points in a small amount of time.”
Rested Gebrelassie surpasses her world record
Haile Gebrselassie of Ethiopia showed again because many consider him the best marathon runner ever, breaking his own world record at the Berlin Marathon on Sunday and becoming the first person to run a 26.2-mile race under 2 hours and 4 minutes.
With a victory over the race favorably flat in 2:03:59, Gebrselassie shattered for 27 seconds his previous score of 2:03:26, raced last year in Berlin.
At 35, Gebrselassie sets the 26th world record for such a stunning career that he produced a world championship or world record at every distance from metric mile to marathon.
Last month, Gebrselassie skipped Beijing's Olympic marathon, fearing - perhaps too alarmingly, in retrospective - that air pollution would worsen its asthma problems.
Instead, he decided to race in Berlin.
In near-perfect conditions - sunny and 48 degrees at the start, 55 degrees on arrival - Gebrselassie won on Sunday with a burning average of 4 minutes and 43 seconds per mile.
As soon as half-browsters like Gebrselassie and Paul Tergat of Kenya went to long distances, the marathon record fell by more than two minutes in the past decades, since Ronaldo from Brazil's Costa won in Berlin in 2:06:05 in 1998.
After Sunday's victory, Gebrselassie told reporters that two weeks ago he had cramps to his calves and lacked a week of training.
Clearly, rest left it fresh, not rusty.
"I'm so happy," Gebrselassie told reporters.
"Everything was perfect.
“I started again a week ago, and I had doubts today, but in the end everything went well.”
He's been somewhat criticised - especially in Kenya, Ethiopia's east-of-Friendy rival - for over-orchestrating his races, generally avoiding many of the world's top marathon stars, relying on opender as if it were a half-border race, and placing world records above the sense of competition.
But these criticisms rarely diminish Gebrselassie's skills as a marathon runner.
With the pitch cadenced by four Kenyans on Sunday, Gebrselassie reached the halfway point in 1:02:04.
Two elite Kenyan runners, James Kwambai and Charles Kamathi, held Gebrselassie in the competition after the openers vanished.
Just over three miles from arrival, Gebrselassie made a final surge to separate from Kwambai, who finished second in 2:05:36, improving his best personal time by almost 5 minutes.
Kamathi finished third in 2:07:48.
In the women’s race, Irina Mikitenko, a native of Kazakhstan who is now a German citizen, has completed a remarkable year winning in 2:19:19, the seventh fastest time ever.
Pauda Radcliffe of Great Britain holds the world record of 2:15:25.
Last spring, Mikitenko, 36, was a surprise winner of the London Marathon in 2:24:14.
She missed the Beijing Games with what's being described as a bad accident, but she's healed to win in Berlin, doing her best personal time of almost five minutes in the third marathon she's raced.
She ran a race doing the calculations well, letting a quartet of Ethiopian and Kenyan runners leave with a great detachment, resuming them after about 18 and a half miles.
Askale Tafa Magarsa of Ethiopia came second in 2:21.31, while Helena Kiprop of Kenya finished third in 2:25:01.
In a separate city, pride and processes of a black life
Tucked away in the themed park on the suburbs of Orlando, a few miles from the splendor, the noise, the huge, lies the quiet city where the pride and complications of the African-American experience come into life.
Eatonville, the first all-black town embedded in the country and Zora Neale Hurston's birthplace, is no longer as simple as it described in 1935: "the city of five lakes, three croquet fields, 300 brown skins, 300 good swimmers, plenty of guavas, two schools and no prisons."
It is now a place of pilgrimage.
Alice Walker, Toni Morrison and Ruby Dee came to the annual Zora! Festival in Eatonville to show their respect to Hurston, Harlem's most famous writer.
Yet in many ways, the city he described - and that makes tourists stop by understanding it in the Florida's tour guide produced by the Federal Writers' Project - remains a place apart.
It's as independent, dignified and private as it was in the 1930s, when Hurston wrote that rural blacks in Florida often resisted sharing their thoughts with the white man, who "knowing so little about us, doesn't know what you're missing."
Even now, in a year where a black presidential nominee, Senator Barack Obama, has been called up for an opening speech on the race, many here remain distrustful of the outsider's gaze.
“We’re very cautious about how our story is told,” said Hortense Jones, 59, a lifelong resident and a member of the city’s oldest church.
"It must be right."
Eatonville has long been defined as a paradox of triumph and difficulty.
It's both a historic model of black seizure and a community of nearly 2,400 where the poverty rate is twice the national average.
It's a literary center but also an example in the shadow of the oaks of the rural culture of southern blacks - sometimes disdained, sometimes rewarded - born of American slavery.
Unsurprisingly, residents here are both proud and protective.
And the concern for Eatonville's imagine really began with Zora, whom everyone here calls Hurston.
She presented the world to her hometown through sincere, dialect-rich books, such as "Mules and Men" (1935) and "Their Eyes Were Watching God" (1937).
Five paragraphs of the Florida guide transformed the city, just outside Rout 17, a road that runs through Florida's often-forgotten centre into a scenario from black history and human drama
Low as a bass drum in both life and literature, Hurston took readers to the store owned by Eatonville's first mayor, Joe Clarke, and then diverted to more private areas.
"Off the street to the left," he wrote "it's the modern public school, brown with white trim, with its well-kept gardens and courtyards, which Howard Miller always looked after, thinking he could just read and write."
She also mentioned Widow Dash's new husband and wrote that Lee Glenn "sells beverages of all kinds and whatever fits the passing rooms."
So in just a few hundred words, Hurston connected Eatonville with self-government but also with illiteracy, second marriage and sex.
Clearly this wasn't Fodor's.
In fact, it wasn’t a portrait that everyone appreciated.
"Zora used to say it as it was," said Ella Dinkins, 90, one of Johnson's girls that Hurston immortalizes by quoting men chanting coarse songs about their beauty.
She adds: "Some people don't like it."
Hurston is still remembered here as a lively eccentric who frequently returned from her family who moved to Jacksonville, Fla.
Augustus Franklin, 77, recalls that when Hurston swooped into the city, he habitually arrived unannounced at a triumphant Chevrolt, smoking and wearing pants in a town that even today prides itself on dignified clothing.
Many inhabitants were captivated, Mr. Franklin says, while others sneered.
“People were always very pleased to see Zora,” said Mr. Franklin.
But, he added, swinging in his chair on the patio at the back above Lake Sabelia, where Hurston was most likely christened, "she never stayed for long."
When Hurston died in 1960, he was poor and his books had gone out of print.
Together with the rest of the world, Eatonville seemed to have forgotten her.
Although she was once a star of literature, a contemporary star of Langston Hughes and the only black woman at Barnard College in the 1920s, she was buried in a plaqueless tomb at Fort Pierce, Fla., where she had lived.
In Eatonville, there were no great commemorative ceremonies, and no major conferences.
“I don’t think they understand her contribution to the world or her legacy to everyone,” said Valerie Boyd, author of “Wrapped in Rainbows,” Hurston was published in 2003.
A change occurred in the 80’s.
Orange County officers wanted to place a five-lane highway across the city to replace Kennedy Boulevard, the two-lane main street.
Orlando's expansion has already pushed La Interstate 4 across the city's western limit.
The proposal came when Eatonville was still recovering from a difficult period in its history.
Forced integration, in addition to other things, had brought an end to the relative idyllic isolation of the community
In the 1950s, the battle over racial mixing brought hate to the community's front door.
"During that period, a group of white boys came and threw oranges and things at the people sitting on one side," says Mr. Franklin.
“In fact, we had a girl who was killed by it once.
They threw a watermelon from a car."
In a 1955 letter to The Orlando Sentinel, Hurston made the Supreme Court a request for forced integration, calling his decision in Brown against the school office "an insult rather than an honor to my race."
Residents now say that the desegregation of schools, despite having been positive in some respects, dilutes the cohesion of Eatonville and jeopardizes the confidence of its young people.
"Black children were used to being hugged - I remember this - you were hugging your teacher in the morning, hugging your teacher in the evening," said N. Y. Nathiri, Ella Dinkins' daughter and the executive director of Preserve the Eatonville Community.
This lasted, he added, until the teachers and students came from the same place.
"You didn't embrace your white teacher because your white teacher...- I mean, there's a cultural division there," Ms. Nathiri said.
Civil rights, however, helped and created space for many other Zora Neale Hurstons - black writer, actress and artist who overcame prejudice, as she did, with cheerful self-confidence and lines such as: "How can some deny themselves the pleasure of my company!
“I just can’t understand.”
In 1975, writer Alice Walker began a long way to Hurston's unplaque tomb and began to fight for the resurrection of her reputation.
Five years later, an acclaimed biography of Robert E. Hemenway's Hurston hit bookshelves, reintroducing it into the American canon.
The projection of the highway came just as his most famous daughter had beamed the limelight again.
And this time, Hurston's old neighbors saw her as a savior.
The community began to design in 1988 a festival dedicated to Hurston to show what could ruin the status with its highway.
Thousands of fans came to the inaugural event two years later, and every January, many return for commemoration.
Many years later, the state backed down from its project on the street.
"The five lanes of the resurrected highway, brought what you would say high speed, a real pride for the city," Ms. Nathiri said.
Ms. Boyd puts it more simply: "Zora saved Eatonville."
The victory over the motorway project helped change the image that the city has of itself.
Out-of-town people like Rachelle Munson, a lawyer who began to come to church here in 1993, began to appear in large numbers, and residents began to reassess the past.
Eatonville joined the historical public register in 1998.
A new one-floor bookcase (named after Hurston, of course) opened in 2006 on a repaved and embellished Kennedy Boulevard.
Today, Eatonville remains an anomaly of Florida: only six miles from downtown Orlando, it can give the feeling of a secondary road in the summer rain, as small as when it was founded with just 27 black families in the 1980s.
(It's 90 percent black today.)
Outsiders who come to look for Eatonville's history, for its significance, are often still treated with caution.
A permit asked in advance is required for most of the interviews, to a few things - such as the murals on Eatonville's oldest church, painted by a white man, which make black men see in the fields - have no permission to be photographed.
Many in Eatonville, like Ms. Jones, a plump teacher, with a penchant for bright red, still feed that their island community would be misunderstood.
And yet, as soon as the Hurston festival expanded, a high level of city pride also emerged.
Young people, in particular, tend to see Eatonville with Hurston saw his entire race: beautiful, problems and everything, nor better, nor worse and fierce, creative, great worker, silly and confused as other races and ethnic groups in America.
It's heartfelt civic affection what can be heard in the voice of Mr Franklin's grandson Edwin Harvey, 18, who plans to return to Eatonville after college to work in the local government or for the Police Department, which he said may need help.
And even those who are younger, like Alondra and Alexia Kenon, 11-year-old twins from Winter Park, seem to have learned to describe Eatonville correctly.
"Many people, if they've just entered here, they'll think, 'Oh, this city is nothing compared to everyone else,'" said Alondra Kenon after Mass on a recent Sunday.
"But if you're really stopping and taking a moment to look at history, it's really a pretty city."
Here in New York we all speak fluently about finance
On the surface, nothing has changed.
If tourists in Manhattan stayed away from newspaper stalls and Huge Times Square television screens, they wouldn't realize they're in cyclone's eye.
They would never guess that the glass, honeycomb offices above their heads the bankers and hedge fund managers monopolizing New York live every day the worst "bad day in the office" since the collapse of 1929.
Farns shake metal trolleys for tea through sidewalks, carrying trays of muffins for meetings for breakfast.
Latino-Americans drag their feet between condominiums carrying, such as human mules, 50 and broken ironed shirts hanging from a stick on their shoulders.
Employees are still waiting in line for their morning bagel to food carts on sidewalks, and it is still impossible to take a yellow taxi between 16 and 19 a.m
It didn't seem that there was a real crisis, until, with all the talk of ruiness and sadness, I decided to open a new savings booklet on Thursday afternoon.
Although I had planned the matter so that it coincided with the late-afternoon break at my J.P Morgan Chase branch, a queue of about twenty was already waiting in line.
Not in front of the cashier's desk, to withdraw money or make deposits, but in front of the customer service counter, where old customers order checkbooks, tourists ask for directions for the Macy department store and new customers open accounts.
Many of those in front of me, I would later learn from Starr, my "confidence banker," were in line for the same reason.
Unlike me, however, they have already taken savings from smaller banks from the places from which commuters, such as those from New Jersey, Pennsylvania and Staten Island.
They withdrew money from so-called thrifts (a bit like a construction company), credit cooperatives and types of city savings banks whose branches operate only within a state, and say they treat customers as family members.
In past years, 15 of those banks have gone bankrupt.
In recent days the Washington Mutual, a bank with 327 billion dollars in assets, has been subjugated to controlled administration, and, while New Yorkers headed home for the weekend one Friday afternoon, another bank, Wachovia, with 440-billion-dollar savings deposits, was looking for a buyer to save it.
Employees and clerks in front of me, frightened for another collapse, were shifting their money to a bank perceived as a safe port, the bank that is demanding more bail on Wall Street.
Ordinary New Yorkers in recent weeks have become competent in financy.
A woman at my trusted Chinese supermarket, speaking on the phone, referred to Lehman Brothers, the failing bank, and her emaciated general manager Dick Fuld familiarly, as if complaining about a lazy colleague.
And Lyn, the concierge in uniform, almost fifty-year-old from my condominium, has switched off to the subject of our nor-running topic about Brad and Angelina or the delivery of my shopping.
She told me, after my visit to the bank, "Now I have all my savings in market funds!.
“I’ve changed, I’m taking no risk.”
Desecrated Muslim graves as Austria turns to the right.
Heinz-Christian Strache, to the left of the Austrian right wing of the Freedom Party and Filip Dewinter of the right-wing Belgian Vlaams Belang party, takes part in a demonstration to present the organization "City against Islamization" in Antwerp, Thursday, January 17, 2008.
They carry signs such as the road ones representing mosques with a red line that passes in the middle.
Police blamed right-wing extremists for desecrating a Muslim cemetery in Austria, the same week that far-right political parties gained a lot in the national general election.
More than 90 graves were severely damaged at Traun Cemetery, near Linz, a few hours between Friday night and this morning, in what the police believe was an organized action.
The culprits sprinkled Jewish symbols such as the Star of David over a few tombs, but detectives believe it may have been an attempt to masquerade extremist motives driven by a hatred of Muslim immigrants.
It's unclear whether the incidenct is tied in any way to the election.
A spokesman for the local Islamic community said they were "deeply shocked" by the news of the desecration, which took place towards the end of Ramadan
Austria has meanwhile embarked on a journey of spiritual research following its turn to the right in yesterday's parliamentary elections.
The two far-right parties that took nearly 30 percent of the vote, Austria's Freedom Party and Austria's Alliance for the Future, campaigned vehemently anti-immigration and some of their slogans were defined by xenophobic critics.
Heinz-Christian Strache, the head of the Freedom Party who won more than 18 percent of the vote, campaigned against veils and burkas and also expressed his opposition to foods that are perceived as connected to Islam.
At the final rally in Vienna, he spoke of a "European brotherhood" to prevent the growth of Islam.
Both parties seem to banish the construction of mosques and minarets, alleging that they are political symbols of a continuous Islamization of Austria and Europe.
Polls and analyses made immediately after the elections, which established the far-right as the nation's strongest political bloc, indicating that change was brought predominantly by young voters who are worried about their future in the European Union.
Abroad travel banned to Gary Glitter.
Gary Glitter, the glamorous rock star guilty of child sexual abuse, has been banned from travelling to France and Spain by court.
Magistrates imposed a six-month ban on Glitter after he had informed the authorities that they controlled that they wanted to go to Spain through France.
The prohibition of foreign travel was granted to Kent police last Thursday after having provided magistrates with evidence of Glitter's previous arrests for child sexual abuse.
Glitter, real name Paul Gadi, wasn't present at Ashford's preture hearing in Kent but was represented by Corker Binning's lawyers.
A spokesman for the Court Service said: "Thursday, September 25 at Ashford's caution, the Kent police filed a request to prevent Mr Paul Gadd's overseas trip to France and Spain until March 25.
"Paul Gadd wasn't present in person."
Glitter, 64, had informed the authorities that he wanted to travel abroad as requested by the register of convicts for sexual offences.
He has to give notice to the authorities if he wishes to travel abroad for more than three days.
He is also required to give the police seven-day notice on his scheduled departure date.
Failure to notify the authority may lead to a five-year prison sentence.
Kent police have said they do not want to discuss individual cases or Glitter's possible commutes to the country.
A Kent police spokesman said: "Kent police will do whatever it takes to protect children in this country or abroad.
"From our point of view, it's easier to control someone who stays in the UK because we have suitable comprehensive systems to manage every potential risk more effectively.
"Protecting Kent residents is Kent's main police concern and we work carefully to monitor the behaviour of those convicted of registered sexual offences in the country to minimize every possible risk to the public.
Glitter returned to the UK last month after spending 27 months in a Vietnamese prison for abusing two 10- and 11-year-old girls.
When Glitter flew to Bangkok where he was supposed to board a flight to London.
But he said he felt sick and refused to fly to London.
The Thai authorities would not let him switch to passport control and eventually took a flight to Hong Kong.
Even the Chinese authorities didn't let him in and went back to Bangkok where Thai police and immigration officers insisted on returning to England.
He eventually arrived at Heathrow Terminal 3 on August 22 and stayed there while his lawyer David Corker went to Uxbridge County, west London, since Scotland Yard had successfully applied for an order to place him in the register of convicts for sexual offences.
Glitter had previously been jailed in the UK in 1999 for possessing images of child abuse.
An Interior Ministry source said Jacqui Smith, the Home Secretary felt reassured and happy that Glitter had been halted from travelling to France and Spain.
"Photographer the master away from the scene to extract his character" Günther Bauer, photographer
Neither heels nor ruffles nor dresses from before.
Only the essence of the great masters of flamenco, stripped of the concentration that characterizes the interpretation, to look at the relaxed appearance of man or woman.
On on the one hand the artist.
On the other, the person.
Two inseparable faces, except in the private, in those moments away from the public, when the intimacy of a gesture, the depth of a stolen look or the expressiveness of the hands, are natural because they do not have to go on stage.
Germany's Günther Bauer in "Flamenco-People" alternates the theatricity of some shots with singers, dancers and guitarists on stage with the less-known side of these masters.
With this lens, the photographer offers an original flamenco iconography, seizing it from different visual angles.
The exhibition, included in the programming of Flamenco Photography Month, reflects the international importance of this dance, every day," explains the artist and clarifies that in "Flamenco-People" it could also be ordinary people, but seeing the photos, the observer realizes that they are not the typical images of artists, performers or performers on the scene.
These people portrayed "on the street" are "artists able to convey passion, sadness, joy when they are staged ... and to capture the audience.
To capture the personality of these adults you don’t need to portray them on stage,
because their personality and character are internal.
What interested me was to create an intimate atmosphere that would allow me to capture with the camera the personality of flamenco greats.”
With a passion for the "Cante de las Minas" festival, Bauer began in 1997 a long path aimed at portraying singers, guitarists or dancers using a slab camera.
They are portraits that, due to the type of machine and material, are reminiscent of classical photography.
The application of different stylistic techniques breaks with the traditional principles of the image, conferring to the photo modernity, according to an idea that reflects the conception of flamenco proper to Bauer: "A music with very traditional roots that evolves and is able to surprise every day."
Not dancing, singing or playing in itself, but the personality of the artist, his character, capable of conquering the audience with his art.
The search for an encounter with the essential elements, with the magic that becomes visible in a certain way of feeling and being, are and have been the constant of this work, on display these days at the El Fotómata gallery.
Famous flamenco artists such as Diego El Cigala, Chano Lobato, Cristina Hoyos, Enrique Morente, El Lebrijano, Antonio Canales, Vicente Amigo, among others, include in this selection of about seventy artists portrayed.
The project has become a kind of anthology of flamenco, and will soon be published in a book that will collect the portraits exhibited at the exhibition.
The passion for portraits led Bauer to repeat the idea two years ago with "Portraits of Veteran Athletes" ("SportARTproject").
Although in "Flamenco-People" the concept is different, the photographer admits "I love to portray people, get to know them and be able to penetrate for a moment in their lives, whether this is a long or short moment.
I think the portrait is the hardest genre in photography.
You meet or compare with people, characters that sometimes you don’t even know or give you just a few minutes to take the picture.
Beyond these circumstances, like a photographer, I always try to concretize my ideas."
The collection of portraits of contemporary flamenco, entitled "Flamenco-people" is set up at the El Fotómata gallery, at number 20 via de la Mata, parallel to Via Alameda de Hercules, and will be open to the public until October 16.
Vargas Llosa unveils that the novel he's writing is a "hazard"
It is set in places he does not yet know and deals with unknown topics: despite this, Mario Vargas Llosa is grappling with a new project he's working on, a novel whose protagonist, Roger Casemant, is an Irish nationalist who lived twenty years in Congo and went through the Amazon.
Always favored for the Nobel Prize for Literature, the writer confessed yesterday on the occasion of the Hay Festival in Segovia, that the project he is dealing with today "is a gamble" and that "everything indicates that I shouldn't write it."
"If I had common sense, I wouldn't write it, but I'll do it anyway and I hope to finish it as soon as possible," so he concluded his intervention at the festival for the exchange of ideas, thoughts and words that for four days saw a hundred writers, historians, journalists and thousands of people pass through the Aqueduct city.
The author of "La ciudad y los perros" was, as expected, the leading figure at the third edition of the speech festival.
Vargas Llosa, who won the Hay Festival award for being the best-rated writer by attendees – an ancient edition of Dickens’ “Our Mutual Friend,” filled seats at the Juan Bravo theater.
Presented by Félix Romeo, the writer found himself in front of an audience to whom time flew when the Peruvian, with a prodigious memory and with great doses of humour, evoked how the literary vocation was born in him, derived from the "extraordinary adventure" of the "pleasure of reading" and "of living beyond what I lived"; as he writes in "disciplined way" to make up for the "lack
The Spanish-American novelist, acknowledged that "there is no way" to measure and know if books can change people's lives, "but I refuse to believe that literature is pure entertainment, an ephemeral experience," Vargas Llosa stressed, arguing that, on the contrary, "it has effects" and that "makes people more nonconformist." For this he called on writers to "do not give in to frivo
Time hinders Pozoblanco Festival, depriving it of bullfights
The bullfighting celebration that was supposed to seal the days of the Pozoblanco Festival was suspended yesterday morning, after the bullfighters selected for the bullfights had verified that the ground conditions of the square were not adequate.
Therefore, the company, in agreement with Fermin Bohorquez, Pablo Hermoso de Mendoza and Leonardo Hernandez, decided to suspend the celebration of bullfighting.
The bad weather raged against this Festival preventing them from celebrating two days out of three.
On Saturday, the rain prevented the second bullfighting.
Jose Luis Moreno, Curro Diaz and Juan Buatista were expected to face a Castilblanco bull.
Therefore, the Festival only had regular unfolding on Fridays.
On this occasion it was Daniel Luque who triumphed, cutting three ears and being brought out in triumph, after bulling with taste and aesthetics.
The same afternoon Alejandro Talavante completed his two bullfights collecting greetings and an ear, granted more by the public than thanks to the bullfighting itself.
The worst of all was El Juli, passing from the Los Llanos arena without glory and without penalty.
With the second bull, after a tremendous mulet, he earned the whistles of much of the audience.
A van with two thousand pounds of hashish crosses the checkpoint in Alava
Five minus ten in the morning.
Officers intimate the alt to the two occupants of a van who instead of stopping they flee.
Officers intimated the alt to the two occupants of a van who instead of stopping fled.
Their cargo, more than two tons of hashish, is a good reason not to stop.
Officers after chasing them manage to arrest one of two occupants, a 28-year-old man, identified as A. A. J., according to Basque's Interior Department.
The arrested individual is charged with drug trafficking and falsification of public documents.
The accident occurred when some officials of Alava's road were carrying out a transport check on the N-1, at the point where it crosses Armiñón municipal constituency.
They were about to stop a van for inspection when the driver, transgressing orders, accelerated abruptly and walked past the checkpoint.
Officers turned themselves in pursuit until the vehicle, after a couple of kilometers, stopped on the edge of the road and its two occupants fled to a wooded area.
Ertzaintza agents then managed to arrest one of the fugitives and seize the 2,178 kilograms of hashish they were carrying and took note once the van, an Iveco Daily, was inspected.
Hashish bread rolls, which were located at the rear of the vehicle, were later placed in police custody for a thorough investigation and weight detection.
In addition, since the officials ascertained that the freshman had been "duplicated" since it did not coincide with that reported in the documentation of the van, the arrested was also imputed the offence of falsification of public documents.
Ertzaintza is continuing its inquiries to arrest the second suspected person, who managed to flee as already mentioned, and to determine the provenance of the seized drugs.
Inmate A. A. J., 28 years old, was taken to the police station to carry out the appropriate procedures and be available to the judge in the next few hours.
Actress Heather Locklear arrested for driving under the influence of drugs
Actress Heather Locklear, Amanda of the popular Melrose Place series, was arrested this weekend in Santa Barbara (California) for guidance under the influence of drugs.
A witness saw her perform risky manoeuvres while trying to get out with the car from the parking lot in Montecito, according to a spokesman for the Californian road magazine.
The witness specified that around 16.30 the Locklear "crushed the accelerator thoroughly, making a lot of noise, and trying to take out the car from the parking lot with abrupt manoeuvres back and forth. Doing reverse, passed several times in front of his sunglasses."
Shortly thereafter, the witness, who at first glance, had apparently not recognized the actress, saw that Locklear had stopped in a nearby street and had left the vehicle.
It was this person who alerted the emergency services, because "she was concerned about Locklear's life."
When the police patrol arrived, she found the actress sitting inside her vehicle, partially blocking the road.
"She seemed confused," and for this reason the agents took her to a specialist center for the use of drugs and alcohol to test her.
According to a police spokesman, the plaintiff proved to be cooperative and the hypothesis of excess alcohol was discarded from the beginning, although "according to the officers at first, we believe Locklear was under the influence of some drug."
Locklear was arrested on suspicion of driving under the influence of some unspecified substance and was locked up in the local prison around 7pm, to be released a few hours later.
Two months ago, Locklear had been re-established from a specialist clinic in Arizona after being hospitalized following an episode of anxiety and depression.
Llamazares urges the Andalusian federation of the IU to assume its responsibilities in the Silva case
UI coordinator-general and Izquierda Unida party member Gaspar Llamazares confirmed today that it will be up to the regional training federation to make a decision on the case of the former Councillor for Youth and Sport of the City of Sevilla, Francisco Manuel Silva, who resigned last week following the accusation of cronyism.
"In these cases I am particularly federalist, and it will be the Andalusian organization that will have to assume its responsibilities," Llamazares said with regard to the resolution that the party will have to adopt against what has been the councillor in the Sevilian council to date.
However, the UI leader stressed the need for "transparency" in all possible investigations with regard to the irregularities Silva would have committed. "The IU has always wanted that this type of investigation we have maximum transparency."
Similarly he stressed that training is regulated by "very strict ethical codes for all public offices."
"Sangre de mayo", Garci's epic bet
Inspiring Benito Pérez Galdós's Episodios Nacionales, Jose Luis Garci re-proposes in Sangre de mayo the famous revolt of the Spaniards against the French, in what is the most ambitious film of his filmography, with almost 15 million euros in budget.
With this film, the director attempted to "illustrate the torrent of characters and at the same time the social analysis" that he has always admired in the work of the famous novelist, he explained today during the presentation of the film, with Quim Gutierrez and Paula Echevarría at the head of an extensive cast of performers.
Sangre de mayo, more than two and a half hours long, debuts this Friday in cinema theaters, to become in the future a television miniseries of more than four hours on TeleMadrid.
Great admirer of Galdós literature, Jose Luis Garci claims to have realized with this shot "a dream of his childhood", and believes he must thank Esperanza Aguirre, who from the Community of Madrid had commissioned him three years before shooting a commemorative film for the second centenary of May 2. The director had accepted as long as the story was based on the novel Episodios Nacionales.
The 15 million budget allocated in full by the Community of Madrid represented for Garci a "unique opportunity" to recreate the proper scenery and customs to portray a "stunned Spain that missed the choice of the leader to fight for and the fact that the struggle for independence led to the people a huge delay in development under the reign of Ferdinand VII" noted Garci.
However, the director regrets that he had not received funding equal to those of Alatriste or Los phanthamthas de Goya - more than twice as much as Sangre de mayo, so it was not possible to shoot some scenes predicted by the scenography he himself had signed together with Horacio Valcarcel, a contributor to almost his entire career.
This, for Volver's director in empezar, has influenced what is his practice of habitual work, staging with the actors, with whom he often uses to rehearse before shooting together with those who won't be part of the cast, because he considers it "humiliating," considering that "part of the director's work resides in going to theatre and cinema to know what's happening in the industry he's working in."
Many of the performers, part of whom fall into the cast of Sangre de mayo, rehearse in front of his car, such as Michael Rell, Carlos Larranaga or Fernando Guillen Cuervo.
Oscar nominee four times - winning one - Garci believes that none of the three films nominated to represent Spain this year at the Hollywood Academy - his Sangre de mayo, Siete mesas de billar francés and, the last-selected Los girasoles ciegos -, do not represent the younger generation of Spanish cinema "which should instead be known."
From this film Garci does not expect prizes.
"At first you think you're working for posterity, then you realize you're only working for prosperity, and at some point you conform to simply shoot an extra film," Garci explains after more than 30 years of career.
Isabel Bayon rediscovers the Sevillian essence of dancer Tortola Valencia
A Sevillian dancer pays tribute to another dancer equally from Sevilla.
Carmen Valencia Tortola, Catalan father (Florence Tortola Ferrer) and Andalusian mother (Georgina Valencia Valenzuela), was born in Triana, and at the age of three moved with her family to London.
She was a free dancer and a specialist in oriental dances.
She led a fascinating life and was a pioneer of women's emancipation, of the caliber of Isadora Duncan, Virginia Woolf or Sarah Bernhardt.
She was a Buddhist and vegetarian, and defended the cause for the abolition of the corset that prevented women from moving freely.
The life of this woman, who marked a revolution in dance and aesthetics in Europe between the two wars, has always fascinated Isabel Bayon.
"I'd have loved working with Pepa Gamboa and she proposed this character to me. It seemed incredible to me."
Supports the dancer: "we don't pretend to tell about her whole life, but gather her spirit.
The titles of his dances have suggested to me some basic elements of flamenco.
So, "the dance de la mariposa" suggested to me a guajira, or "the dance of the snatch" some tangos.
His dance was sensual and I was told that even my way of dancing is part of this aesthetic," she explains.
The staging includes two exceptional contributions: that of Miguel Poveda, who will sing Isabel again in one of her shows, as she does in "La puerta abierta"; and that of teacher Matilde Coral, who exceptionally wanted to work with what had been her student.
"Matilde plays Tortola as an adult, the character of maturity.
“I’m happy because she’s agreed to work in my work, and I feel very honoured for that.”
The staging also includes the musical and guitarist composition such as Jesus Torres and Paco Arriaga, the singing of Miguel Ortega, Morón's Pulga y Moi; El Pájaro's percussion, as well as Rafa La Viola's viola and the collaboration of an actor, Juanfran Juárez "who plays the role of eternal chaperone of these artists who have always have always been well-rounded."
In the work there is also in some form the life of this woman that takes place in distant places, especially for that era, such as India, the United States or Russia, and then all of Europe, since, habitually, she resided in London.
"I wanted to remember her, because I think she's one of the women who had a huge influence in a given historical period and now no one remembers it and much less regards her work as something of value," Bayon said.
The show will begin today, at 8.30 p.m
With the publication of the "Diario de un poete recién casado" also closes the Triennio
The presentation of the first illustrated edition of the "Diary of a Poet Reciencasado" at the church of Saint Stephen (New York), where Juan Ramón Jiménez married Zenobia in March 1916, is part of the activities scheduled by the Department of Culture of the Council of Andalusia to celebrate the "Three Years of Zenobia and Juan Ramon" (2006-2008), organized to commemorate the 50th anniversary
Thus, the "Three-year of Zenobia and Juan Ramon" concludes with the re-edition of this work, with the International Congress that took place in New York, with the restoration of the house-museum of the Nobel and with the traveling exhibition "Juan Ramón Jiménez: Aquel chopo de luz", which was inaugurated last May and which, after touching the various provinces of Andalusia
In addition, the Department of Culture of the Council of Andalusia has acquired a total of eight hundred copies of the "Diario de un Poeta Reciencasado", edited by Pietro Tabernero and illustrated by James Perez-Enciso, to continue the dissemination of the figure and work of one of the highest-value Andalusian poets.
America likes it a little less without Paul Newman
“Sometimes God creates perfect people.
Paul Newman was one of them."
The phrase is from actress Sally Field, who worked with him in "Ausencia de malicia" and gives the idea of the impotence of American cinema, and all of America, in the face of the shocking idea that Newman left.
And yet it cost him a lot to give him an Oscar.
And since 2007 he was retiring.
It was also known that he was very ill.
Despite this, it was a very hard blow for everyone.
"Sometimes what one feels goes beyond any expression, and I've now lost a great friend," cried Robert Redford, "my life and my country could have been better because of its existence."
No one was afraid of being ridiculous or exaggerating.
A father of cinema and homeland died at a particularly dark time, for fear of terrorists and markets, with self-esteem from the American people on the ground, desperate for new motivations to feel worthy.
When times are so hard, men so without virtues and remain good people to admire, you don’t have to be Luis Cernuda to love Paul Newman.
For all he embodied the Platonic ideal of beauty and truth.
He was an official opponent of Richard Nixon and defender of lost and won causes, from the foundation for the fight against the drug created in honor of the eldest-die-dressed overdose to the creation of the brand of home and organic food.
All gifts to a country that has costed a lot to go beyond a suicidal food culture.
The wave of admiration went beyond Hollywood, without caring about either nationality or passports.
The latest James Bond, Englishman Daniel Craig, noted that a man didn't die, it's over an era.
Another Englishman, Sam Mendes, an Oscar-winning director, said working with Newman constituted nothing less than the pinnacle of his career.
The bad boy from Australia, Russell Crowe, ventured to call Paul Newman his greatest inspiration.
"He's left an example of the highest level for all of us," notes George Clooney, perhaps one of the few who today tries to live like Paul Newman did: keeping in high regard the need for righteousness and having a serious and distant private life to the cameras.
This isn't always easy or possible in today's media jungle.
However, some try and Paul Newman is a good example.
He divorced once but was married for fifty years to Joanne Woodward, to whom he remained faithful while certainly not lacking the opportunities not to be.
"Because I'm going to eat burgers today, if I've got a steak in the house?" he used to joke.
In the sky it accompanies him the pride that on earth his daughters are also his admirers, which is not so common in America.
"Out of all the great roles he played, of no one was as proud as that of a devoted husband, loving father, solicite grandfather and passionate philanthropist," they say.
The Imperfect Race of an Olympic Madrid
Yes, the dream of many of us is that sooner or later the Olympics will be held in Madrid and, after the failed attempt for 2012, this could happen in 2016.
But I wonder, are we ready?
If we follow the example of yesterday's race, I'd say no!
They called it the "perfect race," but in my opinion it was by far the worst I've witnessed.
A lot of nonsense, that if they were later taken into account at the time of the assessment and I had to examine them, the answer would be absolutely negative.
But let's go in order and start from the beginning.
Nonsense began with the withdrawal of the ridge, which presupposed it to go first.
It didn't seem like a misconception, but if they force you to go, whether it's to collect everything (including the chip to avoid queues during the race).
But no. No.
You had to go just to take the ridge (first nonsense, go just for it) and (after the relative tail), surprise! they had only T-shirts size 'S' (second nonsense, because I need an XL size).
What world do we live in?
Is it because of the crisis if they decided to buy only small T-shirts, made with less fabric and therefore less expensive?
Well, things of this kind are trifles compared to the day of the race.
In addition to having to go very soon to take the chip, the waiting queue was 15 minutes and, what's more, you had to deposit the backpack.
The wait lasted more than 35 minutes, something incomprehensible for a ride to which more than 11,000 people are expected.
So, as was foreseeable, the race began that we had not even deposited the backpack, although, once successful in the feat, the recovery was even worse.
In addition, the rain threatened the wait for more than 40 minutes, to which were added attempts of popular rebellion and assault on the stand.
In at least 500 people tomorrow we will definitely have pneumonia, and some, like me, won't even result in the ranking in correct order because of the chip which, I suppose didn't record the arrival properly.
In the future, if they ask me about the perfect race, it certainly won't fit into my thoughts.
So I think we're going to keep dreaming of a well-prepared Olympic city.
Microsoft seeks receptionist (virtual)
At the MIT conference on emerging technologies, Microsoft previewed a new system of image recognition and human interaction, a kind of artificial intelligence, but without much intelligence, able to perform some basic tasks, such as calling a taxi (or, in the case illustrated by Microsoft, a shuttle service to move within the campus of the company) by assisting the telephone user.
Thanks to a camera and a system of recognizing shapes and faces, this "receptionist" is able to know how many people are there in a room, who they are - including remembering their location in the company - and to listen and understand keywords in a conversation with them.
It can also group those present into conversation groups.
In the sample video the receptionist detects a thier person, but understands that he does not belong to the group with whom he is talking and that he is not an employee of the company.
He asks her if she's there to visit anyone and tells her she'll be at his disposal shortly.
All with no blue screen.
Impressive.
But this, of course, is the future.
Now the company has better to focus on, for example on the next version of its Windows operating system.
There has been a lot of talk this week about a change of strategy at Microsoft.
Windows 7 will be lighter and won’t have as many associated programs.
Steve Ballmer found that, if computers were equipped with the essential functions and less loaded with commercial garbage, they’ll go five times faster—hey! Steve, it’ll be for “the less.”
All a discovery, if you take into account that Microsoft, version after version has continuously added to Windows features and "extra".
The change of direction is appreciable.
Microsoft is right: the operating system is increasingly losing importance, and with it the software.
When Google launched Chrome into the network, I was one of those who supported the theory that it was Google’s first step to competing with Windows.
Many rightly point out that a browser is not an operating system.
The operating system will always be necessary because it is an essential element for hardware and software to communicate.
She performs the same function as your wife or husband when you have something to say her mother-in-law.
That's true.
It will never disappear, but perhaps it is necessary to think of the "death" of the operating system not as an absolute truth, but relative.
What Ballmer said this week confirms this idea.
People are paying less and less attention to the operating system, and more and more to the web browser.
You take your computer just to launch Explorer or Firefox – or Chrome – and every day we spend more time surfinging on the network than in a remote window environment.
What is the use of half of operating system applications?
Less is more.
And Windows 7 needs to be less, much less than Vista.
Less heavy, less overloaded, less complex.
Microsoft is more interested in integrating its online services so that they constitute a viable alternative to Google.
They desperately need it, in fact, and I bring back here a small example.
I took the photograph placed in this post in one of Microsoft's employee recreation venues in Redmond earlier this month.
On the soft drink machine a sign urges them to use their own search tool - "Your research. Our future".
In 2006, an independent study came to the conclusion that 66 percent of Microsoft employees use Google to perform a network search.
Last week, moreover, the delay in the development of the new version of Windows Mobile 7 was confirmed.
It won't be put on the market in early 2009, but probably at the end of the year, if not in 2010.
Microsoft will certainly try to jointly launch Windows 7 and Windows Mobile 7.
The mobile phone, after all, is increasingly becoming a computer.
But the delay, with Android and the iPhone already on the market, is penalizing.
Fabuleux Marcel
Pablo Picasso took her to the Côte d'Azur, Leonardo di Caprio shone in Edward Zwick's 'Diamante de sangre', as well as Al Pacino in Francis Ford Coppola's 'The Godfather' as well as being part of the typical uniform of the muscular gay parish.
And if we try to enumerate the appearances of the stars, we can recall the image of the forcy with the pointed moustaches lifting the weights in the walking circus.
It is, in essence, the typical men's t-shirt, with the cotton interior, tight and sleeveless.
It's the 'tank', as the Anglophones call it, although, the Belgian Fabuleux Marcel stands out above all.
The "Marcel" excels compared to the others for the high quality of cotton, its unisex cut, its distinctive coat of arms and the small Belgian flag (just recognisable), which the most emblematic models of the t-shirt carry as detail on the neck.
The "Marcel" excels compared to the others for the high quality of cotton, for its unisex cutter, for its distinctive coat of arms and for the small Belgian flag (just recognizable), which the most emblematic t-shirt models carry as a detail on the neck.
