Stars under the starred sky of Paris.
Oh there, there's the way.
1001, the anti-stress number
There aren't 1,000 but 1,000 and a way to forget chaotic and hectic everyday life.
And as if that wasn't enough, they're all collected in a book that will bring relaxation to bookstores on September 30.
The wake-up call is almost a chastisement when it rings every morning at 7 a.m., but it's even worse to imagine yourself involved in the hectic pace dictated by modern life.
The office is called tension and computer back pain.
Stress is the evil of the 21st century.
However there are not one and not even two methods to combat daily exhaustion and the struggle to unhook from the world of work.
No, this number amounts to nothing more and nothing less than 1001.
Beyond the yoga poses, the classic massage or "spa" treatments, Susannah Marriott collects in the pages of her book, all a "menu" of options tailored to the taste and time of each.
The anti-stress work is titled "1001 Ways to Relax" and its calm will reach the bookstores thanks to publisher Pearson Alhambra and DK.
If someone believes that it is not possible to conquer calm in spheres such as work or in personal relationships, this "manual" disproves this belief.
In addition, it suggests alternative solutions, such as being in touch with nature, or 100% natural recipes that contribute to relaxation.
If adrenaline doesn't already flow into your veins to regain body balance and stress has become a problem, it's time to sit on the couch, turn off your mind and focus on reading.
Speaking in Sleep
Some children speak at night.
I'm not referring to those who wake up very agitated to tell us something that happened to them the previous day, but to those children who speak while staying asleep.
The first time it happens, at most you get a scare, but it doesn't really matter much.
It is a benign alteration of sleep called somniloquy.
It manifests with a certain frequency, especially in children and adolescents.
Surely, to most of you they told that you had spoken in your sleep.
This phenomenon can occur in a very sporadic way (once in a lifetime, e.g. associated with an episode of high fever) or more regularly (every day, once a week, once in the month ...), the day after the child no longer remembers anything.
There is no single reason to explain this phenomenon.
Sometimes they are episodes related to other sleep disturbances such as nightmares, nocturnal fears, nocturnal enuresis (peeing in bed), or sleepwalking, but it is not necessarily so.
As already mentioned above, it can be associated with very high fever stages, strength or overexcitation.
In any case, it is a phenomenon that is still poorly studied.
Even the intensity with which it appears is extremely variable.
It may happen that the person speaks with a very quiet tone of voice, with loose words, almost in a whisper or that the opposite happens (screams, shouts, long speeches ...).
In cases where the event is particularly intense, it would be appropriate to observe the child and possibly consult a specialist to check if there is an issue that is generating an abnormal state of anxiety such as an outburst to the situation.
We have said that this is a benign alteration so there is no treatment unless it is associated with other sleep disorders that can be treated (for example, when coupled with insomnia or stress situation).
What can be done, however, is to consider a series of very simple preventive measures such as:
Try not to excite the baby excessively before he goes to sleep.
Propose quiet games and avoid programs or television series that arouse fear or suggest violence.
With very nervous children for whom it is tiring to relax, relaxation techniques can be used to help them acquire some inner calm.
This will allow the child to sleep more quietly.
Once the sonniloquies appear, try to keep calm.
Remember that they are harmless to the child and that the next day he won’t remember any of what happened.
Useful Advice
Don't wake him up.
It's useless, because he's not aware of what's happening, and you'd interrupt his sleep.
Although he's talking, he's asleep and so he's resting.
Remember that if it appears very distressed and the episodes have a certain frequency it is better to consult a doctor or check from where such great anxiety in the child results.
If you understand what he says (which is not the most common situation), don't fall into the temptation to make him an interrogation to get information.
You won’t get anything, because he’s asleep and therefore not conscious to organize his thinking.
In addition, you are able to hear what he says but not to see what he is dreaming to say, so you have to pay close attention to the interpretation of the information and conclusions to which they might lead.
Never tell the child that you could ask him questions during the night to know his secrets.
Not even as a joke.
Such a thing provokes enormous anguish.
In short, don’t worry, it’s nothing serious, it’s an episode that lasts only a few minutes.
Illhaeusern... finally
The Illhaeusern has finally regained the path of victory at home after a long unsuccessful period.
Without playing a formidable game, the Yellow-Blackers have created the greatest number of chances from the neture by turning one into the victory of the match.
The pace was not very sustained during this meeting in which the strikers hardly expressed themselves.
After a first attempt by host team captain Kauffmann who grazed the seven with one shot from afar, Illhaeusern began to get the better of it and one after the other resulted in Dannemarie's defence, Fonderflick from afar, then Hirn high, flagship Romain and Diebold again.
Dannemarie responded with a center-field block, leaving only the unleashed Riahi at the tip of attack.
The start of the second half was decisive with two initial Riahi and Temporale chances for Dannemarie, and immediately after Marais' first goal for Illhaeusern, well-placed, resumed a ball rejected on a first attempt by Diebold (53').
But the Illhaeusern gunner won't be able to savor his happiness for long and will be ejected a dozen minutes later leaving his teammates outnumbered.
But Dannemarie will not be able to take advantage of it and will not pose great problems to the Eleven of the Illhaeusern, backed just to "strengthen" the defense, except in the last few minutes of play when, after a bombastic action, the ball touched the crossbar.
Sarkozy meets angry fishermen
Before leaving for the United States, Nicolas Sarkozy will make stops in Brittany.
In front of the demonstration of the fishermen who on Monday brutally impeded access to several Breton and Norman ports, the President of the Republic decided to go on Tuesday morning to the port of Guilvinec, in the department of Finistère, to enchant their representatives.
The latter have promised to welcome it "with a constructive spirit" and hope that "it presents itself with concrete proposals".
A presidential visit that takes place at the same time as the general assembly of the "crisis committee", created by Breton fishermen in order to take measures to cope with the surge in the price of diesel, which has reached its record with an average of more than 1.14 euros per liter.
According to Elysée spokesman David Martinon, the president "will discuss with them about the possible solutions to help companies to regain competitiveness and to make working conditions and sailor pay safe."
The fishermen are waiting for Nicolas Sarkozy at stop.
On Tuesday morning, they conducted "snail operations" in Morbihan and Calvados, causing nearly 10 km of traffic jams.
Quiberon's port was also blocked, but connections to Belle-île were not stopped.
On the Quiberon peninsula a road blockade has been implemented.
On Monday the sailors have already made evidence of their determination burning, starting from dawn, tires and palettes in front of the fuel depots at the ports of Brest and Lorient, as demonstrations in Concarneau and Saint-Brieuc took hold.
The movement has spread towards the ports of the Atlantic coast and the English Channel, such as Sables d'Olonne, in the Vendée, where the fishing boats have remained at the dock, or in Havre, where about twenty boats have prevented the displacement of ships from commerce.
The fishermen of La Turballe et del Croisic, near the Loire Atlantica, interrupted in the evening the blockade of the well-known Total refinery in Donges.
In Boulogne-sur-Mer they decided to wait for the meeting that will be held Wednesday at the ministry to decide any actions.
In Guilvinec, the "crisis committee" will decide on Tuesday the future actions of the movement and the eventual participation in the meeting scheduled at the Ministry of Agriculture and Fisheries in Paris.
But some Breton fishermen don't want to be represented by those in charge of the National Fishing Committee, which didn't support their movement.
"The strike will continue" as "the fishermen will not yield" et "have no plans to move to Paris," Liliane Cariou, president of Lorient's Fisheries Committee, has announced, for example.
Professional fishermen, who do not pay taxes on diesel, currently pay about 50 cents a liter, but estimate that the performance limit of a boat is reached only with a diesel of less than 0.30 euros per liter.
Paul Newman the magnificent
"Butch Cassidy" has died of cancer, aged 83, at his Connecticut home.
Hillary et Bill Clinton, Nicolas Sarkozy, George Clooney, Julia Roberts, Robert Redford and again Sam Mendes hailed film legend and engaged actor Paul Newman, who died at the age of 83.
Embraer maintain delivery forecast in 2008
The aircraft-building company has 215 deliveries this year, despite threats of recession weighing on the world economy.
Embraer, the world's fourth-largest company in the industry, maintains delivery forecasts for 2008 with delivery of 215 planes, confirmed its chairman Fredrico Fleury Curado.
Over the course of the first semester, it delivered 97 aircraft.
The Brazilian company relies extensively on the Chinese market, particularly for aircraft in the range between 30 and 120 seats.
To date, Embraer has delivered in eight years 41 aircraft to Chinese customers.
AIG would divest more than 15% of its assets
The American International Group insurance company, put back up by the American monetary authorities, would divest more than 15% of its assets in order to reimburse the loan of 85 billion dollars, according to the Sunday edition of the Financial Times.
The AIG is ready to consider the sale of most of its activities, in addition to the divestiture of life insurance branches to the international market and to the United States, specifies the newspaper on the basis of more than authoritative sources.
The AIG's board of directors met on Sunday night to consider possible disposals, according to the FT.
There was no confirmation at AIG.
Assets that AIG plans to divest include the International Lease Financial Corp. air leasing subsidiary, its 59% stake in Transatlantic Holdings as well as its real estate portfolio and private equity investments, the FT adds.
No final decision has been made with regard to such divestitures, the FT continues.
The AIG must quickly find liquidity in order to repay the 85-billion loan granted to it by the Federal Reserve, at the cost of seeing the American authorities take over 80% of its capital.
Paris: chronicle Morning Meeting
The New York Stock Exchange blamed an all-tight slump on Monday, after the House of Representatives spoke out against the Paulson plan to rescue financial institutions: the Dow Jones lost 6.98 percent, a drop never seen, approaching 800 points, and the Nasdaq 9.14 percent.
The Dow Jones Industrial Media (DJIA) ended at 10,365.45, down 777.68 points.
In terms of points, the worst of the losses in its history (684.81 points) higher than that of September 17, 2001, the day of the reopening of the Stock Exchanges following the terrorist attacks of September 11.
The Nasdaq, the index of major tech stocks, lost 199.61 points to 1,983.73 and the enlarged Standard & Poor's 500 index lost 8.81% (i.e. 106.85 points) closing at 1,106.55, according to the closing figures.
Demoralized at the beginning of the session, Wall Street plunged after the unexpected rejection of the bailout of the banks, designed to restore stability to the markets.
With 228 votes against the plan and 205 in favour, the representatives rejected the funding requested by treasury minister Trésor Henry Paulson.
About two in three Republicans rejected the plan.
"It's a negative signal sent to the market, which sees Congress unable to adopt a plan," said Lindsey Piegza, of FTN Financial.
"All this signals how much the market counted on the project," he added, fearing that in a few days it could already be "too late" to adopt one.
"If the project were indeed moribund ... then the batonist would be quickly passed to the Fed - the U.S. Central Bank and the other central banks to solve the crisis, which would further limit credit conditions," Brian Bethune, head of finance and economics at Global Insight, warned.
And again highlighting its state of emergency "every passing day leads to the collapse of a financial institution" in the United States, the Piegza stressed.
Following the failure of the Washington Mutual on Friday, authorities organized the purchase of Wachovia's banking businesses by rival Citygroup.
In Europe, the crisis has accelerated sharply, urging urgent intervention in favour of UK banks, Benellux, Iceland and Germany, a sign that markets "are not only facing an American crisis, but a global crisis," the FTN Financial analyst added.
The bond market has risen sharply.
Treasure of 10-year maturity Treasury bonds fell to 3.632%, against 3.827% on Friday night, while the 30-year yield is at 4.161%, against 4.357% the previous day.
Obama ahead at first TV duel
After Friday's first debate between the two White House nominees, here's a secon to find out who, between John McCain and Barack Obama, was the winner.
Unsurprisingly, the headquarters of the Republican and Democratic campaign proclaimed the respective victory of the televised duel, followed by one-third of America's families.
Polithologist David Gergen believes John McCain's performance, while deserving, has not been bright enough to allow him to curb the drop in polls five weeks after the presidential election.
The Gallup Institute, of which an opinion poll published on Saturday accords a five-point lead to Obama (49% versus 44% for McCain), shows that the Democrat had the upper hand.
An instantaneous poll conducted by CbsNews on an undecided voter sample, found that 39 percent of them deem Obama the winner, while 25 percent bet on McCain.
An excellent performance by his list mate Joe Biden, expected in the course of the first and only debate with rival Sarah Palin, is expected to confirm Obama's advantage on Thursday.
"Underestimating it would be a mistake.
He has a good intuition," Bill Clinton warned this weekend.
Seventy-year-old McCain has never ceased promoting his own unquestionable foreign policy experience, stressing the "naivety" of the adversary about the arguments addressed during the first face-to-face.
"Senator Obama doesn't seem to understand that..." he has often stated on multiple occasions.
“I don’t think Senator Obama has the experience and expertise to become president,” he said.
What from yesterday constitutes a continuous target for the Republican television campaign.
Obama, for his part, pointed out how his election would represent a new future.
Without lengthening, he proved concise and precise.
He promoted his predisposition to the right decisions and the intention to restore the image of the United States on the world stage.
"McCain never understood, as opposed to Obama," he retorted, this weekend, a Democratic commercial, rebuking the Republican that he had not taken the necessary steps to cope with the impact of the financial crisis on the American people.
During the ninety-minute debate, John McCain had a lot to say about me, but nothing about you, Obama denounced yesterday, in front of 20 000 people gathered in North Carolina, in a pouring rain, for an election meeting.
He didn't even utter the words "middle class" or "workers." The financial crisis goes in favor of Obama, regarded by Americans as the person best suited to saving the economy on the brink of precipice.
"Next week it could represent a decisive breakthrough," he considered, yesterday, the influential Democratic senator Charles Schumer.
Democratic Representative Rahm Emanuel, a former member of the Clinton administration, compared Obama's intuition on economic matters to that demonstrated by Clinton during the 1998 financial crisis.
For the Democratic nominee, the current collapse "is the final verdict after eight years of poor economic policy adopted by George Bush and backed by Senator McCain."
Reiterating his intention to reduce taxes for 95% of Americans despite Wall Streed, Illinois Senator also mentioned among his priorities the achievement of the country's possible energy independence thanks to the promotion of renewables and the implementation of a health insurance system.
McCain, for his part, would build a park of 45 nuclear centers by 2030.
Prudently, the twell-questioners dodged the 700-billion-dollar (470-billion-euro) financial bailout plan adopted by Congress last night and extremely unpopular.
In Minsk, "didn't you notice that there were elections?"
Young people and opponents were demonstrating on Sunday night in the main square of the Belarusian capital to challenge in advance the results of the legislation.
A few hundred Belarusians met on Sunday evening just after the polling stations closed in Minsk's central square to protest against the fraudulent character of legislation.
While still waiting for the results, many young people had come to demonstrate against President Alexandre Loukachenko's power: "the dictator, resignation."
Some confess they didn't even vote : "What pros? They're going to do what they're going to, of my vote," says one guy.
They do not believe that that regime that has made a good impression before foreign observers with the aim of resuming in dialogue with the European Union is ready to embark on a democratic breakthrough.
Sanctions, including visa bans for 40 Belarusian executives and asset freezes, could be removed if these elections were considered legitimate by observers.
They weren't, according to the opposition.
"We have been removed from the electoral commissions and our observers have not been able to participate in the counting of votes," complained Iouri Potemkine, a spokeswoman for the Union of Democratic Forces, a heterogeneous alliance of twelve parties.
“It is a façade change,” said Alexandre Milinkieevitch, a former opposition candidate for the 2006 presidential election and leader of the pro-European movement, because the important thing is who counts ballots and in this case is the executive.
If opposition deputies enter Parliament, it's because they've been appointed, and not elected.
But their entry into parliament can involve a televised space, regional meetings and bills.”
The opposition therefore has a duty to enter the political game because "we must start dialogue with the European Union.
For if our economy, which is heavily indebted to Russia, goes into crisis, and we don’t care about any reform, we risk becoming a Russian protectorate.
Now we want to be independent.”
The opposition fears that the organisation for security and cooperation in Europe following the electoral process recognises the validity of the ballot.
It emphasizes that the participation quotas seem too high, and that the generalization of voting in advance (26% of voters in Minsk) gave way to any kind of manipulation.
"Didn't you notice that there was an election? Asks Social-Democrat leader Alexandre Kozouline, one of the two last politicial prisoners freed this year, in August.
There wasn't anyone at the polling stations.
And there will have been at most five opposition deputies in the parliament, the most cost-effective in power."
Nocturnal silence falls on the October square.
On television, President Loukachenko and members of the central electoral commission are pleased with their success.
The meeting - The blood is safe again
The Héma-Québec yesterday celebrated the tenth anniversary of its foundation, and its president, Dott.ssa FrancineDécary, had every reason to rejoice, as Héma-Québec has become one of the safest and most effective blood suppliers in the world.
And above all, he regained the public's confidence, which was seriously shaken by the infected blood scandal in the years between 1980 and 1990.
As we know, the national blood supplies managed by the Canadian Red Cross Transfusion Service were infected with the acquired immunodeficiency (HIV) and Hepatitis C virus in the 1980s.
In the face of such a drama and the protests of the population, the Government of Canada conducted in February 1994 a public investigation, directed by Judge Horace Krever, which aimed to shed light on the circumstances leading to such a tragedy and to disseminate recommendations to prevent the recurrence of such a disaster.
As Judge Krever delayed drawing up a report, some provinces set up a committee with the aim of devising a new blood management system in Canada.
In Québec, this mandate was executed by the Gélineau committee.
Due to Gélineau et Krever reports in Canada, two suppliers were created in Canada: Héma-Québec to supply Québec and Société canadienne du sang (Canadian Blood Society) for all other provinces and territories.
In 1998, when Héma-Québec started their activities, just 38% of people declared that they still had confidence in Québec’s donation system, recalls Dott.ssa Décary.
The situation was mortifying.
Thanks to continuous efforts to improve the safety of blood withdrawals and field research during the following years, we have regained public confidence," he explains before saying proud that at the beginning of 2008, about 94% of Québec's population now had confidence in Héma-Québec.
Dott.ssa Décary, however, is convinced that "trust is something extremely fragile."
Since its initiation on 28 September 1998, Héma-Québec applied the precautionary principle.
At the time, there were numerous cases in Europe of the human variant of mad cow, a new manifestation of Creutzfeldt-Jakob's disease, tells the one who has been the presidency since 1998, the year of its foundation.
“And we absolutely didn’t know if that disease could be transmitted through infected blood.
It was therefore based on one of the main recommendations of Krever and Gélinau reports, according to which, when we have doubts and could achieve a serious disease, in this case Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease that manifests itself with a degeneration of the brain that inevitably leads to death, exclusion measures are taken.
In April 1999, the blood of those who had spent more than a month in the United Kingdom since 1980 was refused.
Now, for some years now, it has been shown that without a doubt this disease is transmitted through infected blood.
This episode thus showed us that it made sense to apply the precautional principle.”
In 1999 a much more efficient technique for the detection of HIV appeared on the market: nucleic acid (TAN) tests that allow to directly recognize the presence of the virus in the blood of donors and not that of antibodies against it, on which the tests used until then were based.
TANS have made it possible to reduce the window period, i.e. from the moment the person contracted the disease to when the first viruses can be detected in the blood thanks to our techniques, the specialist explains.
It was possible to reduce that period to a duration of less than one week.
The risk of ignoring the presence of infected donors and being unable to identify them has thus decreased considerably.
These improvements have significantly reduced the risk of contracting AIDS with a blood transfusion.
Today, one in 12 million people run the risk of receiving infected blood from the HIV virus.
“In other words, this means you’ll need to distribute 12 million blood samples before someone gets infected with the HIV virus.
Since Héma-Québec distributes between 400 000 and 500 000 blood samples every year, it will take more than 24 years before an HIV case transmitted with a transfusion is found.
I believe that today we can feel comfortable receiving or prescribing a blood transfusion to recover health, says the head of management of Héma-Québec.
“Our challenge for the next decade is to maintain the safety of our products.
You never know what pathogen will emerge into the system.
Not later than a year ago the Chikungunya virus appeared on Réunion Island which, like the Western Nile virus, is transmitted in the blood from an insect.
You have to be constantly vigilant," continues Dott.ssa Décary before pointing out that in addition to having safe resources, the Héma-Québec must also provide enough.
“Necessities are increasing as the population ages.
Beinging the most aggressive cancer cures, patients often need an intake of red blood cells or platelets.
The ageing population also has consequences for the number of donors, which on average makes up only 3% of Québec residents acceptable as donors."
However, all healthy people who are over the age of 18 and meet the eligibility criteria can donate blood every 56 days, i.e. six times a year.
On average, 14% of people who volunteer as donors are not accepted, because they are affected by AIDS or malaria, for example, and if they have traveled to a country where the latter is widespread, they will be rejected for a period of one year.
“With this 3%, we manage to maintain an inventory capable of responding to the needs of hospitals.
Cases of scarcity are very rare.
But you have to renew the members of this group of donors as they get older and can no longer make donations.
Now it is difficult to get people to donate blood, but above all to detain and incite them to become regular donors.
Nowadays life is so frantic that you hardly find an hour to donate blood," says desolate Francine Décary.
With an ⁬297-million annual budget, Héma-Québec not only distributes blood samples, but also human tissues, of which bone transplants, eye-to-eye heart valves, and skin that is used almost exclusively for severe burns.
These tissues are analysed, processed and frozen before being deposited with the Héma-Québec which manages the only umbilical cord blood public bank in Québec.
In the umbilical cord, which was once considered a biomedical deviation, blood rich in stem cells abounds that can serve to cure serious diseases, such as leukemia.
"We celebrated, a few weeks ago, the inclusion in the bank of our one thousandth sample of cord blood and we plan to take more than a thousand every year.
You should start proposing them for transplants early next year," says Dott.ssa Décary, which describes this practice of cord-blood drawing as very popular.
"Many mothers are disappointed not to be able to have access to it", since the Héma-Québec does not have the necessary means to propose this complex and expensive program in all the hospitals of Quebec.
Football - L'Impact in the semi-finals
Impact Montréal qualified for the semi-final of the Serie A eliminatories, winning 3-1 against Seattle's Sounders, yesterday, during a second quarter-final game at the Saputo Stadium.
Midfielder Antonio Ribeiro, scored the decisive goal at 90'.
After a goalless first half on both sides, David Testo and Rocco Placentino scored the next goals for Montreal.
For the Sounders, Roger Levesque was ready to mark Matt Jordan.
Recall that at the first game of the round-trip, on Friday, the Impact had suffered a 2-1 loss at the Starfire Complex, in Tukwila, in the outskirts of Seattle.
The Impact will face Vancouver Whitecaps in the semi-finals.
On Saturday night in Minnesota, Thundler filled a 3-1 first in the comeback game by winning 4-3, but the Whitecaps will still participate in the semi-final.
The Impact will face Whitecaps both on Friday and Sunday, but further details will be confirmed in the day.
In short - Kovalev's Decisive Net
Canadien and his aces are ready to restart the season.
Trio formed by Alex Kovalev, Tomas Plekanec and Andrei Kostitsyn animated the offensive phase of play by winning 3-2 against the Panthers, Florida, last night at Centro Bell.
Kostitsyn, the star of the match, scored a goal, made two passes, and picked up a pass for the win, in the fourth of the Hab's preparatory games.
Decisive for the victory the only net signed Kovalev.
"I'm not ready yet, the Russian warned.
“I need another game to feel comfortable.”
"He attempted to overdo it in the first period, noted Guy Carbonneau.
But I think he appreciated the outcome of the match.
"There was quite an ovation."
Charest Points to the North
The Prime Minister does not rule out starting elections before the end of the year.
At the end of the General Council of the Liberal Party, Jean Charest went yesterday to Jean-Talon, in Québec where the new Minister of Health, Yves Bolduc, (left), tries to get himself elected during today's partial elections.
"Expanding the borders of our last great northern frontier": this is the long-term ambition of Jean Charest, presented yesterday in Levis, by means of instruments with a strong visual impact, on the occasion of the general council of the Quebec Liberal Party.
Behind him, during his speech, the projection of a kind of "Image Mill".
On three maxi screens, spectacular negatives of hydroelectric dams, mining crops, images of Quebec seen with "Google Maps", maps, etc
Great means as in the middle of the election campaign.
During the press conference, moreover, the prime minister refused to rule out the start of the autumn elections, even though he noticed that he was very committed.
The "Northern Plan" is the fourth point of a "projection" that the Prime Minister claims to have for Quebec, that of a "new economic space", which includes a first agreement with France on manpower, a second agreement between Canada and Europe and other agreements with Ontario.
Often nominated in the most recent speeches of the Prime Minister, the opening of the North – the northern territory to the 49th parallel – had never been the subject of a presentation.
However, the Prime Minister merely explained why the North would be "a jewel of immense potential", but did not rule on timing or investment quotas, just as he did not explain what will be the precise priorities in the implementation of future projects.
At most, he limited himself to pointing out what in his view deserves a priority position: hydro-electricity, forests, but above all the mining sector, which following the growth of emerging countries and the reconstruction of public infrastructure has experienced several improvements.
According to Charest's list, the North contains "nickel, zinc, copper, platinum, gold, silver, iron, titan, and even diamonds."
That "twich territory twice the size of France," it's here, it's Quebec.
Not only is it here, but it's inherent in us," he insisted, as if to urge a national founding myth.
The foundations of Quebec are rooted in its relationship with the North, he pointed out : gold fever in Habits, national engineering, James Bay.
"It's up to us, it's our future," he added, after a theatrical pause to contemplate the immense map behind him.
The North, 70% of the territory of Quebec, includes a part of the Labrador that Quebec has always vindicated, the Prime Minister specified during the press conference.
“It’s a position traditionally maintained by all governments.
There's a border line that hasn't existed in a long time and, when the opportunity arises, I'll reiterate that position in the name of the government," he indicated.
“Considering climate change, we need to re-evaluate the North,” he said, speaking of the north-west passage, which will be opened throughout the year due to the melting of the ice.
In this new geopolitical context, the Prime Minister esteems that occupying the territory is a duty.”
What about the locals, the First Nations and the Inuits?
The Prime Minister said that the government of Quebec would be very careful in this regard and that the project would meet their interests, especially those of young people.
Two ex-ministers from different parties, Pierre Corbeil and Michel Létourneau (former MP of PQ d'Ungava), have already started the consultation of about thirty communities.
An inter-ministerial committee chaired by Benoît Pelletier and in which Julie Boulet (Transport and Natural Resources) occupies an important position, has started work.
The Prime Minister does not rule out a great meeting, not necessarily a summit, but a time when "common visions will be provided"
The development of the North would also abide by the principles of sustainable development.
The Northern Plan will "concerted, tidy and will respect the environment," he promised.
Jean Charest described this "project" as a continuum with that of the preceding liberal prime ministers.
"Like the liberals who preceded us, we know how to stand out to transform challenges into new opportunities for growth and development."
On the other hand, this was one of the recurring themes of the weekend, which began on Friday with a great cocktail in honor of the 50th anniversary of Jean Lesage's arrival at the head of the party.
One of the resolutions adopted proposes to rename the Beauharnois Central in Adélard-Godbout.
"From Godbout, creator of Hydro-Québec, to Lesage that nationalizes hydroelectricity, to Bourassa, creator of James Bay, our party has always been able to forge the future," Jean Charest said.
During the press-conference, he resorted to another, Conservative this time, John Diefenbaker's "Roads to the North" program, Prime Minister from 1957 to 1963.
Facing the Eastmain1a-Ruper hydroelectric dam project, Charest has specified that its commissioning would be planned for 2012.
"All this remains among us, but I feel that we will make it," he confessed to his militants, eliciting heartfelt applause.
In fact, the prime minister, which in April claimed there would have been no election in 2008 and probably not even in 2009, did not dismantle the possibility of starting a ballot in the autumn, which could happen in accordance with the law.
He emphasizes that he is certainly very committed, especially in a Federal Council mission to China and the Quebec-New York summit.
The General Council of the weekend has provided the liberal leader with a platform of nationalist resolutions, in particular the "constitutional aspirations" that have gone as smooth as oil: the PLQ above all calls for recognition of "Quebel specificity" in the Constitution.
It also hopes that Quebec will appoint members of the Canadian Broadcasting and Telecommunications Council members and that they will have a vetous right over the decisions taken in Quebec.
As for the opposition, the outline of the Northern Plan has been skeptically received.
PQ leader Pauline Marois dusted off the Northern Quebec Development Policy launched by the Landry government in 2001.
According to you, the liberal government, which came to power in 2003, would not respect its application.
If he had done it, "we would have progressed today" commented Marois alongside Jean-Talon's candidate (scrutiny that took place today), Françoise Mercure.
The ADQ said it supported the adoption of the Northern Plan by the government.
"What we hope, is that all this is not simply perceived as a spectacle," commented leader Sébastien Proulx, Mario Dumont's replacement, returning just today from the holidays.
Letters - A disgraced country
It was Vic Toews, ex-minister of "Justice" (irony of fate) of the Harper cabinet, who allowed himself to declare, last summer, that Louise Arbor was a "shame" for Canada.
Dishonoring in this way a woman who fought relentlessly for justice and who deserves all our admiration says a lot both about the prime minister's entourage and Stephen Harper himself.
Here we are before a government that is the only one, of all the Western countries involved, not to have repatriated one of its citizens detained in the Guantanamo concentration camp.
A government that does not take into account the fact that Omar Khadr was a mere teenager at the time when the defending facts took place.
The only government that, despite having signed the Geneva Convention, denies its own word and lets a child-soldier rot in inhumane conditions.
The only one who confides in a parody of justice to establish the culpability of the accused.
We would like to point out, for information, that UNICEF, Amnesty International and the Canadian Bar Association, in particular, call for the repatriation of Omar Khadraffin that he may be subjected to a regular trial in Canada.
It is precisely the same government that closes its eyes to torture: he cares very little that a young adult is now psychologically destroyed, perhaps forever, after so many years of abuse.
We still don't realize it, but we're witnessing an insidious erosion of our rights, freedom and "democracy," a word that Harper doesn't stop flaunting and sounds so senseless on her lips.
Here we are at the moment when there is no longer any morality: you can mistreat, torture, arbitrarily imprison a Canadian citizen according to the discretion of a head of government up to today minority (to see what will be after October 14...).
Numerous electors believe that a party that preaches Virtue, Respection of the Law and the Order represents salvation: they do not see that this government has renounced what constituted the reputation and dignity of our country.
Right here is shame, Mr. Toews, in renouncing all of this, and nowhere else.
Free opinion - Barrick Gold's Orwellian Speech
In the September 17 edition, Le Devoir published a letter from M. Patrick J. Garver, vice-president director of Canadian mining company Barrick Gold.
Pursuing an Orwellian logic, the latter states that the world's largest auriferal company has filed a six-million defamation lawsuit against three authors and a pettered small publishing house with the intention of initiating a "transparent public debate."
If it is true that judges are independent and impartial, it is not the truth that they judge, especially in the case of a defamation lawsuit, but the law.
Unsurprised, this substantial difference is completely hidden by Garver.
Because for Barrick Gold, the best way to conduct a public debate would lead to ruin anyone daring to support a stance contrary to his.
No matter the outcome of the process, the writers and the publisher of the book Black Canada will have to spend a substantial amount of money in order to organize their own defense and fight, with decidedly unequal weapons, against an army of lawyers financed by the virtually bottomless pockets of the mining company.
Moreover, in these imparised arms struggles, the tactic is often to ruin the defendant in order to impose a consensual regulation embarrassing the opposition in addition to scaring anyone who dares to pronounce again on the matter.
If Barrick Gold had really wanted a public debate, it is before the public opinion that he would have to present his own view of the facts.
Contrary to what happens in many countries where Barrick Gold works, in Canada the debates normally take place with the exchange of opinions and not with batons.
It would therefore have been enough for the company to open its books, its documents, and establish a real debate, through newspapers, media, publications and the funding of teams of independent researchers who could shed light on the issue.
But Barrick Gold doesn't seem to have too much public confidence and misrejects the petitions and letters in support of the authors of Black Canada.
Perhaps Garver could explain how he can ask for a "public" debate when his disdain for that same audience is clearly evident?
In the vice-president's world, it seems that this word indicates only the handsomely paid lawyers and not the whole of the Canadians ; here is a rather narrow view of the concept.
In a democratic system, even disparagers have the right to speak.
What Barrack Gold doesn’t understand – or doesn’t want to understand – is that the public that supports the authors and publishers of Black Canada does so first and foremost because it rejects the brutal means adopted by the mining company.
This audience is ready to listen to Barrick and judging, but on the basis of civil exchanges of opinion, and not in the perspective of what many perceive as a kind of gagging (looking for the "public debate", Barrick Gold has just given the authors of Canada Nero an ultimatum instructing them to suspend the use of that word...)
Beyond Garver’s surreal discourse, Barrack Gold’s lawsuit constitutes a direct attack on freedom of university research and the pursuit of the truth, indispensable in every democratic society.
It denies in bulk the right to citing authentic sources and debating facts and interpretations, which represent the very basis of intellectual work.
If in a country like Canada it is impossible to study and discuss on uncomfortable topics to the richest businesses in the world, under penalty of multiple lawsuits, who can?
If Barrick Gold really wanted a public and transparent debate, it could do so according to the scientific norms as the authors of Black Canada do.
You have the right to replicate.
For a long time, the university environment, of which we are part, is able to manage debates and disagreements and to resolve conflicts.
Barrick Gold's excessive measures clearly show that, contrary to its claims, it does not seek transparent debate at all.
This comes at the expense of the university world, as well as the same public debate in Canada, and it’s time for governments to take measures that can limit abusive lawsuits.
Operation - bailout for Wall Street – Dialogue Between Republicans and Democrats
The Bush administration and the leaders of Congress, representatives of the Republican and Democratic sides, yesterday finally came to an agreement on the largest finance-saving plan after the Great Depression of the 1930s.
The new version of America's 700-billion-dollar plan, which aims to curb the financial crisis that has been weighing for months on Wall Street, has yet to be ratified by the two chambers of Congress.
In particular, it foresees: verifications of its effective implementation by elected leaders, new mechanisms that guarantee the reimbursement of public money, new provisions to contain the number of foreclosed homes for non-payments, as well as a right of inspection on wages paid to managers of protected enterprises.
U.S. Secretary of the Treasury, Henry Paulson, the Speaker of the House of Representatives, Nancy Pelosi, and the Republican representative at the negotiating table, Senator Judd Gregg, announced the good news a few minutes after midnight yesterday at the end of a two-day negotiating marathon.
"We've made great progress ahead of an effective agreement on the markets," Bush administration representative Henry Paulson said in a statement.
"Every decision has been made in such a way as to safeguard the American economy and people from the crisis currently underway on Wall Street," the Democrat explained.
“It’s the crux: if we hadn’t acted like this, priceless despirators and upsets would have spilled into the daily lives of the Americans,” Republican Judd Gregg said.
The conclusion of the negotiations was marked by concerns regarding the impact of a new failure on investors.
The time limit agreed corresponded to the opening of the markets this morning.
United later in the negotiations, the two candidates for the U.S. presidential elections were rather supportive of the agreement, without, however, avoiding expressing their discontent with the adoption of extreme solutions.
Democratic Senator Barack Obama is credited with adding some provisions to the agreement, in particular that which will allow owners to maintain their domicile and that inherent in wages aimed at business executives covered by the state.
"When taxpayers are asked to take extraordinary measures due to the irresponsibility of others, there is none to rejoice about.
But these measures are necessary," he commented on the American TV screen.
Interviewed on another channel to see if he will vote in favor of the plan, his Republican opponent, John McCain, replied: "I'd like to analyze the details, but I hope so."
"According to what I've read, it's something that we all have to digest and that will allow us to move forward."
The option of a non-intervention is simply unacceptable," he said.
The 700-billion-dollar plan aims to repay with taxpayers' money the dubious credits, at-risk and unsalable assets of America's financial institutions.
The plan comes after 14 months of crisis due to the outbreak of a housing bubble that led primarily to the collapse of risk mortgage loans (mortgages) , and then extended to other more widespread forms of credit securities (PCAAs) and resulted in the freezing of liquidity and the disrepair of banks and financial institutions.
It was a group of republicans in office that screwed up the first draft of a deal last week.
It seems to have come out of that dead end since Democrats have accepted that the "toxic" assets of some struggling companies are only protected by the government, rather than purchased.
In order to meet everyone in Congress, a provision has been added that, if the government were to cope with losses over the course of the five years to come due to such a program, it could impose new taxes in order to maintain its balance.
The new plan foresees the payment of a first sum of 250 billion to which 100 billion can still be added if the president deems it necessary.
Paying of 350 billion is expected to be ratified by a further congression vote.
In exchange for its aid, Washington will get shareholdings in protected companies that will be worth the dividends in case of profit and that can resell when the waters have calmed down.
According to the plan, the federal government will have the opportunity to delay foreclosure procedures related to real estate loans and to set limits on remuneration and departing allowances paid to the managers of the companies involved.
As of today, the plan's architects would like to submit it to the House of Representatives vote, and the Senate vote on Wednesday.
Despite all the efforts, he's still not sure he's given the go-ahead to their plan.
In fact yesterday there were several votes opposed to the project, both at the Republican and Democratic sides.
"Evidently, Washington doesn't hear from you," Republican Scott Garrett, a member of the House Finance Committee, told his voters.
“Yes, we have a serious financial problem.
Yes, there are solutions.
But don’t take advantage of taxpayers.
Leader of a small group in Congress dubbed "the caucus of skeptics," Democratic Representative Brad Sherman presents the situation differently, but comes to the same conclusion.
"Four hundred eminent professors of economics, including three Nobel laureates, have written to us to say: we ask Congress not to draw hasty conclusions and to carefully examine the way to take," he said.
At the same time, in Europe there was once again evidence of the global nature of the crisis.
After an emergency meeting, the three Benelux countries announced the partial nationalization of the Fortis banking group for 11.2 billion euros (16.3 billion dollars).
The intervention of the governments of Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg became necessary when the French bank BNS Paribas made it known that it would renounce the purchase of its competitor, in full bankruptcy after the loss of 2 billion euros (2.9 billion dollars) for the crisis of at-risk property loans in the United States.
Fortis has 85 000 employees and is part of Europe's twenty largest banks and the ten largest insurers.
"It's not lost money," Dutch Finance Minister Wouter Bos said.
In return, there is influence and right to vote in banking matters.
“It’s certainly something that savers and families appreciate now that the times are unsurpassed.”
During this period, at the other end of the Channel, the British government appears to be being nationalised by another bank poised for the financial crisis.
According to the British press, the government would take charge of all the loans currently underway at Bradford and Bringley bank, including more than 41 billion (75.5 billion dollars) of real estate loans.
Spain's Santander bank announced yesterday that she will be the one to buy the branches and customer accounts of the British bank.
Since the beginning of the economic crisis Bradford and Bringley will be the second bank, after Northern Rock, to undergo a process of nationalization by the British government.
Paul Newman (1925-2008) - Death of a committed icon
Blue-eyed beauty was for a long time her ace up her sleeve, for others her weak spot.
Had he gotten rid of the narcissistic mirror, the American actor with a bewildering smile?
Yes and no.
His discretion, sensibility, and evident talent would expel him from that circle of powerful consecrated to real sacred monsters.
But what constancy!
Paul Newman, who died on Friday at the age of 83, on his Connecticut ranch following lung cancer, remains one of the last icons of splendour American cinema.
Less monumental than Brando, to whom he resembled and of which he was for a long time a sort of rival on the screen, more cautious in his game, certainly more fragile, but also a spokesman for the freedom and development of dramatic convictions.
From mixed couples are often born magical babies.
He was born in Ohio in 1925, to a German Jewish father and a mother of Hungarian descent.
From these European roots will be born one of the glorious faces of the American dream.
A humble ego, the last mark of quality.
Since in him he recognized himself as a whole country in love with the safe-made man, the fighter of the Pacific War, proud in combat, who in the early 1950s studied dramatic art in Yale, later attended the Actor Studio in New York, before triumphing at the theater, on television and early in cinemas.
That same civilian-rights militant man was also the racing driver who ranked second to the 24 Hours of Mans in 1979, as well as sex-symb symbols, although part of a model couple since 1958 with actress Joanne Woodward.
The areas of the shadows of a reserved, discreet, busy star, allergic to show-business scandals leave with him, of which we will never be able to completely unravel the mystery.
An honest man, in the noblest sense of the term.
This weekend Paul Newman's disappearance was a dip in the heart for cinephiles, while his great characters populate the black screen of memories.
Over half a century of full-changing American cinema is revived through its interpretations.
Butch Cassidy's charming cow-boy (1969) and that boy unable to crave his fiery wife (Elizabeth Taylor) in The Rooftop Cat was adapted from Tennessee Williams in 1958.
More recently, we remember him as the sensitive Mr. Bridge (1990), disturbed by a marriage that did not keep what was promised.
So many other Paul Newmans went down in history.
Boxer Rocky Graziano, delinquent, tormented, violent, unforgettable in there someone loves me, by Robert Wise, a role destined for James Dean and resumed after his death, which officially launched Newman in 1956.
That nice crooker in the excellent Stangata, Oscar for Best Picture in 1973.
Bitter and vindictive billiard player in The Slort, magnificent film by Robert Rossen (1961), The Color of Money by Martin Scorsese, which earned him in 1987 the only oscar out of 9 nominations.
That face through the years, it was his.
He's still.
Ages follow each other through its path.
Young people especially know him for the cult movie Slap Shot by George Roy Hill (1977), in the role of Reggie Dunlop, coach of an unorthodox ice hockey team.
His fans found him in 1994 in Mr. Hula Hoop, of the Coen brothers.
The circle had closed with a last-ditch godman of the Irish Mafia in My Father's Era, in 2003.
Unlike the greatest legends of cinema that soon abandoned the trade, Newman will remain in theaters for a long time, denouncing Hollywood in all its glitz, parties and concessions.
Bertrand Tavernier, a French filmmaker, called him, with a hint of irony, "a living challenge to underplaying," judging his performance exaggerated.
The profession hasn’t always been a game for the handsome Paul Newman, to whom the roles seemed to fall like godsend.
However, he often preferred the role of the outreach to that of the young actor: neurotic characters, alcoholic carrions or desperates, of whom he expressed disarray and rebellion with his perfect traits.
As a director, Newman revealed unknown nuances of his true pudic sensibility.
Jennifer's first time, 1968 (based on the novel by Canadian Margaret Laurence), with wife Joanne Woodward in the title role, traced a thin female portrait of melancholy.
He, who lost his son Scott to an overdose, dedicated to him in 1984 the film Harry and Son, a discreet and sad film.
Among the countless tributes rained down yesterday on Newman, his great friend and old comrade-in-arm Robert Redford (had acted together in La Stangata and Butch Cassidy) stated with infinite sadness "His presence made my life and this country better."
Generosity and love for life, two great characteristics of the missing actor.
Beyond a career as a legend, a great militant for civil rights, ecology, independent cinema, a contestant of the Hollywood driving is thus extinguished.
We will have extravised his face to stand out on bottles of vinegar, sauces, biscuits and other foods, judging it a choice of bad taste, although born as gag.
However, the revenue from that sales was allocated to its Children's Foundation in Difficulty.
Newman, instead of resting on his myth, chose to commit until the end.
He left his five daughters a letter that said" Always and until the end, your father was incredibly appreciative to his good star.
Being there was a privilege.”
And just as it was for us to follow the path of such a clear and generous American artist.
Citizen model, therefore, and much more.
What was produced in Zuschlag Invoicing Factory & Partners?
Soon, on 8 October, the hearing of the criminal trial against János Zuschlag and associates will be discussed.
The politician and his associates are accused of recurred commercial fraud committed as part of a criminal association that has caused considerable damage, and of other offences, including falsification of documents.
According to the Public Prosecutor's Office, the money obtained from notices of competition, was used by them for private purposes, and they have often certified the execution of activities through false invoices.
State and municipal money, a total of 65 million guilders, ended up partly in the coffers of the Fiatal Baloldal organization, partly in the pockets of some people.
According to the method, announced by the investigating authorities, they have withdrawn the money obtained from notices of competition on the basis of invoices for non-existent transactions and the associations have never fulfilled their obligations.
According to the accusatory act most of the bills were false.
To do them, invoice blocks were purchased at Józsefváros market, while circular stamps were manufactured in Kiskunhalas.
And in order to decide on behalf of which companies to issue the invoices, it was established among other things during the trip through the hills above the Balaton where János Zuschlag had sent István Lados.
According to the latter's confession he had to choose pensions and restaurants.
He asked for an invoice for consummations, on the model of these then were prepared the invoices to be forwarded to the notices of competition, among others, to those of the Ministry for Youth and Sport, the Ministry of Labour and Social Affairs and the Municipality of Budapest.
Much of these are the work of István Lados and András Őri who, according to our information, has drawn up above all the reports necessary to obtain funding.
One of the choices of Lado's "travelling studies" was a brewery.
According to two invoices issued by Zuschlag and associates in 2003, the place of catering provided meals for a group of 90 people of the Biztos Pont Egyesület Association.
So many people, however, according to the restaurateur, would not all enter the club together.
Similarly, the invoice issued in summer 2002 at the Jobb Jövőért Foundation, hereinafter referred to as Együtt in Harmadik Évezredért, is full of formal and content errors.
A pension in Badacsonytomaj has only three rooms, more than 10 people could not stay overnight, however 130 guests were included in the invoice.
But the compilers of the invoices full of imagination exaggerated when they reported Pigal Kft., this also deals with catering, in whose accounting - according to the information issued to the judicial authorities - did not even appear the documents on behalf of the companies Jobb Jövőért and Hoppá.
Győző Farkas, director of the company, told hvg.hu that when he was summoned to the power of attorney and they showed him the invoices, he immediately understood that it was false documents.
"On timbre the name and VAT number of our enterprise were at the wrong point."
He added: in addition to the formal reasons there was also the fact that according to the invoice a hundred people were accommodated, when in total they have eleven rooms and there are not so many beds.
Moreover, the workmanship was catering and their restaurant has no longer been in operation for some time.
Assiduous invoice compilers did not bother to inquire that the Budapest travel agency of their choice, only organizes trips abroad, so they foolishly indicated on the invoice with cash payment trips to Hungary, which did not agree with the company's practice of issuing invoices.
In addition, as demonstrated during the survey, VAT was also included on the invoice, while travel to countries outside the European Union is VAT exempt.
Among other things, the amount of VAT was also incorrect on the four documents issued by Hotel Révfülöp Kft. in favor of the Jobb Jövőért foundation, in fact on the services offered by the company it is necessary to apply VAT at 15% and not 25% as indicated on the invoices, in addition the hotel employees issued only printed invoices, while the invoice was handwritten.
In addition to this, the "issuers" ruined other invoices, in the case of a company they indicated as an activity carried out the transport of goods by road, as is known from the procedure of the Public Prosecutor's Office, activities that was not carried out by the company originally founded for investment advice.
In addition, due to formal defects - cause non-compliant invoicing format, fake stamp - it has been discovered that the invoices issued in favor of Együtt in Harmadik Évezredért Alapítvány, of XXI. Századi Kulturális Egyesület and Biztos Pont are false.
The same happened in the case of Ravenna Bt. or the Kunfehértó Sports Field, whose representatives denied issuing the invoices in question.
The falsifiers did not even pay attention to the fact that the HIB Kft. of Nyíregyháza was in liquidation when (2001) a group of Fiatal Városért Egyesület had lunch, precisely for this reason the curator of the liquidation of the company, according to our information, denied that he issued that invoice.
They issued invoices knowingly
Another group of invoice broadcasters consists of those who were aware that they had to issue fictitious invoices.
The Esély Budapestért Alapítány Foundation issued an invoice at the request of Fiatal Baloldal - Ifjú Szocialisták in favor of the Együtt foundation in Harmadik Évezredért of almost 4 million guilders.
Ludovic Györfi, one of the defendants, according to the accusatory act, persuaded Collab Kft's director to issue fictitious invoices.
The Biogate Kft currently in liquidation collaborated with Zuschlag and partners, which in 2003 provided invoices issued worth about 5 million guilders to the Jobb Jövőért Alapítány foundation, the Atlasz Egyesület association and the Vörösfenyő Egyesület association.
Not even movement of money because the prepared invoices were then reversed, but the associations forgot to make this appear in their documents.
In the following group of companies issuing invoices belong to those who are connectable to the defendants.
E.g. Ivan Kubatov, authorized to deliver the general manager of Delta Trió Kft, based in Budapest but not available there, which deals with trade in fuel and motor vehicles.
The company released various invoices in October, November and December 2005 related to the sale of IT means, worth about 18 million guilders.
According to data from the investigation the Cash Investment Kft. of Kiskunhalas under liquidation since 14 September 2005 bought these invoices.
The general manager of this company was a Romanian to whom Ivan Kubatov submitted the documents concerning the company between 2002 and 2005.
Kubatov, during the inquiry, acknowledged that he had issued invoices on behalf of the company to Atlasz, Hoppá and Együtt in Harmadik Évezredért Alapítvány and the countervalue was taken from him.
At the express request of Ivan Kubatov Romanian masons they founded the companies Fa-Bani Jos Faipari Kft. and Jos-Investment Bt.
Actually, however, none of the companies were operational, because according to the defendant they had not received work.
However, the companies were useful because at the request of co-defendant lawyer Zsolt Szente, editor of the founding documents of the companies, on behalf of the companies were issued invoices to Zuschlag-related associations: Atlasz and Y-Generáció.
On behalf of the companies Cash Investment, Delta Trió, Fa-Bani Jos, Jos Investment and Linaxtrade Kubatov has issued 15 invoices to the various entities for a total value of 14.2 million guilders.
The power of attorney considers that Kubatov pocketed 10% of the amounts indicated on the invoices, as well as VAT.
The man also used the Szi-Beri Bt. to issue fictitious invoices, fabricated for Biztos Pont's contesting notice.
Y-Generáció and Szövetség at Modern Magyarországért in January 2006 submitted a call for the Socialist Party's public utility, Táncsics Mihály Alapítvány (TMA).
They also won 500,000 guilders apiece but the sums weren't used for the purposes set out in the call.
In order for this not to surface, five fictitious invoices received from the Works Team Kft submitted to TMA.
These invoices were found during the search of Árpád Kemecsei's office at the Ministry of Education.
At the hearing a part of the defendants, as they have already done in the prosecutor's office, will probably report on the progress of the production of invoices.
However, the most interesting thing could be if the number one defendant, János Zuschlag, decides to lift the veil over everything.
His confession so far hasn't been very detailed and although he's promised several times to talk about everything extensively, he hasn't yet.
You have to see if it's in his interest, it could also be that the Socialists have made agreements with Zusi, who - in exchange for compensation of 50 million guilders, much of the damage - would be willing to make the scapegoat.
Zuschlag for now could have been offended since his requests to ease his conditions of captivity, i.e. the granting of house arrest, have so far not been answered.
While non-compensable commercial offenders and causing much greater damages can freely walk or at least wait at their own house for the end of the proceedings against them, which is not granted to him.
The psychology of excrecise licitate: why spend more money?
At auctions shoppers are likely to spend more, to overestimate the goods.
Neurologists and economist researchers at the University of New York have managed to uncover the neuro-economic basis of this well-known and seemingly irrational behavior.
According to their study that appeared in the journal Science men don't want to stay behind in a race such as the eBay auction, so they're willing to pay even a higher price.
The research sought to trace the nervous pattern of this "match" at the level of the nervous system via experimental rods.
One group of volunteers played the lot, the other group of players participated in an auction while with the functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) method they examined the active areas of the brains of the people participating in the experiment.
In both games the participants could win money, but in the game of auction, in which the substantive element is to licitize more than the other, the streaked body under the cerebral cortex enters intensively into operation when you lose to the game.
In addition to other functions, this cerebral area plays a role in the “autogratification” mechanism of the nervous system.
Therefore, according to the researchers, the rise in the price is explained by avoiding the vehement reaction of the streaked body.
To support their theory, economists in the research group carried out a financial pursuit survey.
In this investigation, three groups licited against a partner under different conditions.
To the control group they reported what the real value of the product in question was and they made their bet.
To another group in addition to this they have promised that in case of winning they will receive another prize of 15 dollars.
The third ground instead would have received 15 dollars before the auction, but members of the group would have lost the money in the event of an auction loss.
The difference between the two groups consisted in the fact that for one of them the winning was emphasized, while for the other the loss.
Compromising the neurologist hypothesis the members of the group playing "against the loss" were willing to make higher bets than the control group or "winning".
"These results are not deductible from any of the previously existing economic theories.
In fact, these so far explained the phenomena found at the auctions with the joy of winning and the avoidance of risk.
“Only the methods of creating the neurological image have helped solve the contradiction between the two theories, so that we have come to a third explanation,” said Andrew Schotter, a professor at the Faculty of Economics at the University of New York.
Hepatitis: When to contact your doctor?
Nearly half a billion people in the world suffer from chronic hepatitis B or C, chronic liver disease is mentioned among the first causes of death in Hungary.
In Hungary, the number of patients with hepatitis amounts to one hundred thousand, of which only 7000 are treated: the reason is that many sufferers do not know they are infected.
Hepatitis is the collective name of contagious liver disease caused by several viruses.
There are many similarities between symptoms, typical are hepatitis and jaundice, but their pathogens and diseases caused differ from each other.
Hepatitis viruses spread into two main forms depending on the pathogen: hepatitis A and E via the food canal (e.g. water and infected foods), hepatitis B, C, D and other viruses with blood and organic fluids (sperm, vaginal discharge, saliva).
For those traveling it is useful to get vaccinated against Virus A and B, there is no vaccine against C.
The symptoms of hepatitis A appear two-three weeks after the consumption of infected foods, accompanied by general malaise, weakness, abdominal pain and nausea.
After yellowish spots are formed on the skin and white of the eyes, the feces become lauced and dark urine, healing takes place within two to three weeks.
One million people die every year worldwide from hepatitis B, the most serious variant of the disease, which causes liver cancers and cirrhosis.
The main reason is, according to estimates, that only one in ten people receive pharmacological treatment.
Hepatitis C can also have very serious consequences: if it is not cured it can cause cirrhosis of the liver and liver tumors.
Most infected people don’t know they’re contracting the disease because pathogens sneakily destroy liver cells, in many cases the first symptoms appear after 20-30 years.
So it is very important the prevention and carrying out of regular medical tests.
This disease is much more frequent than average in three groups: those who had a blood transfusion before 1991, drug addicts and tattoos.
To date, the number of people infected with chronic hepatitis C virus amounts to 200 million.
Since even a routine laboratory exam reveals the disease is worthwhile contacting your GP when noticing any symptoms of unknown origin.
Recognized in time the disease is curable, the virus can be eliminated by the body, so the infectious power ceases and the formation of tumors can be prevented.
September 30 is World Hepatitis Day, organized for the first time in 2004 around the world by patient associations.
The aim is to draw attention to chronic viral hepatitis, the causes of its development and the early recognition of the disease.
Who believes in God feels less pain?
According to the result of a recent survey, religious faith functions as a kind of analgesis that made the death of Catholic martyrs more bearable.
Scientists at the University of Oxford performed an interesting experiment, examining how faith in god affects the sensation of pain, Britain's Telegraph said.
As part of the rather bizarre investigation, an electroshock was carried out to 12 Catholic believers and as many atheists, while everyone observed a painting depicting the virgin Mary.
Based on the results, Catholic believers were able to dismiss much of the pain.
Thanks to the exploratory technique of the human brains it was discovered that Catholics were able to activate the part of the brain responsible for pain control.
The test is the first in a series of researches in which in addition to academics also take part scientists, philosophers and theologians of the University.
The test took place exactly like this: an instrument that emits discharges and passes the electroshock was connected to the left hand of the subject of the experiment.
The researchers asked the participants to focus on two paintings, the Virgin Mary painted in the seventeenth century by Sassoferrato and the Dame with the ermine of Leonardo da Vinci painted in the 15th century.
The authors of the examination hoped that the image of the Virgin Mary would originate a kind of religious state among believers, while Leonardo's painting was chosen because it did not differ too much from the other and they considered it a tranquilizing effect.
Subjects from the experiment spent half an hour in front of the MRI scanner and in this time received 20 electroshocks in four consecutive series, while observing either the religious-themed painting or the other.
Catholics reported that the Virgin Mary's vision made them feel "safe," as if "someone cared" about them and "reassured themselves and were pervaded by peace."
Looking at the religious-themed image they accounted for 12% less pain than when they had Leonardo's work ahead of them.
Psychologist Miguel Farias, who took part in the test himself, believes that such a result can be achieved with any image with a strong effect even for atheists, but we must find the suitable image.
"We must look for an image towards which they have very positive feelings, such as a mother or a father," he said.
Anglican Bishop Tom Wright was pleased with the search result.
"The practice of faith should change, and in many cases it manages to change our personality.
After the excellent results, only the question remains if the painting of a Madonna by Leonardo or another painter of similar caliber is presented to believers, what effect could it have on the control of pain?
Would the vision of a quality Virgin Mary bring better results?
You're what you're saying about yourself
On the day of the tenth birthday Google can claim to be both in people's pockets and in the sky above their heads; it knows where they spent their holidays, which surgery they attend and what interests them.
This, however, according to Eric Schmidt, one of the directors of the company, is still not enough.
What concerns the skeptics who make a comparison with the Orwellian nightmare, they do not see the substance of web 2.0.
Eric Schmidt recently told the Financial Times that Google’s main purpose is to collect as much personal data as its users and that’s necessary to stick to the original goal of Google’s founders it was: “Let’s include all the information in the world in a system!”.
According to the director of Google interviewed the company, after 10 years of groundbreaking work, it's still at the beginning that it can handle all the information.
When asked what’s going to happen in five years’ time Schmidt replied: He’d like if Google knew how to answer questions like “what do I do tomorrow?” or “what’s the right job for me?”, but the software doesn’t know how to answer seemingly simple questions because they don’t know enough about users.
Not all Internet users are aware that during their searches on the internet exactly the words searched are recorded, from which IP address they are searched and on which page the interested parties stop after the search.
Through the Personalized Search launched two years ago users e.g. they can store their browsing history, they can relate to what they have searched and clicked, these then in the long run will generate much better tailor-made results; on this same logic is based Recommendations with which the system offers us products and services according to our preferences declared and recorded previously.
What happens if my data is used against me, if they look at what I'm dealing with? - they ask users worried about Google's data monopoly.
In most cases the counter-reactions of Internet users are provoked by the scare felt for the dimension of the potential power represented by the company, and not for the guilt, for the fear of punishment, for the desire for freedom or even for rebellion in power.
However, a fact is confirmed, namely that all the expressions searched are not only recorded but in justified cases can also be provided to third parties, in such legal cases nothing defends the personal rights of Internet users.
The most striking example of this in recent times has perhaps been the coordinated action against paedophiles in Brazil, in which Google has forwarded to the Brazilian government the information that made it possible to identify pedophiles from the Orkut community page maintained by Google.
Internet users concerned about personal rights instead sighed for relief in May when the company - in agreement with the EFF e-legal guardianship - successfully proved the truth in court against the FBI: even later you can't force Google with any point of the constitution to provide any information secretly requested by the FBI.
(At the same time the federal government has successfully snatched almost 50,000 web addresses and a thousand search expressions obtained in reference to the confirmation of the child protection law passed years before, but still failed to obtain the search lists of individual users and the entire list of web pages and keywords dating back to two months.)
Google’s judgment is therefore rather contradictory: those who fear to provide their personal data lash Google because they believe that their intimate sphere and private life is affected, starting to use its services and rising with the rebellion due to multinationals that stand out for the conservative image.
Meanwhile, the American geek culture and the proliferation of enthusiasts, activists and computer users appeared in many countries see in them the future generation of the great companies, the "cool corporation", as opposed to their rival, Microsoft liquidated as conqueror of the world because of Windows, although both fight for the same goal.
This can happen because Google, unlike Microsoft, does not ask its users for an identification code, does not ask for money for its services, the source code always kept updated and the package of programs is much more accessible, youthful and cool than the official practice of the software giant.
But it can also happen because geeks see exactly the problematic relationship between personal rights and the internet, which implies precisely one of the keywords repeated most often in recent years, web 2.0.
Web 2.0 is talking about collaboration, intentionally leaving traces behind us and exploitinging the communicative channels available to us and while the size of these changes for each individual, the substance is the same: showing ourselves.
An average young person can create profiles in multiple community pages, from Facebook to Xanga, can connect them with other services such as Twitter or last.fm that lists his musical tastes, all of these can insert them into systems such as FriendFeed or Blip.fm, he can also write blogs on which their latest photo galleries automatically appear stored on Flickr or on Picasa.
Meanwhile, we read of numerous exemplary cases from which it is clear that the girl, seeing the series of photos taken in the morning from the common circle of friends, learns easily with those who have spent the night her treacherous boyfriend or a company can fire an employee because he wrote a sincere opinion on the company summit: one thing is sure, the concept of private life has now completely reconsidered.
Those who live an on-line life make sure to show themselves and so we can formulate the fundamental thesis behind the behavior of young people.
Those who want to hide something or just want to avoid the monopoly of Google can do it (starting, for example, with Scroogle using the Google search system but at the same time does not voluntarily leave traces).
There are not a few people who do not purposely register with community pages so as not to be found, Google's online applications, from text processing to image management and news collection, can be replaced with alternative versions not related to huge server parks and that do not make up our best profile so far.
The public doesn’t even secretly expect the moment when Google pulls its claws out.
Currently the company with its 156 billion dollars of value is worth more than IBM (140 billion dollars), Intel (124 billion dollars) or Coca-Cola (110 billion dollars), AdWords/AdSense advertising spaces threaten the revenues of advertising agencies, the services introduced in record time instead cause major malaises not only to Microsoft but also to numerous software development companies.
However, critical observers along with all this find alarming points: for example, the company’s topicals completely rejected the protest of the employees after doubling the company’s kindergarten line, considered the repeated daily catering of employees too expensive, but all this diminishes before the fact that in 2006 Google bowed the head to the Chinese government and pursuant to their agreement the company will filter all the results that offend the good taste
An internal rule of the company seems not to advertise a new service in advance but only when it will be usable, but one thing is certain, that the foreseeable problems of the coming months and years will move the world in one point: while some believe that the project of scanning and archivinging books and magazines of Google will assume such proportions that the company will soon be in front of the American Supreme Court, others are afraid of the Health system, in fact in this we can
The fact that Google is building a genetic database can offer good material to the next generation of science fiction writers, the Scroogled tale of the famous Cory Doctorow describes the future with Orwellian accents, in which a man with Google data can be alarmingly approached.
Two minors helped Ózd Ózd's double killer make the tracks disappear
The police questioned two minors about Ózd's double murder, well-foundedly suspected of having helped to make the traces of the crime disappear - the spokesman of the Borsod region's Police Headquarters said on Tuesday.
Young people recognized the offence charged to them, they are currently accused of aiding.
The two minors, two girls according to MTI's information, defend themselves on the loose.
Aiding is punishable for up to 5 years imprisonment.
The suspect in the double murder of Ózd, a 23-year-old local man, on Sunday was placed in pre-trial detention.
He's submissed for multiple premeditated murders committed for profit.
The investigation showed that the young twenty-three-year-old had big debt to a 53-year-old man.
The suspect did not want to pay the debt, for this reason last Wednesday he lured his creditor into an Ózd construction site where he and his 9-year-old daughter drenched.
According to MTI's information, the young man had prepared to commit the crime, a signal is that he committed the murder with a hidden weapon on the spot.
Police are reviewing the weapon and the circumstances of use with the involvement of an expert.
The Warsaw Stock Exchange made an offer to buy the Prague Stock Exchange
The Warsaw Stock Exchange offered 200 million euros, about 5 billion Czech crowns, for the purchase of 100% of the share list of the Prague Stock Exchange.
Ludwik Sobolewski, president of the Warsaw Stock Exchange, confirmed the offer put forward by the Poles despite the Czechs previously excluding the Warsaw Stock Exchange as a possible buyer of the Prague Stock Exchange.
The reason for the exclusion is that the Warsaw Stock Exchange is owned by the state.
99% of the shares are in the hands of the Polish state that it intends to sell more than 60% of its securities within a few months.
The Czech financial daily E15 says that the offer of Poles represents the highest figure so far advanced for the Prague Stock Exchange.
According to the Czech press so far, the Vienna Stock Exchange, the Nasdaq Transatlant Stock Exchange, the New York Stock Exchange, the London Stock Exchange and the German Stock Exchange have been offered.
Petr Koblic, director of the Prague Stock Exchange, said last week that among all concerned the Warsaw Stock Exchange would be the worst owner and did not rule out that this could lead in the future to the close of the Prague Stock Exchange.
Market experts estimate the value of the Prague Stock Exchange at 5-10 billion wreaths.
According to the shareholder agreement, almost 90% of the securities would be sold.
The largest owner of the Prague Stock Exchange is Patria Finance with a share of almost 25%, followed by Česká Spořitelna with 14.7% and Tiger Holding Four S. a.r.l. with 13.5%
The economic crisis from the point of view of European newspapers
Leading European newspapers in their comments on Tuesday dedicated to the international economic crisis already speak of "a second September 11."
The global financial disaster happened thanks to the American president - he accuses La Repubblica - who was not able to compact his own party behind a hastily embraced law, the purpose of which is to remedy a desperate situation.
Instead of a second Pearl Hairpart, as billionaire superfinancier Warren Buffett put it, it would be more accurate to talk about a second September 11.
Even part of Madrid's press sees the scapegoat in Washington.
According to El Mundo the lack of U.S. political leadership has thrown the world economy to the brink.
The danger of the collapse of the American financial system is growinging more and more.
Bush's plan was not perfect but as a result of the congressional vote there is a danger that Washington will not offer a political means of managing the crisis.
The world's economy is suspended in the air, just when the biggest transformation in the history of capitalism is unfolding.
It is no longer possible to leave out the threat - hints the Tages-Anzeiger of Geneva.
Some states today can still offer a bail-of-the-bait doughnut to stalling financial institutions and with taxpayer money they can save them from bankruptcy.
But if more and more banks collapse as in a domino effect then neither the financial means nor new improvised solutions will be enough.
In the long-term it is necessary for member states of the European Union to overcome their national selfishness and concentrate control over dismembered financial markets.
On Monday alone the weakened financial institutions have obtained more than 100 billion euros - recalls the Viennese Der Standard, according to which the anxieties that even European taxpayers, similar to those of America, will have to put their own portfolio in order to remedy the crisis seem increasingly real.
The EU so far hasn’t bothered much about the crisis that has run through the old continent as a tsunami.
"Saviors" do not have to wave 100 billion euros immediately, however it would be better if they established in which case it is appropriate to touch taxpayers' money to prevent bigger damages.
The intelligent machine mimics the flight of the hornet
What could be the best way to develop anti-collision technologies than to mimic obstacle sensors found in nature?
For example, those of bees.
In the surprising project presented at CEATEC in Japan, hornets have given the initial strategic inspiration to Nissan engineers on how to build the next generation of anti-collision systems.
In collaboration with the University of Tokyo, Nissan has built the biomimetic robot BR23C, a robotic mini-car equipped with the properties of bees, with the ultimate aim of creating a system capable of avoiding all collisions.
Flying the bees form their own personal oval space that really closely resembles our protective shield - he explained to Kazuhiro Doi exhibition, director of PR technologies and research and development.
Important is the role of the view of the bee, capable of a view of 300 degrees in each direction.
Engineers drew on the telemetric laser idea to copy the function of the bee's view.
The BR23C detects obstacles up to two meters apart within a radius of 180 degrees, calculates the distance and sends a signal to the on-board microprocessor that immediately transforms it into a command to avoid collision.
In a fraction of a second the robot perceives the obstacle and then, mimicking the movements of the bee, immediately changes direction by turning the wheels of the measure necessary to avoid the accident.
This avoidance maneuver, in a different way from the current system, takes place in a completely instinctive way.
“If that wasn’t the case, the robot car wouldn’t be able to react quickly to avoid collisions,” said Toshiyuki Andoh, director of the Mobility Laboratory and the auto robot project.
It must react instinctively and immediately because this technology corresponds to the damagable and innermost layer of the protective shield, within which the collision is considered currently inevitable.
The whole process must reflect what a bee does to avoid other bees.
This process must take place in the blink of an eye.
But unlike bees the deviation cannot go up or down or diagonally, it can only be two-dimensional and in the steering direction of the wheels.
In this way, instead of the infinite possibilities available to bees to avoid the other bees, the robot, as a possibility available, can rotate on itself, accelerate and slow down.
The BR23C is powered by lithium-polymer batteries, lighter and higher energy density, which means that their service life is higher than lithium-ion batteries.
According to Andoh it is important the instantaneity of the intelligent reaction, not the processing of data or even less the ability to store it.
This robot has to process a few inputs in seconds and react.
You don’t need to memorize them and react with intuitive logic based on the previous maneuvering data, so it only uses a memory of a few tens of Kb.
You don't need a gigabyte or megabyte memory.
This is the first step towards the development of technology for the immediate avoidance of collisions that can be installed on production vehicles within 10 years.
Additional application possibilities can be represented by the unification with the GPS satellite navigation system that avoiding the obstacles present on the road leads to its destination.
They also found the name to the very expensive child
The written version of the US$700 billion economic bailout law has been drafted for the purchase of disproportionate credits, to be applied in several parts.
Half of the sum is spent in the first step.
In the new agreements that lasted until midnight on Saturday according to East Coast schedule, congress leaders decided on the $700-billion bailout plan after a day-lasting debate - Reuters reported.
Nancy Pelosi, Democratic president of the U.S. House of Representations, said.
Both presidential candidates, Barack Obama and John McCain, supported the compromise of the plan.
Treasury Minister Henry Paulson talking about the result of the compromise said: the program will work and be effective, but there is still a lot to do. According to Pelosi it is not about the rescue of Wall Street but about the reversal of the economic chariot.
The written version of the plan was made public before the opening of Asian markets at 8 a.m. according to Washington time (2 a.m
On Monday, the House of Representatives will first vote on the draft Economic Stabilisation Act of 2008 concerning the program, after it will be up to the Senate.
President George W. Bush spoke Saturday night with Pelosi, then greeted with joy at the White House the news of the deal.
We are pleased with the progress achieved tonight and greatly appreciate the efforts of the two parties to stabilise our financial markets and defend the economy - said Tony Fratto White House spokeswoman.
The program, however, differs from the original proposal of the Bush administration, which stipulated that the US$700 billion would all be spent on the purchase of securities with disproportionated credits behind them.
Now as a first step it will be possible to spend at most half of the sum of 700 billion dollars, 250 billion immediately after Monday's vote of Congress, with these the Ministry of the Treasury will buy securities with disproportionated credits, of the fate of the remaining 100 billion dollars will decide the president.
A special college will monitor the program in which the chairman of the Federal Reserve also takes part.
Spending the other half of 700 billion depends on a new congressional decree.
According to the law, the Ministry of the Treasury can apply a combined tactic: it can at the same time buy credits, mortgages and securities with mortgages and other types of "poisoned" credits.
The purchase can take place through rods and in a direct way - they have announced experts of the ministry.
The legal rule makes it possible for interested companies to take part in an insurance program where the Ministry of the Treasury guarantees the means that give concern, for which the companies will pay an adequate price to the likely damages.
Henrz Paulson said: The law gives the necessary means for 700 billion dollars to arrive for the urgent needs of America's monetary system, whether it's the purchase of the wrong financial means, or their insurance or to avoid the risk of the collapse of a large financial institution.
We will take all the necessary steps to make us find ourselves ready to introduce the law just signed - he added.
The directors of banks in crisis and waiting for state aid instead think that the government will limit their exit, defined by overseas slang simply as golden parachutes.
Payment restrictions will only happen in some cases, plus the limitations depend on how the Ministry of the Treasury will offer help.
In essence, the limitation means that if the ministry buys under an aucitation, it will impose a surcharge on the golden parachute, if they do not retire the deceased bank manager.
At the time of the disproportionated credit purchase, the government will intervene aggressively to ensure that the directors’ money is not exaggerated.
To this must be added that the already existing contracts of the golden parachute will not change, the limitation is valid only for new ones to incentivise participation in the programme.
This is the first case in American history of the entry into force of a legal limitation concerning the pay of the general administrators - commented the measure Democrat Barney Frank chairman of the committee responsible for the financial services of the House of Representatives.
Moreover, the state has the right to appropriate a share of the banks participating in the program, decreasing the risk of taxpayers, since in the end they can also gain if the economy, and together with it the saved bank, will get back on their feet.
The proposed law stipulates that over the next five years the president must submit to Congress a proposal that stipulates how to return to taxpayers the expected losses arising from the financial sector.
Of course on both sides there were those who expressed their doubts about the project.
That's not what U.S. citizens have asked us for, not to defend taxpayers' dollars - said California Republican Congressman Darrell Issa.
His Ohio Democratic colleague Mary Kaptur believes that the measures have been taken by the sames who are responsible for the collapse of Wall Street.
Industry prices continue to rise
Industrial domestic sales prices in August 2008 increased by 0.1% compared to the previous month, by 12.9% compared to August 2007 - informs the Central Institute of Hungarian Statistics.
Industry export sales prices compared to July 2008 were 1.1% higher, compared to the previous year fell by 3.9%
As a result of the change in domestic pricing and export, industrial production costs were 0.7% higher compared to the previous month and 3.2% compared to a year.
Industrial domestic sales prices compared to the previous month only increased by 0.1%.
Domestic sales prices in the machining sector also rose to a minimal extent, 0.1%, compared to July.
The biggest increase in prices in August 2008 occurred in the leather and shoe processing sector (2.8%), especially bags and belts with 4.6% and shoe production with 2.5% monthly increase.
The high level of the price of plastic and chemical raw materials caused a significant monthly increase (1.6%) in the products and chemicals sector.
A decrease in prices occurred in the oil processing sector (1.4%), in the production of electrical instruments and machinery (0.9%) and to a minimal extent in woodworking (0.1%)
The prices of paper production, publishing and typographic activities have not changed.
In the other sectors of the processing industry there was an increase in prices between 0.2 and 0.9%.
In the electricity, gas, steam and water supply sector, prices rose in August by 0.3% in domestic industrial sales, primarily as a result of the dominant effect of the increase in the price of gas carried out by the authorities from 1 July.
Compared in 12 months, considering the national sales of the sectors of the processing industry, the biggest increases were in the processing of petroleum products (32.4%).
In the manufacturing of metalworking products, the price increase was 13.2%, 11% in the production of food, beverages and tobacconity, representing a decisive weight.
A decrease in prices occurred in the textile sector (2.5%) and in the fields of the production of electrical instruments and machinery (0.3%).
In the other sectors of the processing industry, the pace of price increases ranged from 0.6% to 9.1%.
In total in the processing industry in the past year prices rose by 10.2%.
Electricity, gas, steam and water replenishing prices exceeded 18.7 per cent those of last year.
In domestic sales among the target groups of the sectors, the largest increase in monthly prices occurred in August in sectors producing investment goods (0.7%).
In sectors producing consumer articles, prices grew by 0.3%, in the energy and subsequent utilisation sectors, prices did not change compared to those of the previous month.
In August 2008, compared to the same month last year, the increase in larger prices was observed in the energy and follow-us sector (16.8%).
In sectors producing consumer articles the increase was 6.8%, for investment goods a decrease of 1.4% was measured.
Industrial export sales prices calculated in guilders grew in August by 1.1% compared to the previous month, compared in 12 months decreased by 3.9%.
The Hungarian guilder within a month weakened against the euro by 1.7%, against the 7.1% dollar, compared to August 2007 strengthened by 7.5% against the euro and 15.9% against the dollar.
Next year in the United States without a visa?
Kinga Göncz sees the possibility that around January 12 the American-Hungarian visa-waiver agreement could come into force - the foreign minister spoke about this in Chicago with the local Hungarian representatives.
“Now it all depends on the fact overseas headquarters, the electronic entry and control system is built in time.”
However, he drew attention to the fact that in the new system there will no longer be an entry permit, however before departure tourists will have to be registered.
The head of Hungarian diplomacy will continue negotiations in Washington.
He will meet with Foreign Minister Condoleeza Rice, to whom he will hand over the Medal of the Cross of the Order of the Hungarian Republic with the Star and will hold talks with foreign policy advisers to Democratic candidate Barack Obama and Republican John McCain.
Barack Obama and John McCain also sided in favour of the banks' bail-out plan
Barack Obama and John McCain, candidates for the presidency of the two big American parties, on Sunday guaranteed their support for the 700-billion-dollar government plan for bailing the banks, but there are politicians reclaiming the resignation of the Treasury minister.
Representatives of Congress and the administration after marathon negotiations lasted for days on Sunday night announced that they had concluded an agreement in principle on the bailout of the banks.
Wall Street's crisis and the financial package of juncting the frozen credit market have become the central issue of the presidential campaign.
Obama and McCain want to show themselves better suited than their rival to getting the country out of the economic crisis.
According to the most recent polls with the foreground of economic issues Obama has again regained some advantage, while the advantage of McCain, obtained after the Republican convention with Sarah Palin's vocacial appointment, has dispersed.
Obama in a speech in Detroit expressed hope that Congress will swiftly approve the 700-billion-dollar plan law.
He pointed out that the proposal contains those consumer-protection steps that have also been endorsed by him.
"Now we must act responsibly (...), the danger threatens your work, your savings, all industrial sectors, complete economic stability", he said in front of a crowd of 35,000 people.
He didn't let go of defining McCain's economic ideas outdated.
McCain in an interview with the ABC explained that "everyone we're going to send this situation down with difficulty (...) but doing nothing is not a viable road."
The Republican senator reported that in the proposal there is the creation of a control committee of the Minister of Finance and the limitation of the good exits of the top managers, things on which he was the one to insist.
Obama told CBS that McCain did not play a meriting role in the agreements, but he was in daily contact with the minister.
President Nancy Pelosi on Sunday reported that the House of Representatives is likely to vote on the bill on Monday.
Press review of the German-speaking world
The pole of the right is intact, only within the pole have voters become more self-aware - this is how Der Spiegel analyzes the weak result of the CSU in Bavaria.
Haider's party is ready to rule Austria to prevent the red-to-black coalition.
Der Spiegel (spiegel.de)
Franz Walter, in the internet edition of the left-wing German magazine, in the article The Revolution of Bavaria devours one's parents analyzes the causes of the dramatic defeat of the CSU.
According to the well-known politologist CSU erosion began long before the sad Beckstein-Huber couple took office.
The Christian-Socials have remodernized Bavaria for decades and just now these modern citizens are moving away from them.
At the same time, the change of epoch is a problem for the whole of Christian democracy, so is Angela Merker.
The defeat was not a great surprise for the CSU, but on Sunday ended a process that exactly ten years ago had been achievable: the indisputable hegemony of a party-state ended.
Already 10 years ago, in 1998, the CSU had plummeted to 47.7%, for the first time below the 50% threshold since the 1950s.
This was repeated three years ago at the Bundestag elections in 2005 still in the period of the much-mentioned Stoiber.
The cause of this is the modernization carried out accordingly in the region, which devours parents.
Sociologists predicted it as early as 40 years ago, but they made a lot of mistake. But secularization, the results of education, the establishment of the Germans of the East and the North in the developed Bavarian region could not remain without consequences on the loyalty of voters to the CSU.
Modernisation goes hand in hand with increasing possibilities and the political consequences are evident.
The pole of the right is intact, only within the pole voters have become more self-aware, critical, obstinate.
It is also surprising that in recent weeks the classic bourgeois groups have moved away from the CSU: freelancers, artisans, graduates and graduates.
Here support for the CSU fell below 40%.
Die Presse (diepresse.com)
The Viennese Conservative newspaper "Who with whom?" In the article "Party presidents are stuck in coalition issues" deals with the confusing Austrian domestic political situation created following the elections.
SPÖ intends to deal with ÖVP.
ÖVP prohibits SPÖ's intervention in her internal affairs.
The BZÖ is open to negotiations in all directions, the greens still do not want to discuss coalition.
The elections have been made, now everything revolves around the coalition; in the discussion broadcast by public TV the leaders of the parties have just seen the cards.
Werner Faymann president of SPÖ confirmed that he wanted to deal with ÖVP.
SPÖ wants to show that collaboration between the two parties is possible, in some regions and social partnership already exists.
Faymann left open the question of whether he can imagine a coalition with Wilhelm Molterer, ÖVP's current president, as vice-chancellor.
As he stated, the Schüssel-Molterer course was in any case rejected.
Repetition of this direction is not considered.
Molterer reacted cautiously to red offers.
As he said, the collaboration will surely not succeed if Faymann attempts to meddle into ÖVP’s internal affairs.
If his party chooses the opposition or enters the coalition it's still an open thing in the party, the issues need to be discussed in any case within the ÖVP.
Jörg Haider leader of the BZÖ repeated that he was open in all directions.
"To prevent the Red-Black coalition we are ready to take responsibility for governing," Haider said.
Járóka: The vicious cycle of poverty and segregation must be broken
Today in Paris began the European Equal Opportunities Summit, organized this year by France with the rotating presidency of the European Union.
Lívia Járóka, Fidesz deputy of the European People's Party in the message addressed to the plenum as the main speaker of the congress drew attention to school segregation because it is the most serious legal violation that affects Roma children and moreover the most serious hurdle for their claim.
Xavier Bertrand French Minister for Employment and Social Affairs, on behalf of the French Presidency, and European Commissioner Vladimír Špidla responsible for Employment, Social Affairs and Equal Opportunities opened the conference at the highest level.
Among those invited to the conference were governments, European social partners and representatives of the major European civil and rights-defensing organisations in the field of equal opportunities.
Járóka, according to the press office of the parliamentary group Fidesz PE, recalled, in addition to the discrimination in recruitment, that the Roma are ousted from the labour market in the first place because of their low schooling.
"Most young Romas do not have a middle-level educational qualification and as a result of bad-quality elementary education only a paltry number of them can achieve a higher degree."
Járóka also emphasised, as a fundamental human right, the key role of education in asserting human rights.
Investing in the education of Roma children can mean the strengthening of the economy of member states of the European Union.
According to the MP it is necessary to make aware in the wider scope between Roma and non-Roma these links.
In fact, the sooner a Roma starts school, the greater its chances of becoming a productive member of the labor market; through taxes paid for income and consumption contribute to the nation’s budget.
"The right of European citizenship of the Roma must guarantee their right to quality education and the elimination of low-level and segregated schools," Lívia Járóka stressed.
According to the congresswoman, the European Union's union of purpose and action is needed to accelerate the processes of desegregation of schools and for this reason it is of fundamental importance to respect for existing and planned anti-discrimination directives.
"The vicious cycle of poverty, segregation and unemployment must be interrupted, we must give Roma children the opportunities afforded by the democratic space so that they can contribute as full citizens of the future of Europe," Járóka asked.
In the name of the "European Year of Equal Opportunities" to be guaranteed for all, the purpose of the European Equal Opportunities Summit organized every year, from last year's Berlin meeting is to ensure a single forum to favor the objectives of anti-discrimination and equal opportunities set at state and European level.
CSU: Pity for a transitional period
At the end of October we will decide the fate of the awkward couple Huber-Beckstein.
Despite the ugly electoral defeat have not yet fallen from the heads, at the tops of the Bavarian CSU the discussion of personal issues was postponed for the moment.
Party president Erwin Huber and regional governor Günther Beckstein still remain in his place, the question is how long pity lasts.
Of the fate of the tandem that has suffered a catastrophic loss of votes of 17% will decide the party's extraordinary congress in the last weekend of October.
The two highly criticized politicians said in any case that they were not attached with all their strength to the chair.
They tried to soften the angry members of the parliamentary group and the grassroots that a day later the "Bavarian tsunami" demanded the personal consequences for the loss of an absolute majority.
5 years ago, under the leadership of Edmund Stoiber then at the peak of his career, the Christian-Social Union gained 60.7%, now after the coup against Stoiber have fallen to 43.4%.
CSU for 46 years has been an "absolute monarch" in Bavaria which gives 18% of Germany's GDP.
However, the Conservatives in the southern region of fundamental importance for the Hungarian economy always matter, in the future they will be forced to join forces.
As the first signal of the reorganization within the party, the Vice-President and Federal Minister of Agriculture Horst Seehofer, considered possible heir of Huber, obtains a place in the narrow exploratory group that will begin negotiations with FDP liberals and Free Electors exponent right-wing values for an eventual government coalition in Munich.
For a strange twist of fate in the list of Free Electors enters the legislature that Gabriele Pauli whose attitude against Stoiber had been exploited as coup opportunities by the Huber-Beckstein couple and then treated without regard to the woman with great ambitions.
CSU executives are now concentrating all of their forces to stabilize Beckstein somehow.
Stoiber, who so far had been plotting in the shadows. The former regional governor and former CSU president called September 28 the bittest day of his political career.
On Monday Huber issued some statements to the file towards the CSU, according to which they deserved more support from the brother party.
Other CSU politicians interpreted defeat as a warning to the CDU and Angela Merkel.
Merkel will not be able to be chancellor after the Bundestag elections scheduled for September 2009 if the CSU, which played a decisive role in the 2005 national victory, will not be strengthered with common forces.
According to Merkel, which does not hide the disappointment over the fiasco, CSU's ground loss will not adversely affect Berlin's grand coalition.
What is certainly relative: it will not be easy to manage the injured CSU, but the crisis of the Social Democrats still lasts.
The SPD achieved its lowest result since 1946 in Bavaria with 18.6%.
Lukashenko-opposition: 110-0
Not even an opposition representative entered the Belarusian Parliament after Sunday's election.
According to Russian observers it is the sign of the well-being and stability ensured in the country led by Alexander Lukashenko, according to the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe (OSCE) instead it is explained by the frauds committed in the vote count.
The OSCE, which sent 450 observers from 43 countries, did not recognise elections.
Although the legislation of 110 people in a country of 10 million inhabitants has been without opposition for more than a decade, before yesterday's elections the opposition gathered under the United Democratic Forces (ODS) seriously hoped that Lukashenko, with the presidential term renewed for the fourth time following the constitutional amendment to the 2006 elections held in disputed circumstances, "granted" a few seats in Parliament to representatives of the opposition.
The ODS has thus renounced a boycott previously assessed.
Behind the opposition's expectations was that Belarus, increasingly economically dependent on Russia, which called for credit to reduce the import of increasingly expensive Russian natural gas and agreed to give the Russians the pipelines holding the Beltransgaz crossing the country, intended to improve the relationship with the EU where Lukashenko and other high-ranking politicians cannot enter for the Belarusian political situation considered undemocratic.
It referred to a détente that the OSCE by assessing the election campaign put the emphasis on positive movements and not on shortcomings, such as the prohibition to show up for a third of opposition candidates.
Monday's OSCE report, however, also criticized the campaign, during which opposition candidates in practice could not appear in the press, and in particular that the opposition could not be present at vote counting.
Despite this, according to the opposition an improvement in the political climate is perceptible: political prisoners have been released, Minsk is also ready for a small confrontation with Moscow continuing to deny the recognition of South Ossetia and Abkhazia from Georgia, even if Russia, with which Belarus forms a federal state on paper, considers the two countries independent for a month.
“We’ve taken two steps forward and one step back, but what’s most important is that the dialogue between Belarus and the EU has begun,” Milinkevich told our newspaper.
According to the 2006 opposition presidential candidate of the 2006 election it is desirable that it remains despite the fact that "the power has been afraid to bring even a single opponent into a weightless Parliament."
The opposition does not foresee protest actions for the results but rather tries to get voters the message of the need for economic reforms.
Obviously we have many problems, we have to print opposition newspapers in Russia, for distribution the local authorities raise difficulties, but even so I see that more and more people support change, even among the elderly - said Milinkevich referring to the fact that not even the most officially helped class, that of pensioners, deems the Lukashenko regime adequate, which considers its main result the creation of social security.
Due to the indebtedness of the country and the rise of the economic deficit it is only a matter of time the disintegration of social security "reminiscent of burial silence" - Milinkevich believes.
As opposed to the opposition politician, Russian observers have judged the elections democratic.
Sergei Lebedev, president of the Independent States Community, told the Russian news agency RIA Novosti: "men believe not in words but in deeds," so the lack of opposition is nothing but the proof of maturity of Belarusian voters.
Brunetta: "From now on breakfast before work"
It has never happened in the history of Palazzo Chigi that there were revolving doors for the entry and exit of employees.
But from today this new measure against the cunning, against those who stamp and then go to have a coffee at the bar during working hours.
The novelty anti-slacker was presented today by the Minister of Public Administration, Renato Brunetta, and the Secretary General of the Presidency of the Council, Mauro Masi.
"Revolving doors - says Brunetta - are a tool to work better, with more productivity, more presences and more services," the minister said.
“Now opens a new season of accountability and transparency.
This is reformism," Brunetta points out again.
With Palazzo Chigi the installation is completed in all the palaces of the Presidency of the Council.
For Masi "the choice of revolving doors is also important from the symbolic point of view".
There were no resistance from employees, assures the general secretary: "It was a process shared by the workers and there was the collaboration of everyone."
In short, no more breakfast during working hours; no more coffee and croissant.
Brunetta's words, which suggests, smiling, "a beautiful cappuccino at 7 a.m. is a holy hand."
During a conference at the Higher School of Public Administration
of Rome, the minister then announces that "in three months the absences due to illness of civil servants have been reduced by 50%, i.e. 60,000 more people at work in a year".
And he adds that it is necessary to make Public Administration more efficient
Italian, "whose wage dynamics - explains the minister - in the last 8-10 years have been double compared to the private sector and well above inflation rates."
Meanwhile, thousands of civil servants have come together to challenge the rules of the Brunetta decree adhering to the appeal supported by Codacons.
"While the Minister of Public Function flaunts today numbers about the least absenteeism at work due to disease - writes the association of consumers - the people of 'industrious slackers' are constantly growing.
In fact, there are thousands in Italy correct and productive public administration employees who have come together to declare war on the illegitimate rules contained in the decree."
In particular, the TAR will contest the exclusion of additional economic treatments for infirmity dependent on cause of service; the extension of the availability time slots of the sick worker, within which are carried out the check-ups, and other aspects contained in the new provisions desired by the minister.
Work accidents, Napolitano: "Crucial prevention"
Every day, 2,500 accidents occur at work in Italy, 3 people die and 27 remain permanently disabled.
For 2007, Inail data confirmed that there were about 1,200 white deaths.
Figures that testify to the seriousness of a phenomenon that remains one of the main causes of death and causes almost twice as many deaths as murders.
To draw the attention of institutions, social forces and the media to this dramatic issue, today the Anmil celebrates in all the provinces of Italy the 58th National Day for the victims of accidents at work, institutionalized on the second Sunday of October.
"The worrying data released by Anmil and the same tragic chronicles of these days confirm how crucial is the issue of prevention at work", says the President of the Republic Giorgio Napolitano, in the message sent to the President of Anmil, National Mutilated and Invalid Labour Association, Pietro Mercandelli.
The Head of State expresses his "living appreciation for the constant associative commitment in favor of prevention in the workplace, the protection of injured workers, the assistance of the families of the victims and the awareness of public opinion".
"Of course, an indignant 'enough' has arisen, sincerely shared, in the face of tragedies that, because of their size, arouse the clamor of the media and the involvement of public opinion, but the daily reality re-proposes dramatic, even repetitive cases in their dynamics."
Napolitano does not hide that "there is undoubtedly also a problem of resources: it is decisive to invest in training and information.
Particular significance takes on the numerous initiatives promoted in the school sector for a more direct awareness by young people entering the world of work.
It is necessary to keep alive the attention to the phenomenon, not to give up in the alarm about its social gravity, to apply and to improve legislative rules.
For his part, the Speaker of the Chamber Gianfranco Fini stressed "how the phenomenon of white deaths constitutes an absolute social emergency, which offends the conscience of each of us and imposes on all the institutions a decisive commitment aimed at urgently ending it."
"Data on accidents at work seem to signal a slight positive trend, but this does not mean that the number of white deaths is still impressive," Anmil President Pietro Mercandelli remarked.
"We wouldn't, in fact, want this "trend to the down" to result in less attention, because it's precisely when the focus eases that the tragedy erupts.
"We believe that at a general level there is still too little to combat the phenomenon of accidents and the contrast can be exercised through multiple actions.
They are necessary, in our opinion - it has emphasized Mercandelli - greater controls and inspections, but also a stronger intervention on the training of the workers, and this starting from the world of the school".
"We need a further effort - he added - with regard to the communication and awareness of public opinion.
But we need, above all, a greater sense of responsibility on the part of employers towards a phenomenon that represents a social emergency."
Mercandelli then denounced "the progressive deterioration of the levels of indemnity protection planned for the victims of work" and the "welfare drift towards which the system is pushing in recent years."
"The goal, therefore, is to return to the worker the insurance guarantee - he added - because the fate of those injured does not have to depend on spontaneism or donation from time to time disposed by the legislator."
Mp3 readers under indictment, put hearing at risk
Listening to loud music for an extended period of time using portable music devices such as Mp3 players can cause permanent hearing injuries.
This is what the European Commission reports, which in a statement issued in Brussels cites the opinion of the Scientific Committee on Emerging and Newly Identified Health Risks (Cerseri) of the EU, published today.
"From this scientific opinion it emerges that 5-10% of those who, for a period of at least 5 years, listen to music with portable music devices weekly for more than an hour a day holding the high-volume device risk a permanent loss of hearing ability."
"I'm concerned that such a large number of young people who frequently use portable music devices and mobile phones at high acoustic levels can unconsciously damage their hearing irreparably," says Meglena Kuneva.
"From the scientific findings - continues the EU - a clear risk emerges and we must react quickly.
What matters most, we need to sensitize consumers and make this information public.
We must also re-examine existing control systems, in the light of this scientific opinion, to ensure that they are fully effective and keep pace with the new technology."
Brussels had asked the Independent Scientific Committee to examine the problem, given the great use of portable musical devices and the increase in the number of young people exposed to the noise they gave rise.
“Scientists confirm that there is cause for concern and the European Commission will now examine together with Member States and stakeholders what measures could eventually be taken to better protect children and adolescents from exposure to noise from portable music devices and related equipment.”
Currently, there is already a European safety standard that limits the noise level of portable musical equipment to 100 dB, but concerns about hearing damage that can result from excessive exposure to such noise sources are growing.
It is possible to greatly limit this damage by resorting to solutions such as the reduction of levels and duration of noise exposure.
In its opinion, the EU Scientific Committee points out that users of portable music equipment if they listen at high volume (more than 89 decibels) music for only 5 hours per week exceed the limits currently in force relating to the maximum noise allowed in the workplace.
Those who listen to music in such conditions for more prolonged periods risk a permanent loss of hearing ability after a period of 5 years.
This would affect 5-10% of users of such devices and their number can be quantified between 2.5 and 10 million people in the EU.
Rome, Alemanno kick-start the Commission for the future of the capital
Today it starts, in March it closes, in June the sums will be drawn.
This is the calendar set by the Capitol for the works of the "Commission for the Future of Roma Capitale", which settled this morning in its new headquarters behind the Baths of Caracalla.
In six months, President Antonio Marzano and the other 49 members of the Commission will develop the guidelines for Rome.
Once closed in March, the Board and the City Council will evaluate analyses and projects, so as to be able to present in June the Strategic Plan for Rome with the "official stamp" of the Capitol and its mayor Gianni Alemanno.
"The Commission is absolutely independent and there are represented all political, social and cultural ideas - Alemanno emphasizes - It will have to provide proposals for an idea of development in Rome of which the political decision-makers, who remain the Council and the City Council, will then be able to avail themselves.
There is no superposition of roles but only the desire to have a place to draw up a great project for Rome.
In March he will finish his work and then it will dissolve, because this experience must have a precise time limit.
In June - the mayor announces - we will present the strategic plan using the work done by the Commission for the future of Rome Capital."
To those who accuse the Commission of uselessness, since its opinions will not be binding, Alemanno replies: "Everything depends on the work that the Commission will be able to do and the quality of its proposals.
If there will be quality, as well as in France with the Attali Commission and as I am safe in Rome, its contribution will be fundamental.
Too often the policy is crushed on the management of the newspaper and on the emergencies that from time to time.
On the contrary, we want to look longer and farther, in a deeper perspective, working seriously on the development of Rome and its future."
Moreover, the mayor of Rome points out that "this Commission is neither a useless entity nor a bandwagon: it is absolutely gratuitous, it will not cost a euro to the Roman citizens and it will serve to reason and to involve the best intelligences that the Capital expresses."
For Alemanno, in fact, "it is necessary to have a design of strategic reference, not closed or immovable, to allow our city to make an authentic leap, which he absolutely needs, thinking with unified spirit to its future".
A future that, the mayor of Rome specifies "will not be limited only to the current municipal boundaries but will look far beyond" not only in the perspective of the future metropolitan city and its legal status as Capital but "also in view of the role that Rome can and must play as Capital of Europe and the Mediterranean".
Obama? The first anti-American president. Here’s why
He wrote a well-known American politician who wants us to pass as independent: "Barack Obama will be the second anti-American president.
The first was Jimmy Carter, which with his decisions decimated the U.S. economy and had relations (as he does even today as a private citizen) with the enemy Islam."
Attacks against Obama are increasing just as the date of the election is approaching.
She wrote a Miami lady in a web: "This person is full of ambitions.
“Obama has a big nerve: he believes he’s going to win the tightest position of a nation that’s had two governors and five black senators.”
They're actually attacks that don't say much; they're attacks clearly of a racial nature and they don't respond to voters' question: Will he be a good president? Then there are the polls; they all say Obama will win and with a good detachment.
Also in this case there are those who speak with the heart but not with the brain.
A Mississippi newspaper, in the bowels of the southern United States, wrote an editorial: once in the booth to vote we know well to whom the American patriot will give his vote.
It comes out one side of the U.S. mentality kept covered to the last.
"Say whatever you want, then we'll take care where to put the cross, whether on Obama or on McCain."
There are people who swear: if Obama wins, we'll leave America.
Things never said before, which seems almost certain the success of the black candidate.
However, despite all these reasonings, the winning weapon for the Democrats is George Bush.
They asked 1500 people: what were the mistakes made by Bush during his tenure? 89 percent replied: Iraq.
Changes America.
In Vietnam this nation lost 50 thousand soldiers and was sent home kicking in the butt.
In Iraq there are 4500 soldiers who lost their lives and the Americans were not forced to pack.
And yet, Iraq for the majority has been Bush's weak spot.
Those who take the place of George W will have an enormous task to perform.
USA, the economic meltdown is also felt on journalists: in the newspapers it is time for cuts. And many blame the internet, "murderer" of the printed paper.
Five hundred American journalists were interviewed by the PEW Research Center about the state of their profession and mind control regarding the future.
Well, today journalists are almost all pessimistic about their future.
Yet the newsmen did not openly state that they felt less concerned about credibility.
They don’t feel as isolated as they used to.
The problem for U.S. journalists is money, the money.
The 2008 crisis for these journalists is a crisis of destroyed economic model.
In addition, the cuts made in the newsroom, in the room where the editors work to put together the newspapers, is a big problem.
It is a problem that must be solved, according to the journalists interviewed, if you do not want the traditional journalism as the general public understands it substantially.
A clear but complex pessimism pervades the American journalist today.
Six out of ten newspapermen and executive seniors are convinced that the native-wide journalism is taking the wrong path.
In the local field the executive group is at 65%.
Printed against broadcast.
It's not hard for journalists to see where the web goes.
The major publishing houses open a gap towards the internet, for many seen as the "murderer" of the printed paper.
The "economic pressure" that reporters warn has increased to 79%.
The 500 journalists interviewed conceded that they are embracing technology in the hope that it will "do something good" for their journalism.
The biggest split today in American journalism is between who directs the newsrooms - i.e. the editors - and the reporters who work for them.
56% of national executives are sure that their reporters share their values, while locally the percentage drops to 23%.
For some this has to do with "job safety."
Executives who plan to lose their jobs within 3 years are at 47% nationally.
For staff reporters it goes to 30%.
“Safe work” at the staff level (our editors) in America is always on the agenda.
Only 13% of journalists work with a contract.
In an immense nation like the United States, with 300 million inhabitants and thousands of newspapers, magazines, radio, TV and the internet, employers often offer what they want and thus becomes a matter of "take it or leave it", take or leave.
Managers almost always receive large wages, while the editor can earn even 10 thousand dollars a month, in addition to the pension and payment of insurance.
For the holidays, then, the reasoning becomes complicated: tot days after the first year, tot after the third to get to 15 days overall after a certain number of years.
But American journalists, by their admissions, "sick" often throughout the year.
It's all about climate.
UNIPOL: TRIBUNALE ROME, DANILO COPPOLA REMAINS IN GROTTAFERRATA
The president of the second criminal section of the tribunal of Rome, Carmelo Rinaudo, decided to reject the request of the real estate Danilo Coppola who had asked to be able to move from his city, Grottaferrata, to a house in the city of Rome.
Coppola is being investigated for bankruptcy as part of the counter-pact investigation related to Unipol-Bnl climbdown.
In the course of the trial against him, last week, he obtained the release from the domiciliaries transformed into an obligation of residence in Grottaferrata.
His lawyers, Michele Gentiloni and Gaetano Pecorella, had asked that the real estate developer could travel the journey from the provincial town to a house in the capital.
According to the court, however, these movements (to which the PM opposed) would allow him to influence his company and to access the evidence accusing him.
Elites in peril
The phenomenon had already manifested itself a few days ago with the vote of the House of Representatives against the $700-billion bailout launched by the White House: a significant part of Americans was, and is, more interested in punishing Wall Street than saving the economy.
Yesterday’s New York Times confirms that the current crisis is splitting the Republican Party, increasingly divided between its old East Coast soul, moderate, favorable to federal institutions and the business community, and its new soul, instead, conservative, hostile to “Those of Washington” and to the world of finance, strong especially in the Central and Southern States.
The soul, precisely, that became strongly alive with the vote of which I said at the beginning.
However, this just mentioned is just an example of the changes, of the great changes, that the economic earthquake in progress perhaps heralds or already suggests: not only in the United States but throughout the West and perhaps not even here.
Both in the U.S. and in Europe the crisis seems to act as an accelerator of incubating phenomena for some time that in the new clime solidify and finally come to light.
The first of these phenomena is the re-actualization, the extraordinary revival, of the dual category State-sovereignty in relation to a sort of renationalization of the economy.
The crisis, in fact, is a crisis of banking-financial institutions that have, yes, dense ties with abroad, but that first of all see the balance sheets of people and families living in a single country, in a single state.
Which immediately creates a political problem for those who govern it: that is, how to respond to the difficulties and protests of those people and those families who, among other things, are also an electorate.
In short, the crisis appears economically global but politically it is almost exclusively national.
Political internationalism seems essentially out-of-the-game or not having much to say: the glarious evidence is given by the European Union that divided as usual between the different interests and between the different state strategies fails to decide any common policy.
And so it is from the government of every single state that everyone expects interventions, bailout and relaunch plans, new rules, and above all the disbursement of funds: since when it comes to the corners it is only the states that own the economic resources, the mass of financial resources able to try to get things back on track.
And they also possess the necessary means and the legitimacy to use them: two resources of immeasurable value, in certain circumstances, of which probably no market and no international organization will ever dispose to a similar extent.
Moreover, this new emphasis that the crisis places on the state-national element is in perfect harmony with the ever-increasing importance that the most recent developments in the economy tend to attach to a factor very closely linked to that element: territoriality.
Paradoxically, in fact, while we were convinced that we had now entered the realm of the network, the increasingly sophisticated technology, the intangible, while we were convinced that globalized finance was now destined to dominate the world, we suddenly realized that our future will instead have to deal increasingly with those very unintangible things that are water, crops, oil.
All things that, look a little, can certainly be transported from one place to another of the earth but are still absolutely linked to a circumscribed space, to a territory.
So that those who find themselves to be sovereign, certainly have several more cards than those who are not, those who have the misfortune of living in a place without crops, without oil and without water.
I come to the second novelty that presages a fracture.
Who says Territory, State, Government, inevitably says Politics, and therefore Leadership.
In this regard, the economic crisis seems to produce two converging phenomena.
On the one hand the awareness of the objective need for authoritative leadership, the request of someone who knows how to take matters into his own hands.
On the other side a wave of disrepute for existing leaderships, economic species, turned out to be so inadequate and full of shady areas.
And at the same time something even deeper and in a certainous sense: a disreputation, an insuffering, an image of inadequacy, a sense of distance, which tends to involve the entire ruling class in an increasing number of countries in the West.
That is, it seems to make more and more road, in vast sectors of the population, the belief that before their actions are the same ideas of the social elites so far in vogue, their way of feeling and being, their culture in the overall meaning of the term, to have made their own time and to be increasingly foreign to the opinions of majorities.
The demand for leadership, in short, subterraneantly fuels a ramified but mighty movement of delegitimization of the ruling classes and traditional political structures, which manifests itself in the sudden "electoral revolts" or in the sudden turns of the collective moods of the latest and very last times, from the conservative rise in Austria-Bavaria to the collapse of Labour acclaims in Great Britain.
One has the impression that the traditional elites, their parties, their programs, but also their rites, their newspapers, their accredited intellectuals, their formats would say someone, are increasingly struggling to understand, and therefore to represent, what is not taking shape in the deep layers of Western societies and that the economic crisis reinvigorates, enhances, agitates potentially to a great extent.
In the face of all this, talking about a "rebellion of the masses" on the agenda would frankly exaggerate.
But keeping your eyes wide open is certainly not at all.
Super glue for walking on walls
Defeat the laws of gravity thanks to a super glue and in the future walking on the walls like Spiderman.
It's the goal of several American research centers and now a team of four institutes has come very close to us.
With an adhesive of only 2.5 square centimetres, researchers have managed to keep a 100-kilogram body glued on a vertical wall.
STUDY – The U.S. journal Science has published findings by researchers from the University of Dayton, Akron, the Georgia Institute of Technology and the Air Force Research Laboratory.
The material invented by the team of scholars is an evolution of existing ones using a particular carbon structure, called carbon nanotubes.
“The principle of glue can be imagined as a series of tubes arranged like a pile of spaghetti,” explains Zhong Lin Wang of the Georgia Institute of Technology in Atlanta.
The scientists were inspired by the paws of geckos, covered with microscopic hairs that manage to ensure a perfect adherence to the animal.
The tests conducted on different materials, such as glazing, Teflon and sandpaper, have shown that the new adhesive is ten times more potent than the paws of these reptiles and at the same time "detaches easily from the wall", explains Liming Dai, a researcher at the Faculty of Engineering at the University of Dayton.
WALKING ON THE WALLS? -"This material could be useful as a transient adhesive" adds Liming Dai.
Maybe to walk on the walls just like Spiderman? Maybe yes, but the researchers think of a much less fascinating use such as the connection between electronic devices, because carbon nanotubes are conductors of heat and electric current.
Other applications could involve aerospace engineering.
For the moment no superheroes.
THE OTHER SEARCH – The University of Berkeley, California, is also researching a new sticker.
In an article in the journal of the American Chemical Society, Californian scholars presented a microfibre-formed adhesive such as hair self-cleaning from dusts during movement on a surface to always maintain a perfective grip.
Medications: never with fruit juices
Pay attention to grapefruit juice, orange and apple juice.
These drinks, consumed especially in summer because they are thirst-quenching and rich in vitamins, when taken at the same time as some life-saving drugs alter their absorption by amplifying or decreasing depending on the case the effectiveness with even serious consequences for health.
To reiterate the risks of both packaged and fresh fruit juices associated with taking some medicines David Bailey, professor at the University of Western Ontario in London, and from the 236th national meeting of the American Chemical Society underway in Philadelphia launches a new alarm: just a glass of grapefruit, orange and apple juice is enough to reduce the absorption of certain life-saving drugs, nullifying their beneficial effects and putting the health of patients at risk.
Grapefruit juice had already ended up under indictment because it was held responsible for increasing the effects of some antiarrhythmic, antibiotic, anxiolytic, antihistamine and neurological medicines up to toxic levels.
DRUGS AT RISK - The same Italian drug agency had given notice in a news bulletin two years ago alerting doctors and patients not to underestimate the danger from this mix and to pay attention to what you drink if you take drugs such as Diazepam or Methadone.
NOT ONLY GRAPEFRUIT - According to the experts also orange juice and apple contain substances that inhibit drugs.
Along the list of active ingredients inhibited by grapefruit and other juices.
Michele Carruba, director of the department of pharmacology of the State University of Milan: "These discoveries are usually immediately reported to the various international and national drug agencies that possibly change the information notes of the drugs.
So always read the illustrative leaflets of the medicines you take and if there is anything that is not clear to us, do not be impressed but ask for clarification from the doctor or the pharmacist", the expert told the Ansa agency.
The Nobel Prize in Economics to Paul Krugman
The Nobel Prize in Economics was awarded to American Paul Krugman for his studies on patterns of commerce and the localization of economic activities.
Krugman, 55, is a neo-Keynesian who teaches at Princeton University and is a well-known columnist for the New York Times.
Krugman is one of the biggest critiques of choices in economics and foreign policy of the Bush administration.
A few months after the September 2001 attacks, in the days when Enron failed, Krugman had predicted in an editorial published in the New York Times that the second event would be more decisive than the first, in terms of consequences on U.S. history.
A prediction that sparked quite a few controversies and that for years was denounced to the economist, but that has now proved to be exact.
“I am surprised and also a little shocked by the speed with which the memory of scandals such as Enron’s collapse or Worldcom has disappeared from the public’s attention,” Krugman said.
According to Lorenzo Bini Smaghi, of the Executive Committee of the European Central Bank, Krugman is an "economist of great value", but also a popularizer with a "very aggressive style, particularly critical against the excesses of liberalism of the Bush administration".
Open Office, here is the third edition of the "anti-Microsoft" package
It's the day of the release of OpenOffice.org 3.0, the open source suite of personal productivity that aims to reach more and more users, from home to office, from professionals to public administration, as a free alternative to the better-known Microsoft Office.
After almost a year’s work by volunteer developers, behind whom SUN Microsystem monitors, the new Open Office is shown to the general public with different news: to the welcome screen and to the revised interface and made less spartan, if you add others more substantial such as the native compatibility for the Apple Mac OS X operating system and support for the new ODF (Open Document Format) 1.2, the latest ISO standard version.
Attention also paid to interoperability with the Office 2007, Open Office suite is able to open documents with the new Microsoft extension (.docx, .xlsx, .pptx).
Interesting some specific features of some forms such as the one that allows you to reduce the size of PowerPoint presentations, useful for example to send them by e-mail, or the ability to import PDF files into the graphics processing module, preserving the original appearance of the page.
Also on this front you can create "hybrid PDF" documents, which combine the graphics typical of the Adobe format with the elements of a spreadsheet or a text processor.
The collaboration is an element that has received several improvements within the applications: in the spreadsheet you can intervene in several hands between different users, avoiding conflicts over the changes, in the text processor, each collaborator is assigned a color in the comments for better recognizability during the review phase.
Some companies are also working to bring some OpenOffice features to the Web (on the cloud), and in the coming weeks are expected to release the final result.
The Italian version can be downloaded at http://it.openoffice.org/download/3.0.0/download300.html and has been added to the English one that has been located on mirrors since Friday.
In addition to Windows and Mac OS, as usual the suite is also available for the Linux operating system.
On request can also be shipped at home or in the workplace the installation cd.
And if the theme of gratuitousness can be a tow for dissemination in homes, it is precisely in the business environment that Open Office the suite attempts the big bet, already being able to count on several projects carried out in public administration and schools.
There is also no shortage of companies that offer an ideal training, consulting and assistance service for small and medium-sized companies, which could open up business perspectives.
Open Office consists of five main modules: Writer, for the corresponding video writing of Word; Calc, is the spreadsheet that aims to replace Excel; Impress, for Powerpoint emulation presentations; Dbase, as in the case of the most famous Access is the program that allows you to work on databases; Draw, is finally the graphic module.
The goal for 2008 is to reach 5 million downloads, and considering that to date we are at almost 3 million and 600 thousand, with the release of the new version it is likely that you can also exceed the expected figure.
Kohl is also positive is the fourth case of doping
New case of positivity at the last Tour de France: it's the Austrian Bernhard Kohl, of the Gerolsteiner team, third-placed and winner of the jersey of best climber.
To report the news is the website of the newspaper L'Equipe.
26-year-old Kohl is the fourth runner positive for Cera, the third-generation Epo, after Italians Riccardo Riccò and Leonardo Piepoli and German Stefan Schumacher, also from Gerolsteiner.
Tests of Kohl's blood samples are among those carried out in recent weeks by Lausanne and Chatenay-Malabry labs.
If the counter-analyses confirm the positivity to Cera, the Austrian will lose third place at the Tour and meet the two-year canonical disqualification.
To confirm the news of Kohl's positivity to the third-generation EPO is the head of the French anti-doping agency, Pierre Bordry, who has informed the Associated Press that he has notified the positivity to the Austrian authorities.
The 26-year-old Austrian placed on the final podium behind Spaniard Carlos Sastre and American Cadel Evans, also clinching the polka dot jersey of best climber.
IMMIGRANTS:NEW YORK TIMES WONDER, IS ITALY RACIST?
Italy "from a country of emigrants has only recently become a privileged destination for immigrants" reluctantly welcomed.
Is this turning Italy into a nation of racists? That's how much you ask in a lengthy article the New York Times that dedicated a lengthy article to the debate on the issue of racism in Italy starting from the case of the young Abdul William Guibre originally from Burkina Faso killed last month in Milan for stealing a box of cookies.
This case highlights what the Nyt calls the "paradox of the north": the northern regions are at the same time those where there is "more integration and more work" but also "the highest anti-immigrant sentiment in the country and the highest support to the Northern League".
To confirm the growing tension between Italians and immigrants, the NYT also cited the episodes of intolerance of Milan, Parma, Rome, Ciampino and Castel Volturno.
Not only that.
Even the new legislation wanted by the League that tightens the brakes on immigration and facilitates the expulsion of illegal immigrants is cited as a paradigm of climate change in Italy.
Tendency emphasized by both the Pope and the President of the Republic: "Giorgio Napolitano meeting Benedict XVI at the Quirinale has asked that the State and the Church work together to defeat racism."
Victim students of the dear-rents up to 900 euros for a room
The renting market for university students knows no crisis.
According to Sunia's latest survey, the tenants' union, taking a single room can cost a student up to 900 euros in Milan, 700 in Florence, 650 in Naples, 600 in Rome.
"The phenomenon has now assumed very serious characters to the point of excluding entire groups of young people from the right to study for the very higher overall costs that the families of origin should support - explains the head of the study office of Sunia, Laura Mariani - in these cities the strong demand from students has so deformed the market to trigger a process of generalized increase even for residents ".
In the majority of cases, rental offers contain a number of violations: free, unregistered contracts, no rule limit, connected sub-rents.
Extraordinary action is needed for Sunia which, through the revision of tax taxation, can calm prices.
Looking at the detail of the survey, we see that in Milan, for a bed in the Brianza area, it takes about 450 euros; for a single room you range from a minimum of 650 (Black Bands area) to an average value of 800 (Lambiate area, Udine and Fiera) to a maximum of 900 (in the Vittoria area).
In Florence a bed costs on average 350/400 euros, a room about 700.
In Bologna, where students are concentrated in areas close to the University, it takes 250/280 euros for a bed in a double, from 370 to 500 for a single.
In Rome in areas close to the central universities (San Lorenzo, Piazza Bologna) are asked about 600 euros for a single room, 450 for a bed in a double.
Values only slightly lower (550 euros for a single room) in areas close to the other Universities (Ostiense and Cinecittà).
You only save if you move to outlying areas: 300 euros for a bed and 450 a single in Prenestina, Centocelle and the like.
In Naples for a bed you need 300/450 euros, for a room you spend from 400 to 600 euros, with the highest prices in the Policlinico, Vomero and Colli Aminei areas.
In Bari for a bed it takes 250/350 euros, at least 350 for a single.
In smaller cities prices are lower in absolute terms, but have a greater weight in the city economy.
In the cities in which the phenomenon of rents to non-EU students is more frequent, as in Perugia, there is finally a further anomaly: the increase of about 25/30% of the fee required of foreign students compared to that applied to Italians.
Goodbye old cinema Paradiso, the cities erase the historic halls
Historical cinemas close in favor of the Multiplexes.
"A city without movie theaters is a blind city, it's like an eyeless face."
The image offered by the director Giuseppe Tornatore effectively photographs what is happening in the large Italian urban centers but also in the cities of the province.
The process of marginalization of cinemas, with the birth in the periphery of the large multiplexes and the consequent death by consumption of the monosales in the central areas of the cities, is one of those events that appear to us inescapable and incontrovertible but also has all the characteristics of a real cultural crime.
To disappear are often historical places and full of memory "it is as if a friend, an acquaintance with whom we shared a piece of street and many emotions", observes the director of Nuovo cinema Paradiso, the film that twenty years ago earned Tornatore the Oscar and the Grand Prix of the Jury of Cannes, and that told the poetry of a provincial room and the nostalgia for a way of living cinema lost perhaps forever.
The figures provided by ANEC, the main association of exhibitors, speak clearly: in the last five years 316 traditional cinemas have closed their doors, and to pay the biggest price are mainly theaters in the main cities: 10 less in Florence, 9 in Milan, Naples, Bologna and Rome, 6 less in Turin, 5 in Palermo.
Among the regions most affected by the closures exceed Emilia-Romagna with 82 single-sale cinemas lost, followed by Tuscany (44) and Lombardy (41).
And to show that the first responsible for their disappearance is the advent of the multiplex, these are precisely the three Italian regions that have recorded the largest number of new multiplex cinemas: 195 more screens in Lombardy in the last five years, 81 both in Tuscany and in Emilia-Romagna.
"The Italian exercise has developed irrationally" observes Paolo Protti, president of ANEC, "it is useful that new structures be built but we should ensure the survival of those already existing, because when a city hall closes, not all its visitors are willing to move into the multiplexes.
Not to mention the non-allocation for the fund in favor of merchants: the previous debt of the state is about 10 million euros.
It is disgraceful, from an ethical and moral point of view, that the Government does not comply with existing laws.
To support the sector it would take between 16 and 18 million euros per year".
Interesting the case of Bologna, the first Italian city that, worried about the closures, has decided to run for cover with the signing of a protocol between the Municipality and Anec, the first of this type in Italy, to protect and promote single-room cinemas both in the historic center and in the suburbs.
Among the rules provided there are tax facilitation measures in favor of managers and benefits for the public in terms of mobility: spectators will be able to enter the limited traffic area and will have special rates for parking and in some car parks.
“It’s an initiative that I like,” observes Giuseppe Tornatore, “and I hope that other cities will follow the example of Bologna by setting up other measures.
It is certain, however, that in order to counteract the overpower of multiplexes, traditional rooms must also improve the quality of sound and image, because the public today is more demanding.
The defense of traditional cinema is a theme that for me has always been important: the closure of the Supercinema of Bagheria, the place of my childhood and adolescence, was the spark that convinced me to make "Nuovo cinema Paradiso", to which I had been thinking for 10 years.
I have been living in Rome for 25 years, and when I pass in front of the Rialto or the Quirinale in Via Nazionale now closed, where I have experienced so many shared emotions, not to see the billboards blackened by the dust I turn to the other side".
Ties between politics and 'ndrangheta arrested mayor of Gioia Tauro
"The mayor of Gioia Tauro was willing to align the choices of the Municipality with the interests of Cosca Piromalli."
The accusations that the magistrates of the Anti-Mafia Directorate of Calabria address to the mayor and the deputy mayor of Gioia Tauro Giorgio Dal Torrione and Rosario Schiavone, and to the mayor of Rosarno Carlo Martelli, arrested this morning for external competition in mafia association.
In handcuffs also Gioacchino Piromalli, the elderly mediator between the clan and the public administrations, and his granddaughter lawyer, of the homonym of the boss, already convicted by association for mafia-style delinquency, sentenced to compensate 10 million euros.
"In contempt of any legal and moral norm, as well as common sense," write the prosecutors, the two local administrations had expressed their willingness to pay advice to lawyer Piromalli.
"It would thus have been granted to the cosca - write the judges - to officially enter into the municipalities facilitating the possibilities, already considerable, of control and address of the public administration."
The president of the Calabrian regional junta Agazio Loiero does not hide that "collusion between local authorities and organized underworld there are several" in his region: "There are areas where the mafia intensity is stronger than in others, where collusion is almost inevitable.
But there are so many local politicians who resist the pressures of the 'ndrangheta, heroes who keep themselves clean.'
This morning's arrests follow those of last July with which the police announced that they had beheaded the Piromalli cosca.
Since the end of last April, the city council of Gioia Tauro was dissolved but already in 1991, and always for alleged conditioning between organized crime and local administration, local politicians had been relieved from the task of administering the Municipality.
Giorgio Dal Torrione, mayor of the UDC at the time of dissolution, had been elected at the head of a centre-right coalition after the May 2006 run-off.
He cried out for scandal when the Prefecture ordered the dissolution of the city council.
He called into question the then Deputy Interior Minister Marco Minniti, accusing him of having concocted everything for a political matter, but the judges, in the arrest order signed this morning, explain that Cosca Piromalli had even come to ask for the variation of the project of the A3.
From the environmental interceptions ordered against Dal Torrione, the prosecutors write, it emerged "the readiness to marry the needs of an entrepreneur, representative of a larger group notoriously close to the Pyromals, standardizing the choices of the public administration to the interests of thing."
When the energy comes from the waves now the big ones take to the field
It's an exterminated field, occupying more than 70 percent of the planet's surplace and it's discovered by the world's big energy companies.
Waves, currents, tides are the new renewables on which they have set their sights, and now they try to get their hands on, behemoths like Chevron.
The possibility of deriving energy from the movements of the sea has been studied for a hundred years now and in the 1970s, in conjunction with the first oil crisis, research intensified.
When the price of oil fell, around 1980, the idea of exploiting marine energy was put aside, but now the environmental demands and the new increase in oil give impetus to the sector.
The World Energy Council (WEC) in London estimated in 2007 that 15 percent of the world's electricity needs could be covered by sea-wave motion exploitation plants.
This amount would be twice that currently produced by nuclear power plants.
And in addition there would be currents and tides.
Colepses to the attack.
The first company to bet on marine energy was Enersis, which in 2005 signed an agreement to build a plant in Northern Portugal.
When the agreement was announced it seemed a revolutionary step.
Two years later to bet on marine energy are more and more and among these companies have always been devoted to traditional energy.
“Chevron is studying technologies on ocean energy and is considering the possibility of cooperating with us, the spokesman for a company associated with the U.S. oil giont to the Wall Street Journal.” “Chevron is considering the possibility of cooperating with us.”
The cooperation in question concerns obtaining a permit from the U.S. Energy Commission, which is necessary to start a marine energy exploitation project in Alaska.
It is not a small signal, especially when you consider that in Alaska the U.S. continues to extract oil and recently President Bush has started a plan for new drilling.
In addition to the Chevron there are the Verdant Power Inc. in New York and New Jersey's Ocean Power along the path of energy that comes from the sea and the sums invested so far, although not known in the details, indicate according to observers a rising interest.
Technology to be fine-tuned.
Experts talk about a technology still in its infancy, not least because marine energy doesn't escape all doubts about the environmental impact.
For now plants such as Portuguese or some in Scotland are small and do not seem to create major problems for the ecosystem.
To produce energy on a large scale, however, technologies with a greater environmental impact must be used.
And the choices are different: Ocean Power, for example, uses a system of buoys connected to each other.
The push of the waves, which moves the buoys up and down is converted into hydraulic pressure from pistons and cylinders inside the floats.
The pressure spins a turbine which in turn feeds a generator.
Electricity is sent to shore through submarine cables.
For now there are four plants of this type beining planned, waiting only for federal approval and part of the energy could serve for a military installation.
Verdant Power, on the other hand, already produces energy for a mall and a parking lot using six underwater turbines in the East River in New York.
The movement of water when there are tides makes the turbine blades spin, creating a rotary movement that feeds a generator.
Verdant said it has a lengthy list of customers waiting for the necessary permissions to take advantage of energy.
After the waves, the tides.
A particular interest, they say at the Institute for the Research of Electricity, deserves the potential of tides at this time.
Although it is an intermittent phenomenon, the tide is more predictable than the wind, the sun or the waves and therefore more reliable as an energy source.
It is precisely on tidal energy that states such as that of New York, Maine and Alaska, together with others bordering the coasts, have already invested 7 and a half million dollars in 2008 and expected investments of up to 35 million for 2009.
